1785545486982161	mess-premise-p7	itc:v3:AWL8nzkmSSTnMQAAAAAAQf191aqu7AAAAAEvAw		item.comment	bn-2u01	{"body":"MEASURED — open on current trunk is LINEAR in log size (~0.10 s/GiB warm), and the two terms that dominate it are NOT the ones Track A removes. RECOMMENDATION: RESCOPE (part 2).\n\n## Prior data (step 1)\n\nspikes/recovery_scale/REPORT.md (Spike D era, prototype seglog v2 scanner, NOT the engine): FULL scan 1.1 s/GiB cold / 0.76 warm, flat in segment size; A7/F5 last-segment fast path ~0.3 s cold at 256 MiB. Directionally still right for the *scan* term (I measure 0.577 cold / 0.478 warm s/GiB on the real scanner — faster host), but it predates the engine: it never measured LogEngine::open, sealed sidecars (bn-2ib), the registry-in-log fold (bn-2di), or the .pcol payload sidecar. All of that needed refreshing.\n\nspikes/segment_effect/REPORT.md (the hypothesis under test) claimed checkpoint open = 4.90% of full scan at 10M capsules / 1M streams — but its denominator is a PURE IN-MEMORY BTreeMap/dense-kernel fold of already-decoded capsules. That denominator does not exist in this engine: production open already skips sealed segments' bytes entirely.\n\n## Method\n\nmaw ws bn-2u01 (from main, epoch 54aaa20a8303). `mess_log::probe`: process-global atomic ns/count accumulators wrapping every phase of LogEngine::open_with / recover / finish_recovery / load_sealed, plus the read-vs-parse split inside recover_segment_with_image. Driver crates/mess-store/examples/openscale.rs. INSTRUMENTATION NEVER MERGES.\n\nCorpora built through the real LogEngine::append_batch (Durability::Process, production-default 256 MiB segments), realistic shape: 250 B JSON-ish payloads with per-record varying content, 100-EVENT BATCHES, N streams round-robin; engine dropped after build so every roll-seal drains. Not the degenerate 1-event/batch shape.\n\nCold cache: posix_fadvise(DONTNEED) over every segment + sidecar before each rep (recovery_scale's technique; drop_caches needs root). Warm best-of-4, cold best-of-3, serial, under flock on $HOME/.cache/mess-bench/global-measurement.lock. Host: Threadripper 9970X, ext4 on Samsung 9100 PRO 4TB PCIe5 NVMe, 125 GiB RAM. Load 3-5 for the final passes (box baseline 2-3); an earlier pass at load 14-18 landed within 15% — noted, not used. Corpora deleted; pgrep clean.\n\n## Scaling curve (best rep; wall = LogEngine::open_with)\n\ncorpus   | log       | streams | segs(sealed) | WARM      | s/GiB | COLD       | s/GiB | c/w  | RSS after open\ncsuffix  | 0.246 GiB | 1k      | 1 (0)        | 117.7 ms  | 0.478 | 142.0 ms   | 0.577 | 1.21 | 7 MB\nc05      | 0.505 GiB | 1k      | 3 (2)        | 50.8 ms   | 0.101 | 100.8 ms   | 0.200 | 1.98 | 159 MB\nc2       | 2.001 GiB | 1k      | 9 (8)        | 199.6 ms  | 0.100 | 283.3 ms   | 0.142 | 1.42 | 596 MB\nc8       | 8.006 GiB | 1k      | 33 (32)      | 794.6 ms  | 0.099 | 993.8 ms   | 0.124 | 1.25 | 2364 MB\nc2s50k   | 2.002 GiB | 50k     | 9 (8)        | 287.9 ms  | 0.144 | 2134.6 ms  | 1.066 | 7.4  | 627 MB\nc8s250k  | 8.006 GiB | 250k    | 33 (32)      | 1190.8 ms | 0.149 | 10494.7 ms | 1.311 | 8.8  | 2536 MB\n\ncsuffix is the all-unsealed case (one full 252 MB active segment) — the pure scan path. The rest are normal sealed-tier opens.\n\nYES, LINEAR. At fixed stream count s/GiB is flat (0.101 / 0.100 / 0.099 warm). It is linear because of the .pcol term below, not because of a log scan.\n\n## Composition — warm best rep, 8.006 GiB\n\nphase                                        | 1k streams |    %  | 250k streams |    %\n.pcol sidecar eager load (LOAD_SEALED_PCOL)  |  782.7 ms  | 98.5% |   696.7 ms   | 58.5%\nregistry rebuild (finish_recovery)           |    1.58 ms |  0.20%|   426.6 ms   | 35.8%\n  - of which one pread per registered name   |    1.30 ms |  0.16%|   274.8 ms   | 23.1%\nsealed-sidecar heads merge                   |    3.63 ms |  0.46%|    46.4 ms   |  3.9%\nactive suffix: raw read + CRC/parse + post   |    2.78 ms |  0.35%|     2.09 ms  |  0.18%\nActiveIndex apply_committed                  |    0.033 ms|  0.004%|    0.025 ms |  0.002%\npayload decode                               |      0     |  0%   |      0       |  0%\n\nCOLD, 8.006 GiB / 250k streams: registry rebuild 9452 ms (89.9%) — of which 9271 ms is 250,000 cold random preads at 37.1 us each; .pcol 963 ms (9.2%); heads merge 48 ms (0.46%); suffix 2.6 ms (0.02%); apply 0.034 ms (0.0003%).\n\n## Two things to say loudly\n\n1. OPEN NEVER DECODES PAYLOADS. recover_payload_decodes() = 0 in all 22 measured opens, every size, every stream count, chain off (the default). bn-2ib already delivered that. \"Effects+suffix skips payload decode\" buys EXACTLY ZERO on open. Half of Track A's stated value proposition is already banked.\n\n2. apply_committed is 0.002%-0.36% of open in every realistic shape. bn-3hm4's 18.6% was the degenerate 1-event/batch shape. Worst case here (a full 252 MB active segment, 10,472 hot entries) is 0.42 ms = 0.36%, at 40 ns/entry. The suffix apply is not a cost centre at any size.\n\n## The unmeasured elephant: eager .pcol load\n\nSealedSegmentIndex::open (mess-index/src/sealed/segment.rs:678) calls SealedPayloadIndex::open, which does std::fs::read of the WHOLE .pcol columnar payload sidecar into the heap — for EVERY sealed segment, at open, eagerly. My corpora produce 0.285 GiB of .pcol per GiB of log, so open reads ~0.29x the log size and keeps it resident forever. That is the entire reason open is linear in log size, and the entire reason RSS after opening an 8 GiB store is 2.4 GB.\n\nCounterfactual (MESS_PROBE_NO_PCOL=1 skips the attach; sealed reads then fall back to the raw log, D1):\n  c05   50.8 ms -> 3.11 ms  (16x)      RSS 159 MB -> 11 MB\n  c2   199.6 ms -> 2.80 ms  (71x)      RSS 596 MB -> 8 MB\n  c8   794.6 ms -> 8.20 ms  (97x)      RSS 2364 MB -> 19 MB\n  c2s50k 287.9 -> 92.4 ms   (3.1x)     c8s250k 1190.8 -> 550.4 ms (2.2x)\n\n(part 2: unit rates, projections, the implied checkpoint win, recommendation, caveats)"}	blake3:H5fZIQSfPzgl-BskovwfeKGqE5QaS_dtpSOd4NIGBl0
1785545487003620	mess-premise-p7	itc:v3:AWL8nzkmSSTnMQAAAAAAQf191aqu7AAAAAEvBA		item.comment	bn-2u01	{"body":"(bn-2u01 premise check, part 2 of 2 — continues the previous comment.)\n\n## Unit rates (fitted, then validated)\n\n  .pcol eager load        97.8 ms/GiB of log warm   |  117 ms/GiB cold   (3.13 / 2.55 GB/s)\n  registry rebuild         1.7 us per registered name warm | 37.5 us cold (1 random pread/name)\n  sealed heads merge       115-150 ns per (sealed segment x stream touched)\n  active suffix scan       0.478 s/GiB warm | 0.577 s/GiB cold  (43% read, 30% CRC/parse, 19% post)\n  apply_committed          40 ns per hot batch entry\n  device (1 thread, buffered, 10.3 GiB): 3.16 GB/s COLD vs 3.34 GB/s warm\n\nModel: open(G GiB, S streams, U GiB unsealed) [seconds]\n  warm = 0.098*G + 1.7e-6*S + 1.4e-7*M + 0.478*U\n  cold = 0.117*G + 37.5e-6*S + 1.6e-7*M + 0.577*U\n  M = (#sealed segments) x min(S, ~9600 batches/segment)\nValidation: c8s250k cold predicted 10.37 s vs measured 10.49 s (1%); warm 1.26 vs 1.19 (5%).\n\nCOLD-CACHE CAVEAT, MEASURED NOT ESTIMATED. On this device sequential cold ~= warm (3.16 vs 3.34 GB/s: a 1.06x multiplier), because a std::fs::read into a fresh Vec is memcpy/page-fault bound and a PCIe5 9100 PRO feeds it faster than that. So the .pcol and suffix terms barely move cold (1.2x). The RANDOM term explodes: one cold pread per registered name costs 37.5 us vs 1.7 us warm — a 22x multiplier. Cold penalty is entirely a function of how many random reads open does, and today that number is \"one per stream name that ever existed\".\n\n## Projections at stated target log sizes\n\nAssumptions, stated explicitly: same shape as measured (250 B payloads, 100-event batches, ~279 B/event on disk, 256 MiB segments => ~3.85M events and ~9,600 batches per segment, 0.285 GiB .pcol per GiB of log); worst-case unsealed tail U = 0.25 GiB (a full active segment); chain off; seal_pack off.\n\n  10 GiB (~38.5M events, 40 segments)      warm      cold\n    10k streams                            1.17 s    1.75 s\n    100k streams                           1.32 s    5.13 s\n    1M streams                             2.85 s    38.9 s\n\n  100 GiB (~385M events, 400 segments)     warm      cold\n    100k streams                          10.6 s    16.2 s\n    1M streams                            12.2 s    50.0 s\n    10M streams                           27.5 s    388 s  (6.5 min)\n\n## The implied checkpoint win\n\nSuffix fraction of measured open: 0.18%-0.36% warm, 0.02% cold at 8 GiB. So \"checkpoint+suffix ~= suffix_fraction x measured + checkpoint-load\" gives ~0.002 x measured + L. The suffix is not the constraint; L and what the checkpoint does NOT cover are.\n\nCheckpoint-load estimate L (bn-18y1 kernel state = registry bijection + per-stream heads): ~40-64 B/stream on disk, ~80-100 B/stream resident; sequential page read at 2.5-3 GB/s + BLAKE3 verify + map rehydrate at ~80 ns/entry => ~0.2 us/stream, i.e. ~50 ms at 250k, ~0.2 s at 1M, ~2 s at 10M streams. (Spike D's own 78 ms open at 1M streams / 28.1 MB of blobs is consistent.) Note bn-3hm4's ActiveIndex memory figures do not apply here: the hot index is 45 KB-10 MB and irrelevant; the checkpoint's size is set by the REGISTRY, which nobody has measured before this bone.\n\n100 GiB / 1M streams, four worlds:\n  today                                    warm 12.2 s   cold 50.0 s\n  + Track A checkpoints only               warm 10.1 s   cold 12.3 s   (1.2x / 4.1x)\n  + lazy .pcol only (no Track A)           warm  2.4 s   cold 38.3 s   (5.1x / 1.3x)\n  + both                                   warm  0.32 s  cold 0.39 s   (38x / 128x)\n100 GiB / 10M streams:\n  today 27.5 / 388 s; checkpoints only 11.9 / 14.3 s; lazy .pcol only 17.7 / 376 s; both 2.1 / 2.6 s.\n\nRead that carefully. Neither fix alone reaches design 18.3's 1.0 s open; both together do, comfortably. But the two fixes are NOT the same size of work, and the checkpoint half does not need the SegmentEffect algebra to deliver its share.\n\n## Recommendation: RESCOPE\n\nDROP as written: bn-1mzi (canonical SegmentEffect algebra) and bn-2dbp (emit SegmentEffect sections) as OPEN-TIME PERFORMANCE bones. Everything the head/stream-path algebra would accelerate is already served by the sealed sidecar and measures 0.46%-3.9% warm / 0.46% cold of open. Their two headline claims are dead on arrival here: \"no payload decode\" is already true (0 decodes, always), and \"ordered apply at 24M transitions/s\" targets a phase (apply_committed) that is 0.002%-0.36% of open. If bn-1mzi is kept it must be justified as CORRECTNESS/Fjall-removal groundwork, with no recovery-time claim attached.\n\nDO FIRST, outside Track A — one small bone, no algebra: make the .pcol attach lazy or mmap-backed in SealedSegmentIndex::open. Target metric: open wall time and post-open RSS on an 8 GiB / 1k-stream store: 794.6 ms -> <20 ms, 2364 MB -> <30 MB (measured counterfactual: 8.20 ms / 19 MB). Also audit the SealPack path (bn-3of, seal_pack off by default): open_pack std::fs::read's the whole pack AND decodes EVENT_TYPE_IDS into a Vec<u32> of length event_count per segment — 1.5 GB resident at 385M events. Same fix needed before that flag is ever turned on.\n\nPROCEED, rescoped, as ONE bone not three: a KERNEL-STATE SNAPSHOT covering the registry bijection + per-stream heads only — bn-18y1's page/manifest/anchor/install machinery, without bn-1mzi's frontiers/allocators/dedupe-epoch/snapshot-slot algebra and without bn-2dbp's per-segment effects. Target metric: cold open of a 100k-stream store. Today 5.13 s at 10 GiB (89% of it 100k random preads); the gate should be <0.5 s cold and <0.2 s warm, with the digest-equality property from Spike D retained. bn-3ctl (recovery integration + fallback ladder + digest evidence) survives essentially unchanged and is where the user-visible result lands — it just integrates a snapshot instead of a checkpoint-plus-effect-algebra.\n\nCHEAPER ALTERNATIVE TO EVALUATE FIRST inside that bone: SealPack already RESERVES a REGISTRY_DELTA section kind (bn-2dbp's own words) and does not emit it. Writing each sealed segment's $registry records into its sidecar turns finish_recovery's O(#names) random preads into O(#segments) sequential section reads with no new durable format, no manifest, no GC, no alternating pointers. That alone takes 100 GiB / 1M streams cold from 50.0 s to ~2.5 s (the residual is the fold at ~0.7 us/name). A full checkpoint only earns its keep above ~1M streams, and only after both this and the .pcol fix. I would gate bn-18y1 on measuring the registry-delta variant first.\n\n## Caveats / not covered\n\n- ONE SHAPE. 250 B payloads / 100-event batches / round-robin streams. .pcol bytes/event (113 B here, vs phase5's 8.85 B/event on a 34.7 B/event msgpack corpus) is highly payload-dependent — the .pcol term scales with payload compressibility, so a store with small shreddable payloads pays less than 0.098 s/GiB, and the registry term then dominates sooner. The RATIO conclusions are robust; the absolute .pcol constant is not.\n- One registration batch per stream name (measured N_REG_PREADS == N_STREAMS-1 exactly). A workload creating many names inside one append unit would batch them and reduce the pread count proportionally.\n- Streams <= batches by construction, so I could not test S > 250k at 8 GiB; the 1k/50k/250k points are linear (1.5/1.6/1.7 us warm, 37.0/37.5/37.8 us cold per name) and the projections extrapolate that line.\n- Checkpoint-load L is an ESTIMATE from state size, not a measurement — nobody has built one. If registry name strings make pages bigger than assumed, L grows linearly and the 10M-stream case degrades first.\n- Host contamination: sibling agents' JVM/rustc storms pushed load to 14-18 during an intermediate pass. Final numbers were taken at load 3-5 and the contaminated pass agreed within 15%.\n- Not measured: multi-segment crash recovery (several rolled-but-unsealed segments), chain-on opens (which DO decode every payload by design), parity/filter attach cost, and open under concurrent append."}	blake3:Ldbht7HF2aEKq6f_RjDJblm5MrUMbxX_UsYr_az2RIU
1785545614817288	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExvhE		item.comment	bn-2u01	{"body":"Lead decision: RESCOPE accepted as recommended. Verified the .pcol eager-read claim in code (segment.rs:678 attach -> payload.rs:834 std::fs::read of the whole sidecar). Actions: (1) bn-1mzi + bn-2dbp closed — declined as perf bones; open never decodes payloads (bn-2ib banked that win) and the paths the algebra would accelerate measure 0.46-3.9% of open; reopen-by-construction if something downstream needs the algebra for correctness. (2) New bone: lazy/mmap .pcol attach — the measured 98.5%-of-warm-open elephant, dispatched immediately. (3) New bone: registry-delta-in-sidecar experiment (SealPack already reserves REGISTRY_DELTA) — blocks bn-18y1; if O(#segments) sequential registry reads deliver ~2.5s cold at 100GiB/1M streams, the full checkpoint machinery below 1M streams is unnecessary. (4) bn-18y1 rescoped to registry+heads kernel snapshot, pending that experiment; bn-3ctl rides on bn-18y1's fate. (5) SealPack eager EVENT_TYPE_IDS decode (1.5GB resident at 385M events) noted on Phase 8's goal bone. Instrumentation workspace destroyed unmerged."}	blake3:hMbWZcaGm_2cg2PMniwZqJzfP3iqSZGsUdPuVdWvlBE
1785545614839919	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExvxE		item.move	bn-2u01	{"state":"done"}	blake3:KGLSjHdAN4A30HzLkMQyze9p0fp6zlu-XgxsyaP1FwM
1785545630219320	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExwBE		item.create	bn-bka2	{"description":"bn-2u01 measured SealedSegmentIndex::open eagerly std::fs::read-ing every sealed segment's whole .pcol payload sidecar (segment.rs:678 attach -> payload.rs:834), making it 98.5% of warm open time and ~all of open RSS in realistic shapes: at 8 GiB the counterfactual (MESS_PROBE_NO_PCOL) took open from 794.6ms to 8.2ms and RSS from 2364MB to 19MB. Fix: attach the .pcol lazily — parse the header/footer/block table at open (bounded bytes), map or read block payload bytes on first columnar read touching them (mmap or on-demand pread; keep the existing silently-degrade contract: missing/corrupt .pcol = no columnar payload, raw log stays payload authority per D1). Sealed read-path performance must not regress: phase5.sealed columnar_replay and payload-codec floors are the guard. Target: 8 GiB / 1k-stream open <20ms warm, open RSS <30MB, all 14 mess-bench floors pass.","kind":"task","title":"Lazy .pcol payload-sidecar attach: stop reading whole sidecars at engine open"}	blake3:7ndTHSQeLnxVz_8wlSc9_cfYbWD07ZUlqK1ubNgTEcU
1785545630350611	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExwRE		item.create	bn-26pp	{"description":"bn-2u01's cold-open composition at high stream counts: registry rebuild = 89.9% of a 10.5s cold open at 8 GiB/250k streams — 250k random preads at 37us each, O(#names). SealPack already RESERVES a REGISTRY_DELTA section and never emits it. Hypothesis: writing each segment's $registry records into its sidecar at seal time turns registry rebuild into O(#segments) sequential reads — projected 100GiB/1M-streams cold open 50s -> ~2.5s with no manifest, no GC, no alternating-pointer machinery. Prototype in a workspace, measure with bn-2u01's openscale driver (recoverable from its workspace snapshot: maw ws recover bn-2u01), ABBA against trunk. If it delivers, bn-18y1's full checkpoint machinery is unnecessary below ~1M streams and should be declined; if not, bn-18y1 proceeds rescoped to a registry+heads kernel snapshot. Run AFTER the lazy-.pcol bone merges (same mess-index/sealed territory).","kind":"task","title":"Experiment: emit per-segment registry deltas into the sealed sidecar (REGISTRY_DELTA) and read them at open"}	blake3:YeUlEL349DSqaatD-mLgnH-N7qWkU9WdCuUaNpJqQNQ
