1785546062648786	mess-pack-sidecar	itc:v3:AWKfJk-eZz_EAAAAAAAAQb6197d_gAAAAAEqAw		item.comment	bn-ozi5	{"body":"Shipped the v1 pack snapshot sidecar storage core in workspace bn-ozi5, commit b9f05b43, review cr-2ywo0t (NOT self-approved; needs a reviewer). Scope followed the lead's ruling: contract essentials are binding, the certification-benchmark protocol is context, and the source-breaking public trait work is deferred.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED (crates/mess-store/src/pack_snapshot/, 4314 lines incl. tests)\n- format.rs — pure encode/decode: identity file, pack header, commit-framed records, self-describing record body (carries the stream NAME, so no reverse id->name side map is needed the way fjall's snapshot_stream_names was), sealed-pack index+footer, root descriptor, structural filename parse/format. CRC32C via mess_log::crc — zero new external deps.\n- sidecar.rs — the storage core: Sidecar::open_writer / open_reader, save/save_with_mode, load, roll/seal, root publication, root pruning, recovery (interrupted-seal completion + torn-tail truncation), an in-memory durability proof ledger, and a test-only fault hook at every named durability boundary.\n- mod.rs — PackSnapshotBackend<B>: Backend + SnapshotStore + SubscribeBackend, mirroring FjallSnapshotBackend's delegation exactly. open / open_with(SidecarOptions) / open_read_only.\n- Exported from lib.rs alongside the fjall backend. FjallSnapshotBackend is completely untouched.\n\nCONTRACT ESSENTIAL -> TEST MAP\n- Discardable acceleration: a_wiped_sidecar_falls_back_to_full_replay, every_root_deleted_falls_back_to_full_replay, a_corrupt_record_falls_back_to_full_replay, an_unknown_format_is_a_miss_not_a_failure, every_root_corrupt_degrades_to_no_snapshots_not_an_error, deleting_the_whole_sidecar_degrades_to_no_heads, a_corrupt_frame_mid_pack_is_a_miss_never_a_wrong_answer.\n- One writer / clones share one owner / read-only readers: a_second_writer_fails_loudly, clones_share_one_serialized_writer_owner, cloned_stores_share_one_writer_and_all_saves_land, a_reader_never_mutates_the_directory_and_takes_no_lock (byte-for-byte directory listing compare), a_reader_on_a_missing_or_empty_sidecar_simply_has_no_heads, an_offline_reader_can_inspect_a_live_sidecar.\n- Store UUID / non-reused ids / reserved temp names: identity_is_created_once_and_reused_across_reopen, a_corrupt_identity_starts_a_new_namespace_and_never_reuses_ids, ids_are_never_reused_even_after_every_root_is_deleted, reserved_tmp_names_are_never_discovery_candidates, filenames_roundtrip_and_tmp_is_never_a_candidate.\n- Commit framing / bounded records: every_frame_truncation_is_rejected (every prefix), frame_bit_flips_are_rejected (every byte), frame_length_is_bounded, root_rejects_every_truncation_and_flip, root_with_corrupt_counts_does_not_over_allocate.\n- Roll / sealed immutability: rolling_seals_packs_that_are_then_never_appended (freezes sealed sizes then keeps writing), footer_roundtrip_and_incomplete_seal.\n- Discovery + fallback + >=2 roots: a_stale_root_naming_a_missing_pack_falls_back_to_an_older_root, pruning_retains_at_least_two_roots_and_keeps_loading.\n- Buffered vs Durable: buffered_issues_no_barriers_and_durable_does (barrier counter is exactly flat under Buffered), a_durable_save_promotes_inherited_buffered_records (promotion sweeps in unrelated Buffered records; also asserts a Buffered root can never read back as Durable).\n- Parity with the backend it replaces: pack_and_fjall_backends_return_byte_identical_snapshots (120 workloads, byte-compares every StoredSnapshot AND events_replayed), plus the whole fjall_snapshot.rs suite re-run on packs (law over 300 prefixes with an explicit replay-vs-snapshot byte compare, tail-only load, reopen survival, fold-version deploy invalidation, subscribe delegation).\n\nCRASH-TEST INVENTORY (honest results)\nTwo models, both documented in the test module header:\n1. Injected abort at a named fsync boundary, then drop + reopen. This faithfully models a PROCESS crash (page cache survives, no cleanup runs). Steps covered and all recovering: record:append, record:sync, pack:dirsync, root:write, root:sync, root:rename, root:dirsync, roll:index, roll:sync, roll:rename, roll:dirsync, roll:newpack, prune:presync, prune:unlink, prune:dirsync. Each asserts: the pre-crash head survives, the in-flight head is all-or-nothing, and the sidecar is immediately writable again and re-readable after a second reopen.\n2. Byte-level mutilation for what a process crash cannot produce: a_torn_active_tail_is_truncated_at_the_last_valid_frame (partial frame FOLLOWED by a complete valid frame, proving the scan does not resynchronize), a_partially_persisted_footer_recovers_by_truncation, corrupt frame mid-pack, corrupt roots, missing pack.\nNOT covered, stated plainly: a real SIGKILL of a separate process, and true power-loss reordering of unsynced writes. mess-log has SimFs/CrashFs for that but it is wired to mess-log's own Fs trait, not std::fs, so reusing it means routing the sidecar through that abstraction — flagged as future work rather than faked.\n\nTEST EVIDENCE\n- 42 new unit tests + 12 new integration tests, all pass.\n- Full `cargo nextest run -p mess-store` (MESS_SNAPSHOT_LAW_ITERS=25): 238/238 pass, 5 skipped, 50.7s.\n- `cargo check --workspace --all-targets`: clean. `just fmt-check`: clean. `cargo clippy -p mess-store --all-targets`: no warnings from the new code (pre-existing ones elsewhere unchanged).\n- I did NOT run `just test` (workspace-wide) — lead's instruction.\n\nDELIBERATELY DEFERRED\n- The source-breaking public Snapshottable/SnapshotStore trait work (aggregate/schema IDs, codec IDs, SnapshotCompatibility/SnapshotCoverage public API). Internally fold_version is the compatibility key and format::Coverage is the ordered lattice (Empty < Through(0) < Through(1)).\n- The COW content-addressed radix tree. v1 uses a flat root listing every head, so a save is O(N) in published heads; the exact substitution point is documented in sidecar::select_root.\n- Real content GC/reclamation. Root pruning is implemented with the amendment's durable ordering (barrier, unlink, barrier) so deletion slots in without a format change; no pack or record bytes are ever deleted, so superseded records and orphaned frames accumulate. The active pack is never collected.\n- Grouped publication, pinned-root scan_snapshots pagination, CertifiedSnapshotRef (v1 writes only UnverifiedCache — a record claiming certification without both semantic hashes would be a lie), and the whole certification benchmark protocol.\n- Adoption: nothing in mess-cli, examples/social, or the spikes points at the new backend. That is bn-3l8n.\n\nFOUR DESIGN DECISIONS THE REVIEWER SHOULD SCRUTINISE\n1. Equal-coverage conflict rule DEVIATES from the ADR. ADR 0002 says a valid current record with a different state identity at equal coverage is a conflict and remains current. I implemented last-write-wins instead, because (a) split-brain is prevented here by the exclusive writer lock, not by this rule, and (b) the existing seam has no way to surface a conflict and EventStore::load_cached relies on a save replacing the head. Lower coverage IS rejected, and a byte-identical re-save IS idempotent (both tested), so rules 1 and 2 hold.\n2. The durability proof ledger is in-memory only. A reopen forgets every proof, so the first Durable save after a restart re-promotes (extra fsyncs, never fewer). Conservative and costs no on-disk format; the persisted ledger can be added later.\n3. Pack sequences and root generations share ONE monotone id counter whose high-water is durably reserved in IDENTITY every 4096 ids. Without this, deleting every root rewinds the counter and lets a new root reuse a generation number — the ABA the contract forbids. Cost: one durable identity rewrite per 4096 ids, which means a Buffered save occasionally pays a barrier. I judged identity safety worth it; say if you disagree.\n4. A corrupt IDENTITY mints a brand-new UUID namespace (contract-sanctioned: ignore old artifacts, never adopt their ids) rather than failing. Old packs are then orphaned on disk forever since v1 does no deletion."}	blake3:mOfGioDwKuQACn9YZq1GLh5kdjviGXCb1X0Aem519_o
1785546290991212	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExyxE		item.comment	bn-ozi5	{"body":"Lead review (cr-2ywo0t approved): read the seam (mod.rs in full), the save/publish/roll ordering core, and traced the dir_proven lifecycle — set by roll's dirsync, invalidated on successor-pack creation, re-proven by Durable root publish, checked in the Durable closure promotion. Ack ordering is correct: closure proven -> root durably installed -> return. Independently re-ran mess-store 238/238 (bounded law profile), cargo check --workspace --all-targets, fmt-check. Rulings on the four flagged decisions: (1) equal-coverage last-write-wins ACCEPTED for v1 — matches the live fjall seam semantics (differential parity tests prove it); the ADR conflict rule transfers to the deferred coverage-API bone as a requirement. (2) In-memory proof ledger ACCEPTED — errs toward extra barriers, never fewer. (3) Shared id counter with 4096-stride durable reservation ACCEPTED — the anti-ABA property is contract-required and one identity barrier per 4096 Buffered saves is negligible. (4) Corrupt-IDENTITY new-namespace minting ACCEPTED — contract-sanctioned; orphaned packs are GC's problem when GC exists. Full just test runs on trunk after merge (gate shared with bn-bka2)."}	blake3:AU2grflXkG3Xbr_srpUbk4l4teaJREkK28tDmfKzol4
1785546306610094	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExzBE		item.move	bn-ozi5	{"state":"done"}	blake3:bvFOwkefX5RwX57mgApyVct64p46pPFvsGNkB6uoztQ
1785548127269198	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExzRE		item.comment	bn-bka2	{"body":"DONE (pending review). Workspace bn-bka2, commit 5706e09c, review cr-377lcv (not self-approved). Part 1/2: mechanism + semantics.\n\n## Mechanism: on-demand pread, not mmap\n\nNo mmap facility exists in the workspace — `memmap2` appears only in `spikes/perf_replay/Cargo.toml`, which is not a workspace member, and there is no `memmap*` entry anywhere in `Cargo.lock`. mmap would have been a NEW dependency, and two further reasons decided against it:\n\n1. **SIGBUS breaks the silently-degrade contract.** A read error or truncation under a mapping raises SIGBUS, which cannot be turned into `Err(Corrupt)` and degraded to the raw log. `pread` returns an error we can degrade on. For a sidecar whose whole contract is \"if anything is wrong, drop it and read the log\", that is decisive.\n2. **The read path never wants the whole mapping resident.** mess-store already caches *decoded* `.pcol` blocks (`BlockReader::pcol_blocks`, keyed segment/generation/block). The compressed bytes are needed only transiently to feed one `Block::decode`, so a per-block `pread` into a scratch `Vec` dropped after the decode is exactly the lifetime the read path has; mmap would keep touched pages in RSS for no benefit.\n\nThe workspace already speaks this dialect: mess-log's `FileHandle` is a positioned pread/pwrite seam, and mess-store's `BlockReader` keeps one read-only fd per segment for the engine's lifetime. Retaining the fd (rather than reopening by path per read) is also a correctness property: the fd pins the inode the block table describes, so a concurrent re-seal renaming a new `.pcol` over the path can never be read through the old block map (the bn-2ib review-F1 bug class).\n\n## What open does now\n\n`SealedPayloadIndex::open` reads the 32-byte header, the 24-byte footer, and the `n_blocks x 32`-byte block index — bounded by block count, ~0.4% of a sidecar — validates magic x2 / version / header-footer `n_blocks` agreement / index-region sizing / every block span inside DATA / contiguous `first_event` coverage summing to `event_count` / kind-vs-dict_id legality, and keeps the `File`. Block bytes are `pread` on first touch and dropped after the decode. `from_bytes` is untouched and still eager (verify-on-seal, SealPack payload sections); new `open_eager` is the whole-image + `content_crc` path, used by `archive_reblock` (rewrites the file, reassembles everything anyway) and `mess-cli verify` (whose job is reading every byte).\n\n`&self` throughout, no interior mutability, no lock: positioned reads do not touch a shared file offset, so concurrent readers of one `Arc<SealedSegmentIndex>` do not serialise (test `lazy_index_serves_concurrent_readers`).\n\n## Corruption timing (the semantic change; D1/I5 preserved)\n\n`content_crc` covers the whole image, so a lazy open cannot verify it — reading it all IS the cost being removed. The format's two reserved fields now carry crc32c of the block index (header offset 28, was `reserved`) and of each block's stored bytes (block entry offset 28, was `reserved2`), gated by a new header flag `FLAG_SPLIT_CRC` (flags bit 0, previously always 0):\n\n- open still refuses structural damage, and now also a torn block index (`index_crc`);\n- DATA-region damage is caught at the FIRST READ of the affected block, before the codec sees the bytes, as `PayloadError::Corrupt` — which every caller already degrades on (mess-store `decode_capsule` falls back to raw frames; the raw log is the payload authority, D1). Only that block degrades, not the whole sidecar;\n- pre-bn-bka2 sidecars (`flags == 0`) attach and read exactly as before, with the codec's own decode failure as the trigger — no rewrite, no migration;\n- `content_crc` is still written and still verified by every eager path, so nothing is removed from the format.\n\nDocumented in the `payload` module docs (new \"Lazy attach and where integrity is checked\" section), the file-layout block, and at the `SealedSegmentIndex::open` attach site.\n\nNote the per-block CRC is not a nicety: without it this change would have been a correctness regression, not just a timing shift. `engine_block_reads`'s corrupt-`.pcol` fixture flips a byte mid-file, and a raw-fallback block whose zstd frame happened to survive the flip would have served wrong payload bytes where the old open-time CRC dropped the sidecar."}	blake3:W9_gGWjKKnm4tRZaBEHlA0kEFmJ3jV1h26rLwC63iD4
1785548157525767	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExzhE		item.comment	bn-bka2	{"body":"(bn-bka2 part 2/2: measurements, floors, tests, follow-ups.)\n\n## Open-time A/B — this host, SAME binary, ABBA, `MESS_PCOL_EAGER` switch (NOT committed)\n\nCorpus via the recovered bn-2u01 openscale driver (adapted, probe calls stripped; NOT committed): 1k streams, 100-event batches, 250 B payloads, 256 MiB segments, warm, ONE PROCESS PER REP (so the RSS delta is not masked by a retained allocator arena), under `~/.cache/mess-bench/global-measurement.lock`. Corpora deleted after; pgrep clean.\n\n| corpus | attach | open wall, 4 reps | RSS after open |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 8.006 GiB, 33 segs (32 sealed), 2338 MiB .pcol | eager (= trunk) | 727.7 / 726.1 / 738.4 / 727.8 ms | 2417 MB |\n| 8.006 GiB | LAZY | 13.22 / 13.37 / 12.94 / 13.85 ms | 22.7 MB |\n| 2.001 GiB, 9 segs (8 sealed), 586 MiB .pcol | eager (= trunk) | 179.8 / 183.1 / 179.9 / 181.0 ms | 609 MB |\n| 2.001 GiB | LAZY | 4.07 / 5.00 / 4.10 / 4.05 ms | 9.1 MB |\n\nBone targets at 8 GiB / 1k streams: open <20 ms -> **13.3 ms** (55x); RSS <30 MB -> **22.7 MB** (106x). Both met. bn-2u01's no-pcol counterfactual was 8.2 ms / 19 MB; the ~5 ms gap is the 7.7 MB of block tables the lazy attach does read and parse (240k blocks x 32 B).\n\nSealed READ after the attach (40 whole streams, 312k events, 2 GiB corpus) — the path the floors do NOT cover, since the bench seals in-process and reads through the memory-attached index:\nLAZY 1.709 / 1.673 M ev/s (RSS after read 71 MB) vs EAGER 1.723 / 1.768 M ev/s (RSS 672 MB). ~3% slower: the per-block pread + crc32c, absorbed by the decoded-block cache.\n\n## Floors: mess-bench full, ABBA (trunk, bka2, bka2, trunk) — ALL 14 PASS IN ALL FOUR RUNS\n\n| metric | trunk mean | bka2 mean | note |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| phase5.sealed.bytes_per_event | 8.858 | 8.858 | identical in all 4 runs — split CRCs cost zero bytes (reserved fields) |\n| phase5.sealed.columnar_replay.ev_per_s | 16.16M | 15.40M | trunk's own two runs spread 15.32-17.00M; bka2's agree to 0.2% and sit inside that spread |\n| phase5.seal_verify.ev_per_s | 2.175M | 2.117M | -2.7%, within spread; seal now also crc32c's each block |\n| sealed.global_scan.ev_per_s | 2366M | 2175M | pointer path, untouched by this change |\n| sealed.stream_replay.ev_per_s | 1407M | 1348M | pointer path, untouched |\n| engine.sealed_replay.ev_per_s | 6.06M | 5.82M | seal-time attach still eager, so this path is byte-identical to trunk |\n| recovery.s_per_gib | 0.11 | 0.11 | |\n| mess_log.buffered/durable, foldchain, load_verified | ~ | ~ | within spread (trunk's load_verified alone spread 19%) |\n\nCaveat: the trunk arm is trunk HEAD 3c262ee7 (includes bn-ozi5); my base is its parent 54aaa20a. The reader_contention rows (head_reads +61%, resolve +51% in my favour) are a bn-ozi5 delta or noise, not mine — that workload never touches a `.pcol`. Host load 2.7-11 across the runs. Ledgers in the session scratchpad.\n\n## Tests\n\n- `cargo nextest run -p mess-index`: 144 pass (was 138), 5 skipped.\n- `cargo nextest run -p mess-store`: 185 pass, 5 skipped — including `engine_block_reads` (corrupt + truncated `.pcol` byte-identical to the oracle, now exercising read-time degradation), `engine_reopen`, `engine_seal_pack`, `snapshot_law`, `cache`.\n- New: `lazy_open_skips_payload_bytes_and_tears_surface_at_read` (DATA region destroyed, header/index intact -> eager `from_bytes` REJECTS the image while lazy `open` ATTACHES: the direct proof it never reads payload bytes; then the torn block errors on every read shape while every other block stays byte-exact); `lazy_and_eager_reads_agree_byte_for_byte` (point/range/block/all); `lazy_open_rejects_structural_damage` (index CRC, span out of range, footer magic, truncation, husk, bad magic, missing file); `legacy_sidecar_without_split_crcs_attaches_and_reads`; `lazy_index_serves_concurrent_readers`; driver-level `wrong_segment_pcol_dropped_at_open_torn_one_degrades_at_read`.\n- `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` green; `just fmt-check` clean; `cargo clippy -p mess-index --all-targets` adds no new warnings (3 pre-existing, in pack.rs/driver.rs). Did NOT run full `just test` (lead's call).\n- Instrumentation removed before commit: the `MESS_PCOL_EAGER` A/B switch and `crates/mess-store/examples/openscale.rs`. No mess-store source touched (bn-ozi5 was concurrently in that crate); the only files changed are mess-index `sealed/{payload,segment,driver}.rs` and mess-cli `verify.rs`.\n\n## Follow-ups (deliberately NOT in this bone)\n\n1. **Seal-time attach is still eager.** `SealDriver::encode_and_write_payload` returns `from_bytes(bytes)`, so a LIVE writer still holds every segment's whole `.pcol` in the heap after sealing it — the same unbounded residency, on the write side (8 GiB of appends = 2.3 GB retained). Left alone because `engine.sealed_replay` seals in-process and is the one floor it would move; wants its own bone (a one-line change plus a floors run).\n2. **One fd per sealed `.pcol`, opened at engine open** (mess-store's segment fds are opened lazily, per read). ~800 fds at 100 GiB with the log handles: fine at this host's 524288 limit, tight under a 1024 default. Fix if wanted: an fd pool keyed by segment, keeping the inode-pinning property above.\n3. `mess-index` is now explicitly unix-only (`std::os::unix::fs::FileExt`); it already was transitively via mess-log's runtime.\n4. SealPack's `KIND_PAYLOAD_COLUMNS` still parses eagerly through `from_bytes` — `open_pack` reads the whole pack anyway; Phase 8 already carries that note plus the `EVENT_TYPE_IDS` eager decode."}	blake3:y54wBXhM1OZ5P2lpxEsLcMVzzvUwUSF_v4V_tNAelw8
1785548422129482	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExzxE		item.create	bn-2gns	{"description":"Carries the items bn-ozi5 v1 deliberately deferred and bn-3l8n's adoption does not need for Fjall deletion: source-breaking Snapshottable/SnapshotStore change to stable aggregate/schema IDs + codec ID/version (no type_name/TypeId/unstable-hash identity), public SnapshotCompatibility + SnapshotCoverage types over the internal Coverage lattice, the ADR equal-coverage conflict rule (v1 ships last-write-wins matching fjall semantics — lead ruling on bn-ozi5, cr-2ywo0t), pinned-root bounded scan_snapshots(cursor, limit) for doctor/inspect at scale, and CertifiedSnapshotRef once the fold-chain hashes are wired. None of this blocks bn-fj34.","kind":"task","title":"Snapshot coverage/compatibility public API: the deferred ADR 0002 trait break"}	blake3:7g8HDuQmuVQzC2ZNKzYi2BLMZWqHYIG7m12MxEUomcg
