1785551941909808	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx1BE		item.comment	bn-fj34	{"body":"bn-fj34 done in workspace bn-fj34 (commit 47663ecf); review cr-32z77k requested from @mess-dev. Not self-approved, no push, no merge, claims held. (1/2: evidence. 2/2 follows with the surface accounting.)\n\n## Dependency exit (the gate)\n\n  $ cargo tree -i fjall            -> error: package ID specification `fjall` did not match any packages\n  $ cargo tree -i fjall --workspace -> same\n\nCargo.lock -161/+3. Dropped: fjall, lsm-tree, byteorder-lite, byteview, compare,\ncrossbeam-skiplist, enum_dispatch, flume, interval-heap, lz4_flex, self_cell, sfa,\ntwox-hash, varint-rs, xxhash-rust, plus duplicate-version entries of hashbrown and\nquick_cache (the workspace versions of both remain).\n\n## Annotated grep -rn \"fjall\" crates examples --include=*.rs --include=*.toml\n\n21 hits, ZERO live code and ZERO live claims. All prose about what fjall *was*, or\nabout immutable golden bytes:\n\nHISTORICAL NARRATIVE (11)\n  mess-store/tests/registry_perf.rs:5         \"the co-durable fjall name barrier ... disappears\"\n  mess-store/tests/engine_roll.rs:411,414     \"Before ... it wrote the name to fjall BEFORE\"\n  mess-store/src/registry/log.rs:18           \"not blocking Fjall retirement on the v4 ...\"\n  mess-store/src/engine.rs:282                \"replacing the fjall stream_names/type_names load\"\n  mess-store/src/engine.rs:2912               \"came from fjall, not the log\" (past tense)\n  mess-log/src/committer.rs:1366              the removed PreBarrier name-flush hook\n  mess-index/src/active.rs:35                 \"mirrors the fjall memtable shape the SPIKE measured\"\n  mess-cli/src/registryfold.rs:3              \"mess inspect USED TO read ... out of fjall's\"\n  mess-cli/src/store.rs:94                    \"bn-3l8n MOVED the sidecar FROM the fjall head table\"\n  social/src/store_backend.rs:21,91,117       pre-bn-3l8n legacy .snapshots dir comparison\n  mess-cli/tests/doctor_app_snapshots.rs:192  \"the same contract the fjall head buffer HAD\"\n\nMY OWN DELETION NOTES (3)\n  mess-index/Cargo.toml:9                     records what the removed dep was\n  mess-store/tests/pack_snapshot.rs:12,256    what the retired differential was called\n\nIMMUTABLE GOLDEN TARBALL CONTENTS (4)\n  mess-cli/tests/golden.rs:316,582,594,609    v3/v4 carry pre-pack fjall sidecar bytes; the check\n                                              asserts they MISS safely (bn-3l8n's reviewed\n                                              compatibility statement). Not regenerated.\n\ngrep -rn \"MetaStore\\|mess_index::meta\" crates examples -> ZERO hits.\n\n## Fresh + reopened store create no meta dir and no fjall file\n\nExtended the existing near-miss. engine_name_durability's\n`the_engine_opens_and_serves_with_the_whole_meta_directory_deleted` was vacuous once\nthe directory cannot exist (it only ever showed the dir was *ignorable*), so it became\ntwo positive tests:\n\n  a_fresh_and_a_reopened_store_lay_down_only_the_log_layout\n    enumerates the ENTIRE on-disk tree at four points (fresh+live, fresh+closed,\n    reopened, after a post-reopen append) and requires every entry to be\n    LOCK / seg-*.log / sealed/ / sealed/seg-*.{pidx,pcol,filter,par}. Explicit\n    named assertion that no `meta/` is ever created. Still asserts the store WORKS\n    (reads, heads, a new-name append) at each point.\n\n  an_app_store_with_snapshots_adds_the_pack_sidecar_and_nothing_else\n    fresh and reopened app store persisting real snapshots: no `meta/` in the store\n    root OR inside the sidecar root (the pre-bn-3l8n sidecar was itself {meta,blobs},\n    so that is the second place a KV store could reappear), and every sidecar entry\n    is LOCK / IDENTITY / *.pack / *.open / *.root.\n\n## Test evidence\n\n  cargo nextest run -p mess-store -p mess-index -p mess-cli -p social\n    MESS_SNAPSHOT_LAW_ITERS=25   ->   530 tests run: 530 passed, 16 skipped\n  cargo check --workspace --all-targets   ->   clean (bn-1qa)\n  just fmt-check                          ->   clean\n  clippy: warning-site diff vs trunk is ZERO net-new. Only delta is two pre-existing\n    type_complexity hits in engine.rs shifting 579/585 -> 587/593 because I added\n    doc lines above them.\n  mess --help and mess rebuild-index --help confirm --meta is gone from the surface.\n\nDid NOT run full `just test` or mess-bench, per instruction — the lead gates the merge."}	blake3:TtZZN61GOBapfQsqJfuP3WsuroqW9HMSy3GNDzZkHpc
1785551977209489	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx1RE		item.comment	bn-fj34	{"body":"bn-fj34 (2/2): removed public surfaces, the one non-mechanical finding, and test/doc rework.\n\n## Every removed public surface, and what happened to it\n\nDELETED\n  mess_index::meta                    whole module — MetaStore, all 7 keyspaces, MetaTable,\n                                      CommitGroup, Head, SnapshotHead, StreamId, Dump, MetaError,\n                                      DEFAULT_DEDUPE_CAPACITY, public codec fns\n  mess-index tests/meta.rs            the checkpoint API's only caller (bn-11mk confirmed)\n  mess-index tests/bench_lookup.rs    synthetic component baseline; research 13 §7.3 says do not\n                                      retain fjall just to keep it runnable in a production crate\n  mess_store::fjall_snapshot          FjallSnapshotBackend + SnapshotBackendError (+ its suite)\n  EngineOptions::dedupe_capacity      VERIFIED DEAD, not just fjall-flavoured: declared and\n                                      defaulted, read nowhere. Confirms bn-2ctq. Removed.\n  rebuild-index --meta                flag + RebuildOptions::meta + rebuild_meta(). Nothing read\n                                      the table; the generic group write also advanced snapshot\n                                      and dedupe high-waters it had written nothing to.\n  backup collect_meta() + role \"meta\" bn-2di made $registry the name authority, so the last\n                                      non-rebuildable artifact is gone. The cut is the log alone.\n  store::meta_dir()\n  metaread::is_locked_error()         matched fjall's \"FjallError: Locked\" display string\n  metaread::snapshots_of/decode_ref   the legacy dense-keyed source + its 14-byte ref decode\n  doctor \"meta-store-locked\"          + degraded_fold_version_finding()\n  doctor \"registry-unavailable\"\n  inspect \"registry-unavailable\" advice\n  retention \"snapshots-unavailable\" advice\n\nRENAMED\n  EngineError::Meta -> ::Registry     display \"meta: {0}\" -> \"registry: {0}\". Every construction\n                                      is registry-fold / name-density / dangling-id integrity;\n                                      zero callers outside engine.rs. Trimmed the now-redundant\n                                      \"registry: \" message prefixes so it reads once.\n\nDEMOTED / RE-ANCHORED (kept, meaning changed)\n  inspect registry.snapshots_available  pinned `true` and documented. Kept ONLY for JSON schema\n                                        stability — there is no \"unavailable\" state left, only an\n                                        empty set. Say the word if you'd rather drop the field.\n  inspect \"stream-name-lookup-unavailable\"  condition moved meta.is_err() -> folded.is_err(); the\n                                        one remaining state where a name filter cannot match.\n  metaread::read                        -> MetaFacts (was Result<MetaFacts, String>). Infallible.\n  metaread::LiveSnapshot                lost Copy, gained `stream: String` (see finding below).\n\n## FINDING — retention explain was silently vacuous. Please review this part.\n\nNot introduced here: bn-3l8n shipped it, and deleting the legacy source exposed it.\n`decide_segment` matches LiveSnapshotRef::stream_id against the SEALED INDEX's ids,\nwhich are the engine's interned DENSE ids. A pack snapshot's stream_id is\ninterim_stream_id() — an FNV hash of the name. Different id spaces. So against any\nreal app store every segment reported `deletable` no matter what blocked it —\nvacuous in the DANGEROUS direction, for a read-only explainer that fronts a\ndestructive operation. The only reason the blocked-case test passed is that its\nfixture wrote a legacy <dir>/meta head, dense-keyed by construction.\n\nDeleting that source left a choice: drop the blocked-case coverage, or fix the join.\nI fixed it, because the AC \"doctor, inspect, retention ... use the self-describing\nsnapshot replacement\" is exactly this. LiveSnapshot now carries the pack record's\nself-describing stream NAME (ADR 0002 §1's whole point), and retention::run resolves\nname -> dense id via registryfold::fold — offline, lock-free, the same fold inspect\nalready uses. Unresolvable names drop out with an advisory; an unfoldable $registry\nraises a Warn `registry-unfoldable` stating the verdicts account for leases only,\ninstead of silently under-reporting an empty live set. Fail-closed.\n\nThe new test asserts the reported live_snapshots are in the DENSE space AND that the\ntwo spaces genuinely differ, so it cannot silently go vacuous again.\n\nIf you'd rather ship the deletion alone, this is a separable hunk:\nretention.rs + metaread.rs (the `stream` field) + retention_explain.rs.\n\n## Test rework\n\n  pack_snapshot.rs   pack_and_fjall_backends_return_byte_identical_snapshots ->\n                     pack_snapshots_match_their_replay_derived_expectation. Per the lead ruling:\n                     same seed (0x0DDB_A11B_ADC0_FFEE), same 120 iterations, same corpus, every\n                     pack-side assertion KEPT and re-anchored on replay-derived expectations —\n                     covered version / empty-prefix flag / fold_version vs the appended prefix,\n                     the PERSISTED state blob byte-compared against fold(prefix), loaded ref ==\n                     saved ref, accelerated load == fold(all) at the full-replay version,\n                     events_replayed == n - p, full.events_replayed == n. Only the fjall arm went.\n  doctor_lock_state.rs      premise INVERTED. Holds the real D9 lock (a live LogEngine + sidecar)\n                            rather than simulating a fjall lock, and asserts the fold-version\n                            check RUNS against a live writer — the degradation bn-ve0 could only\n                            apologise for. Plus: meta-store-locked never reappears, envelope keys\n                            stay lock-independent, registry-store-locked stays info/healthy exit.\n  inspect_report_shape.rs   same treatment; the snapshot half is now proven lock-independent too.\n  retention_explain.rs      fixture is a real PackSnapshotBackend save at the backend seam.\n  rebuild_byte_equal.rs     RebuildOptions{dry_run:false}.\n  doctor_app_snapshots.rs   metaread::read no longer .expect()ed.\n  golden.rs                 the bare `store.join(\"meta\").is_dir()` assertion is RECLASSIFIED, not\n                            dropped: it now reads \"this immutable fixture is the legacy shape it\n                            is supposed to be\", parallel to step 6's pre-pack sidecar assertion.\n                            Tarball bytes untouched, as instructed.\n\n## Docs\n\n  docs/spec/07-backup.md   §1.2 rewritten. This spec was materially WRONG on trunk: it still\n                           called stream_names/type_names the durable source of truth and said\n                           the cut MUST include meta/. bn-2di moved the bijection into $registry;\n                           there is now no non-rebuildable metadata at all. §3 layout and §4\n                           restore text follow.\n  docs/testing.md, README.md crate table, engine.rs + pack_snapshot rustdoc, mess-testkit tempdir.\n  UNTOUCHED historical records: ADR 0002, notes/, spikes/, docs/perf/envelope.md,\n  docs/perf/experiments-d10.md, and the golden v3/v4 tarballs."}	blake3:3DOJ4XsrKPQNEp3sWoh6SAoSuuH3u-FqQQfNRX9EFbY
1785552106423341	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx1hE		item.move	bn-fj34	{"state":"done"}	blake3:JUP0CQIGAeOUu-IZShY1PMqTbJ3YgGz_8fhvIDrNeXQ
1785552373426355	mess-regdelta	itc:v3:AWKc8nJM5JmTkxAAAAAAQbvXVura6wAAAAExAQ		item.move	bn-26pp	{"state":"doing"}	blake3:uMwq_8GU-nk8hDQq3g9c8t5_RjJHjd6CG-XlCv-VLW0
1785552373438701	mess-regdelta	itc:v3:AWKc8nJM5JmTkxAAAAAAQbvXVura6wAAAAExAg		item.assign	bn-26pp	{"action":"assign","agent":"mess-regdelta"}	blake3:ASSWRsRmn4rZfShdnLY9IkOhmkjw4hUlCSekKphp6ws
1785556197399780	mess-regdelta	itc:v3:AWKc8nJM5JmTkxAAAAAAQbvXVura6wAAAAExAw		item.comment	bn-26pp	{"body":"DELIVERS — 38.5x cold open at 8 GiB/250k streams, no regression anywhere measured. RECOMMENDATION: KEEP (merge) and DECLINE bn-18y1 + bn-3ctl below ~1M streams. Part 1/2: what was built + the ABBA tables.\n\n## What was built\n\nWorkspace bn-26pp, commit e11fad5f (on epoch 1b462430, i.e. after bn-bka2's lazy .pcol). Review cr-b61os0, NOT self-approved. 7 files, +1562/-10.\n\nPLACEMENT: a `.reg` SIBLING FILE per sealed segment (`sealed/seg-<id:020>.reg`), not a `.pidx` section and not (only) SealPack's reserved REGISTRY_DELTA. Reasons, in order:\n1. SealPack is off by default (Phase 8), so a section there helps nobody today — bn-2u01 already said this and it is still true.\n2. Splicing a variable-length region into `.pidx` means a format_version bump for every reader of every existing store: its footer commits fixed-width offsets validated by an exact content_crc AND an exact `footer_start - dir_off == dir_len` invariant. This is verbatim the argument `sealed::filter` makes for `.filter` being its own file.\n3. A sibling file costs a pre-existing store exactly nothing: no `.reg`, no migration, no rewrite, old path.\nNew module `mess-index/src/sealed/regdelta.rs` owns the format (magic + format_version + flags word, crc32c over the whole image, strictly-bounded parse that must end exactly at the footer), the path helper, and the cross-check predicate.\n\nCONTENT: for each stream-0 batch in the segment, `(first_global_pos, [payload bytes])` — exactly the tuples `registry::Fold::push_batch` wants. Taken from `SealInput::payloads`, which the sealer already holds because it re-read the rolled segment to build `.pidx`/`.pcol`. Segments with no registration get NO FILE (the common case; the old path already costs nothing there).\n\nSAFETY (the log stays sole authority — bn-2di/D1, I5). A delta is used only if ALL of these hold, else the existing pread path runs unchanged:\n- crc32c over the whole image matches, magic/version/flags known, structure exact;\n- segment_id, base_pos and event_count equal the pointer sidecar's;\n- the delta's `(first_global_pos, frame_count)` list is EXACTLY the list that sidecar's own directory yields for that stream, same order.\nSo a delta can only ever supply the payload BYTES of batches the CRC-validated, footer-cross-checked pointer sidecar already agrees exist, at the positions it already agrees they occupy. It cannot invent, drop, or move a batch.\n\nREAD ONCE, NEVER RETAINED. The delta is opened during recovery, drained into the fold, dropped. It is NOT attached to `SealedSegmentIndex` and the sealer does not keep the copy it wrote. At 250k streams the deltas are 12.2 MiB; at 10M they would be ~0.5 GiB, and this is exactly the regime the format exists for — retaining them would trade a cold-open win for a permanent RSS loss. (First cut did attach them at open, `.filter`-style; measured, then changed. Post-open RSS came out 11% BELOW trunk, below.)\n\nBlast radius: sealer emission (driver.rs), open consumption (engine.rs `recover`'s sidecar-trusted branch only), the format module, one `&self` predicate on `SealedSegmentIndex`, and a compile-time assert in mess-store that mess-index's restated REGISTRY_STREAM_ID still equals the real one. No public API removed or changed.\n\n## Method\n\nHost Threadripper 9970X, ext4 on Samsung 9100 PRO PCIe5 NVMe, 125 GiB RAM. bn-2u01's `openscale` driver recovered from its snapshot and adapted (probe module dropped; added a `reg on|off` switch and best/median/worst summaries). INSTRUMENTATION NEVER MERGED — it lived as an uncommitted example, is deleted from the workspace, and the trunk-baseline workspace is destroyed.\n\nCorpora built through the real `LogEngine::append_batch`, Durability::Process, production-default 256 MiB segments, 250 B JSON-ish payloads, 100-event batches, N streams round-robin, engine dropped so every roll-seal drains. Same shape bn-2u01 used.\n\nKEY METHODOLOGICAL POINT: both arms measure THE SAME CORPUS BYTES. The corpus is built once with the workspace build (so `.reg` files exist); trunk ignores the `.reg` extension entirely (`load_sealed`'s match arms), so corpus variance is eliminated as a confound — the only difference between arms is whether the reader uses the deltas.\n\nABBA (trunk, mine, mine, trunk) per cell. Cold = `posix_fadvise(DONTNEED)` over every segment+sidecar before each rep, best of 3. Warm = best of 6. Serial, under `flock $HOME/.cache/mess-bench/global-measurement.lock`. Load gate < 5.0 checked before every pass; the reported run ran at 1-min load 1.97-3.28 throughout (host baseline 2-3), and it reproduced an earlier run taken at load 2.1-2.9 to within 3%.\n\n## Cell 1 — 8 GiB / 250k streams, COLD (best of 3, ms)\n\n  A1 trunk  9523.4        B1 mine   243.7\n  A2 trunk  9515.9        B2 mine   250.3\n  trunk mean 9519.7  ->  mine mean 247.0    38.5x   (-97.4%)\n\nA1 vs A2 agree to 0.08%; B1 vs B2 to 2.7%.\n\n## Cell 2 — 8 GiB / 250k streams, WARM (best of 6, ms)\n\n  A1 trunk   483.7        B1 mine   205.5\n  A2 trunk   481.8        B2 mine   191.9\n  trunk mean 482.8   ->  mine mean 198.7     2.43x\n\n## Cell 3 — 8 GiB / 1k streams, COLD (best of 3, ms)  [the no-regression cell]\n\n  A1 trunk    65.11       B1 mine    26.70\n  A2 trunk    65.00       B2 mine    26.70\n  trunk mean  65.06  ->  mine mean  26.70     2.44x\n\n## Cell 4 — 8 GiB / 1k streams, WARM (best of 6, ms)\n\n  A1 trunk     9.73       B1 mine     8.66\n  A2 trunk    10.69       B2 mine     9.40\n  trunk mean  10.21  ->  mine mean   9.03     1.13x\n\nThe low-stream cell IMPROVES rather than regressing: 999 registrations still cost 999 cold preads on trunk (65.06 - 26.70 = 38.4 ms, i.e. 38 us/pread — exactly bn-2u01's 37.5 us unit rate).\n\n## Cell 5 — within-binary control (same binary, `.reg` renamed to `.reg.off`)\n\n  cold  NOREG 9520.3  ->  REG 251.8    37.8x\n  warm  NOREG  507.9  ->  REG 212.0     2.40x\n\nThis is the null control that matters: identical binary, identical corpus, the only variable is whether the deltas are visible. It reproduces the trunk-vs-mine result exactly, so the effect is the deltas and nothing else about the two builds.\n\n## Cell 6 — noise floor (consecutive identical passes, warm 250k, best of 6, ms)\n\n  trunk 540.5 vs 484.8   -> 11.5% spread\n  mine  194.6 vs 211.8   ->  8.8% spread\n\nWarm 250k numbers carry ~+/-10% run-to-run noise; the cold numbers do not (0.08%). Both effects are far outside the floor."}	blake3:PziaPvgA7o1L1axAxw1D-vPbKVjvW3WH09UDJaraD2o
1785556205698828	mess-regdelta	itc:v3:AWKc8nJM5JmTkxAAAAAAQbvXVura6wAAAAExBA		item.comment	bn-26pp	{"body":"(bn-26pp, part 2/2 — continues the previous comment: costs, gates, differential evidence, recommendation, caveats.)\n\n## What it costs\n\nDISK. c8s250k: 12.2 MiB of `.reg` across 27 of 32 sealed segments, on 8.006 GiB of log + 2.31 GiB of sidecars = +0.5% of sidecar bytes, +0.12% of the store. ~51 B per registered name. c8s1k: 0.04 MiB, one file (all 1000 names register in segment 0). Zero for a segment with no registration.\n\nSEAL PATH. Separate ABBA (4 GiB / 125k streams, build the same corpus with each binary, 4 passes):\n  append wall     trunk 8.09 / 8.36 s     mine 8.02 / 8.21 s   (untouched, as expected)\n  seal drain      trunk 24.52 / 23.36 s   mine 25.09 / 23.95 s  -> +2.4%, INSIDE the 4.7% trunk-vs-trunk spread\n  sealed bytes    trunk 1.154 GiB         mine 1.160 GiB        (+6.1 MiB for 125k names)\nOne extra small crash-atomic write (temp -> fsync -> rename -> dir fsync) per segment that carries a registration, best-effort exactly like `.filter`: a write failure never fails the seal.\n\nRSS after open (rep 0, 8 GiB / 250k): trunk +173.5 MB, mine +153.9 MB — 11% LOWER, not higher, because the pread path's 250k transient batch buffers cost more retained arena than reading 27 files does. (Allocator behaviour, not a designed property; the designed property is that the delta itself is not retained at all.)\n\n## Gates\n\n- `cargo nextest run --workspace`: 1044 passed, 27 skipped, 0 failed (includes mess-soak smoke).\n- `cargo nextest run -p mess-index -p mess-store`: 383 passed.\n- `cargo check --workspace --all-targets`: clean. `just fmt-check`: clean. `cargo clippy -p mess-index -p mess-store --all-targets`: no new warnings in touched code (the 8 that fire are all pre-existing).\n- mess-bench `run --mode full` against floors.json: **PASS, all 14 floor-gated metrics**. recovery.s_per_gib 0.11, recovery.serial.s_per_gib 0.23, recovery.wall_s 0.06 — the recovery.* floors are the direct guard and they are unmoved.\n\n## Differential evidence (folded-from-sidecar == folded-from-log)\n\nNew suite `mess-store/tests/registry_delta_differential.rs`, seeded random histories (random stream counts 8-47, random append order biased so new names keep appearing, random batch sizes 1-4, 1-6 message types, 96 KiB segments so many segments roll and seal):\n\n1. `sidecar_records_are_byte_identical_to_the_log` (6 seeds) — for every `.reg` on disk, its `(first_global_pos, payloads)` list is BYTE-IDENTICAL to the list an independent raw scan of that segment's log bytes produces for stream 0. This is the strongest form of the claim and it is checked at the bytes, not at the state.\n2. `every_fallback_recovers_the_same_registry` (6 seeds x 5 conditions) — the engine opened with deltas present, with every `.reg` deleted, with half deleted, and with one truncated + one bit-flipped + one replaced by a valid delta from another segment, all produce the identical observable state (every name -> id, every id -> name, every head, total_events) AND agree with an independent OFFLINE FOLD OF THE RAW LOG computed in-test (the same thing `mess-cli::registryfold` does, re-implemented so no engine code is shared with the oracle). Both high-water marks are compared and every id in `1..=hwm` enumerated, so an extra/missing/renamed entry cannot hide.\n3. `a_legacy_store_upgrades_in_place` — a store whose segments were sealed before this format reopens identically, then appends seal new segments WITH deltas while the old ones keep the pread path; the mixed store still folds to what the log says.\nPlus, in mess-index: driver tests for emission / no-file-when-no-registration / refusal of missing, truncated, bit-flipped, foreign, and layout-mismatched deltas; and unit tests that reject EVERY single-byte flip at every offset, EVERY truncation length, unknown version, unknown flag bits, and non-ascending batches.\n\n## Projections (extrapolation, stated as such)\n\nUnit rates from the two cells: cold open ~= 0.0033 s/GiB (segment headers + `.pidx` + `.pcol` headers + filters + suffix) + 0.88 us per registered name (sequential `.reg` read + record decode + `RegistryState::apply` + Book build). Trunk's per-name term is 37.5-38 us (one cold random pread), unchanged.\n  100 GiB / 1M streams   cold: trunk ~38 s  ->  mine ~1.2 s   (~32x)\n  100 GiB / 100k streams cold: trunk ~4.6 s ->  mine ~0.42 s\nNote bn-2u01's 50.0 s figure for the first cell predates bn-bka2; with lazy `.pcol` merged, trunk is ~38 s there.\n\n## Recommendation against the decision rule\n\nCold open at 250k streams improves 38.5x (gate: >=5x). No warm regression (warm improves 2.43x at 250k, 1.13x at 1k). No low-stream regression (1k cold improves 2.44x). Floors green. => **KEEP: merge, and DECLINE bn-18y1 and bn-3ctl.**\n\nThe sharpest way to put the decline: bn-18y1's own rescoped acceptance gate was \"<0.5 s cold and <0.2 s warm on a 100k-stream store\". This bone delivers 0.247 s cold / 0.199 s warm at **250k** streams — the checkpoint's gate is met at 2.5x its stream count, by a sidecar with no manifest, no anchor, no alternating pointers, no GC and no interruption-safe install protocol. bn-2u01's own condition (\"a full checkpoint only earns its keep above ~1M streams, and only after both this and the .pcol fix\") is now satisfied on both counts.\n\nIf a >1M-stream target ever becomes real, reopen bn-18y1 scoped at the RESIDUAL, which is no longer I/O: it is the 0.88 us/name fold (decode each `$registry` record, apply it, build the Book's maps and name arcs). A kernel snapshot would rehydrate maps at ~80 ns/entry instead, so ~10x remains available there — but it is a CPU/state-rehydration bone, not a \"stop scanning the log\" bone, and it should be scoped and justified as such.\n\n## Caveats, honestly\n\n- ONE SHAPE, one host. 250 B payloads, 100-event batches, and exactly one registration batch per name (bn-2u01 measured N_REG_PREADS == N_STREAMS-1; same here). A workload that mints many names inside one append unit batches its registrations and therefore both costs less today and gains less from this.\n- Cold cache is `posix_fadvise(DONTNEED)`, not a cold boot, on a PCIe5 NVMe where sequential cold ~= warm. The win is entirely about eliminating RANDOM reads, so it should hold or grow on slower/rotational/network storage.\n- Trunk's warm advantage is fragile in a way mine's is not: in the null-control cell, trunk's first warm rep after a mine-only sequence measured 9.46 s, because mine never warms the log's 250k scattered pages (it reads ~33 MB total). Trunk's \"warm\" number requires 2.3 GB of scattered log pages resident.\n- `.reg` is NOT in `mess-cli`'s backup file list and NOT checked by `mess verify`. A restored-from-backup store silently falls back to the pread path — correct, just slower. Small follow-up; deliberately out of this bone's blast radius.\n- SealPack (`seal_pack = true`, off by default) does not emit the reserved REGISTRY_DELTA section, so a pack-sealed store keeps the pread path. Correct, not accelerated. Follow-up if/when Phase 8 turns the flag on; the format and the cross-check are reusable as-is.\n- Not measured: chain-on opens, multi-segment crash recovery, open under concurrent append, stores >8 GiB, stream counts >250k (streams <= batches by construction, same limit bn-2u01 hit).\n- Warm 250k measurements carry ~+/-10% run-to-run noise (Cell 6). The 2.43x warm claim is well outside it; a hypothetical <1.2x warm claim would not have been.\n\nWorkspace bn-26pp is committed-unintegrated and held; bone stays `doing`; claims held. No push, no merge, no self-approval. Corpora deleted, trunk-baseline workspace destroyed, `pgrep` clean, /home at 33%."}	blake3:ihuwRMGJ5FWcP5UD4e_NVFVQocFTRUzHfUnbySvCiVY
1785556407092171	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx1xE		item.move	bn-26pp	{"state":"done"}	blake3:pYDKCAXgD-myCHPVcplADcYJLMhH3KbT2Pl6Yn1J8YI
1785556417601377	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx2BE		item.comment	bn-18y1	{"body":"DECLINED (bn-26pp, 2026-07-31). The rescoped gate this bone carried — cold open <0.5s / warm <0.2s at 100k streams — is met at 250k streams (0.247s / 0.199s) by the merged .reg registry-delta sidecar, with no manifest, anchor, alternating pointers, GC, or install protocol. The residual above ~1M streams is no longer I/O (0.88us/name of record decode + RegistryState::apply + Book build) — if a >1M-stream target ever becomes real, reopen as a state-rehydration bone scoped at that, not as checkpoint machinery."}	blake3:_lB2kptynp0yGdWGe4ClRUQFO-WjMGY7IYloxX3yVTg
1785556417628498	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx2RE		item.comment	bn-3ctl	{"body":"DECLINED with bn-18y1 (bn-26pp, 2026-07-31): checkpoint-plus-suffix recovery has nothing to recover from — there are no checkpoints. Open already selects sidecar fast paths per segment with per-segment fallback to the canonical log, which is this bone's fallback-lattice idea realized at smaller scale. The 100M-event corpus measurement and recovery-evidence records ride the existing recovery.* floors and doctor surfaces."}	blake3:7XbSmDJBXh0AjCQxifebpbWlPuDhaDrIfORt1ZkDQZs
1785556417644914	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx2hE		item.move	bn-18y1	{"state":"done"}	blake3:a3ZDmsoG_d8qVj21htTC63EyNZY0t9cutGE6DKgRYw4
1785556417662222	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx2xE		item.move	bn-3ctl	{"state":"done"}	blake3:2gSHlq3ZKIm8L8K242FLub7a5oBP6Sgv1g2jiMBttrg
1785556417665114	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx3BE		item.move	bn-35f2	{"reason":"auto-completed: all children of bn-35f2 are done","state":"done"}	blake3:TdE_Hx17IJAcNDtJlP_Wj1noH5O8DpshX1GsSo5cFFY
1785556430802258	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx3RE		item.comment	bn-35f2	{"body":"Phase 7 closed, 2026-07-31, one session. Track B shipped whole: pack snapshot sidecar core (bn-ozi5), end-to-end adoption (bn-3l8n), Fjall deleted with 16 transitive crates (bn-fj34) — the one-authority architecture is real. Track A: the premise check (bn-2u01) found the roadmap's two mechanisms aimed at costs that no longer exist (open decodes zero payloads; apply is per-batch), and found the real ones — .pcol eager loading (fixed in bn-bka2, 55x warm open) and registry-rebuild random preads (fixed in bn-26pp, 38.5x cold open at 250k streams). bn-1mzi/bn-2dbp/bn-18y1/bn-3ctl all closed DECLINED with the measurements on each; none of the algebra/checkpoint machinery was built. Side products: retention explain de-vacuoused (bn-fj34), full suite 13x faster (pack adoption removed fjall opens from the long-pole test), SealPack audit notes on Phase 8. Follow-ups filed: .reg in backup/verify surfaces; coverage/compatibility public API bone carries the deferred ADR items."}	blake3:dMYTjQjjxLHFn9YOY1zTgGRbxLTiUjtPehid73dVhEg
1785556442121863	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx3hE		item.create	bn-w5my	{"description":"Follow-up from bn-26pp: sealed/seg-*.reg files are not yet in mess backup's file list (a restored store silently falls back to the pread open path — correct but unaccelerated) and mess verify does not check them (a corrupt .reg is silently ignored at open, also correct, but verify --full should REPORT it the way it reports .pcol/.pidx damage). Small, mechanical: add the extension to the backup cut list with the same discardable classification as .pcol/.filter, and a verify_reg pass mirroring verify_pcol (CRC + layout cross-check vs the pointer directory via accepts_registry_delta).","kind":"task","title":"Add .reg registry-delta sidecars to backup and verify surfaces"}	blake3:DJ4yX2OgVxBgSxUJx6b1Gfzz9RCQbtmjWCnKtgVA-TU
1785556442145224	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx3xE		item.comment	bn-35fy	{"body":"Second note from Phase 7 (bn-26pp): SealPack's reserved REGISTRY_DELTA section is still unemitted, so a pack-sealed store keeps the O(#names) pread path at open — correct but unaccelerated, and at odds with the 38.5x cold-open win .reg gives loose-sidecar stores. When Phase 8 defaults SealPack, either emit the reserved section (reusing regdelta's encoder and the accepts_registry_delta cross-check) or accept the regression knowingly. Pairs with the earlier EVENT_TYPE_IDS eager-decode note."}	blake3:TE1rI__msg_WqJyVfrm5lya83iGdDu6qzRSAkgNzvXg
1785556778579071	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx4BE		item.create	bn-2lir	{"description":"2026-07-31, one occurrence in six full-suite runs: mess_db rocks::read::test::test_get_messages::it_gets_messages_starting_from_given_pos panicked unwrapping DB::new — 'While lock file: /tmp/331hkm-rpc3915t-8esgpx/LOCK: Resource temporarily unavailable' (flock EWOULDBLOCK). Not reproducible: 1x isolated, 3x module (11 tests), and a full 1044/1044 rerun all green; no stray processes held it (pgrep clean, dir already swept). Forensics so far: SelfDestructingDB::new_tmp names the dir with ident Id::new() = ms-timestamp<<64 | fastrand::u128 (ident 2.0.0 git, fastrand 2.5.0, RandomState-seeded per process) — a cross-process path collision needs identical timestamp AND identical 64 random bits, ~2^-64, so the obvious theory does not hold. Open question: how does a freshly minted path's LOCK come back EWOULDBLOCK? Candidates worth checking when it recurs: rstest case expansion sharing state in one process; RocksDB lock-release ordering in SelfDestructingDB::drop racing dir removal; EAGAIN from something other than contention. Legacy crate, untouched by current work; filed for the record per test-the-mechanism discipline — do not paper over with a retry loop until the mechanism is named.","kind":"bug","title":"flaky: mess_db rocks test hit EWOULDBLOCK on its own fresh RocksDB LOCK file (1/6 full-suite runs)"}	blake3:0VX7fQq_-TgT7eFOjLNp7FoWNcItBRFrLMUaQpXYcaY
