1785599935864653	mess-foldhash	itc:v3:AWLmTJP-SSZnkQAAAAAAQe1q_6q29AAAAAEvAw		item.comment	bn-dcr	{"body":"## bn-dcr premise measurement — REPRODUCED, ADOPTED\n\n### Finding that changes the framing\n\nThe bone (and the task brief) describe the HashMap as the **sparse-region\nfallback** left behind after bitrank landed. That is not what the code does.\n`DIRCODEC_BITRANK` (crates/mess-index/src/sealed/pack.rs:479 `should_use_bitrank`)\nis a **serialization codec only**: `decode_stream_directory` rehydrates *both*\ncodecs into `Vec<DirEntryRaw>`, and `SealedSegmentIndex::from_pack` inserts\nevery entry into the same `HashMap<u64, DirEntry>` regardless. There is no\nin-memory bitrank lookup. So this map is the in-memory directory for **every**\nsealed segment, dense and sparse alike — the opportunity is strictly larger\nthan the bone assumed, not smaller.\n\n### Method\n\nScratch harness (NOT committed; session scratchpad), mirroring spike H's\n`h0_hashmap_sip` vs `h0f_hashmap_fold` arms: byte-identical 48-byte DirEntry\npayload, same query mix (75% uniform hit / 25% mostly-in-span miss), same\nbatch32 throughput loop and serial dependency-chain loop, rdtsc/rdtscp with\nmeasured overhead subtracted, thread pinned to core 24.\n\nDeliberate deviations from the spike:\n- **Null-control arm**: a *second*, independently-seeded SipHash map measured\n  alongside. Its apparent \"win\" over the first SipHash map bounds the\n  harness's own noise floor.\n- **Palindromic ABBA ordering** (A F B B F A per rep) so monotone drift cancels.\n- **Production codegen**: plain `--release`, no `-C target-cpu=native` (the\n  spike used native; the mess workspace sets no release-profile overrides and\n  no rustflags, so native would not ship).\n- **Different host**: spike ran on a Ryzen 9 3900X (Zen 2); this is a\n  Threadripper 9970X (Zen 5), governor `powersave`. Absolute ns are not\n  comparable to the spike's; the relative delta is.\n\nCorpus: the spike's own two engine-written stores are still on disk at\n`~/.cache/mess-bench-scratch/directory_tournament_store{,_big}` — 39 real\nsealed `.pidx` directories, n=109..8056, U/n 1.00..93.3 — so the real corpus\nshapes are the spike's, not a synthesis. Synthetic dense/sparse/clustered\nshapes added on top.\n\n3 independent 51-rep runs; loadavg 2.67 / 2.88 / 2.83 at run start.\nContaminated cells would show up in the null control; they did not.\n\n### Table (median of 3 runs; delta = fold vs SipHash, + is faster)\n\n```\n                                        batch32 p50 ns/op   batch32 p99 ns/op    serial ns/op\nshape                       n     U/n    sip  fold     D%    sip  fold     D%    sip  fold     D%   null%\nREAL engine segments (39) 2017     3.9  6.57  3.44  +47.7   8.14  5.01  +38.5  12.27  8.91  +27.4   +0.0\nsynth n=1k    (all 4 shapes)      6.57  3.44  +47.7   8.14  5.01  +38.5  11.78  8.35  +29.0\nsynth n=10k   dense U/n=2       7.20  3.44  +52.2   9.08  5.63  +38.0  13.75 10.19  +25.9\nsynth n=10k   sparse U/n=64     7.20  3.75  +47.9   9.08  5.63  +38.0  13.86 10.38  +25.1\nsynth n=10k   sparse U/n=1000   7.20  3.75  +47.9   9.38  5.32  +43.3  13.91 10.35  +25.6\nsynth n=10k   zipf-clustered    7.20  3.44  +52.2   8.76  5.32  +39.3  13.89 10.33  +25.6\nsynth n=100k  dense U/n=2      10.33  5.31  +48.6  13.77  9.08  +34.0  21.44 17.64  +17.7\nsynth n=100k  sparse U/n=64    10.32  5.32  +48.4  13.45  9.07  +32.6  21.43 17.67  +17.6\nsynth n=100k  sparse U/n=1000  10.32  5.01  +51.5  13.46  8.45  +37.2  21.50 17.62  +18.1\nsynth n=100k  zipf-clustered   10.02  5.00  +50.1  13.45  8.45  +37.2  21.44 17.66  +17.6\nsynth n=1M    dense U/n=2      15.02  5.63  +62.5  24.10 10.33  +57.1  34.80 30.38  +12.7\nsynth n=1M    sparse U/n=64    15.96  5.63  +64.7  25.66 10.01  +61.0  34.48 30.44  +11.7\nsynth n=1M    sparse U/n=1000  17.21  5.95  +65.5  29.72 11.27  +62.1  34.70 30.90  +10.9\nsynth n=1M    zipf-clustered   16.27  5.63  +65.4  25.98 10.33  +60.2  34.47 30.43  +11.7\n(synth null-control column: median 0.0%, worst cell -7.2% at n=1M sparse1000)\n```\n\nREAL per-segment batch win distribution:\nmin +28.1 / p25 +47.6 / **median +47.7** / p75 +50.0 / max +52.4 —\n**39/39 real segments clear the +20% bar.**\n\nNull control: median |delta| 0.00%, p90 ~4%, worst single cell 22% on\n`real-s1-seg26-n109` (the smallest directory, 109 keys, entirely L1-resident,\nwhere TSC quantization is 0.31 ns/op and a different `RandomState` seed\ngenuinely reshuffles probe lengths). Every other real segment's null control\nsat under 8%. That one segment's individual win is the least trustworthy\nnumber in the table and it does not move the median.\n\nRun-to-run: REAL median batch win +52.1 / +47.6 / +47.7 across the three\n51-rep runs; serial +27.8 / +27.3 / +28.0. Stable.\n\n### Decision: ADOPT (rule: >= 20%)\n\nReproduces at **+47.7% median batch** on the real corpus shapes — 2.4x the\nbar, versus the spike's +53% claim on a different host. Also holds on every\nsynthetic sparse and clustered shape (+47.7 .. +65.5%), i.e. the win is not\ndensity-dependent, which makes sense: it is a hasher swap, not a\nrepresentation change. Serial-chain latency (+27.4% real) tracks the spike's\n+29%.\n\nHonest scoping note: this is a **3.1 ns/lookup** saving. It is real, it\nreproduces, and it is essentially free (one type alias, zero new packages) —\nbut it is one directory probe inside a sealed read that costs orders of\nmagnitude more, so do not expect it in the envelope floors. It did not appear\nthere (all 14 PASS, unchanged).\n\n### Change\n\n- `crates/mess-index/Cargo.toml`: `foldhash = \"0.2\"`, default features only.\n- `crates/mess-index/src/sealed/segment.rs`:\n  - new `type DirMap = HashMap<u64, DirEntry, foldhash::fast::RandomState>`\n    with the collision/DoS assumption documented **at the type**: keys are\n    internal interned u64s from the `$registry` stream; an attacker picks\n    *names*, never *ids*; explicit ban on reusing foldhash for any\n    string/byte-keyed map in the crate; note that `fast::RandomState` is not\n    HashDoS-resistant.\n  - both constructors (`from_bytes`, `from_pack`) use\n    `with_capacity_and_hasher`.\n  - new test `directory_products_are_hasher_independent`: 16 independently\n    seeded opens x both directory codecs (dense stride 1 -> BITRANK, sparse\n    stride 1013 -> SORTED) x both readers, asserting present lookups exact,\n    absent lookups `None` (below/above/adjacent probes), `stream_ids()`\n    strictly ascending and identical, `global_entries()` identical, and\n    re-serialization byte-identical.\n\nHasher-independence audit: `dir` is only ever `.get()`/indexed\n(stream_range / stream_head / resolve / stream_entries / global_entries). It\nis never iterated. Every ordered product walks the ascending `stream_ids`\nVec; `seal_sidecar` and `pack::encode_pack` build from the ascending\n`SealStream` input. No latent order dependency found — nothing to paper over.\n\nCargo.lock delta: **one line** — `foldhash` added to mess-index's dependency\nlist. foldhash 0.2.0 was already in the tree via `quick_cache`, so zero new\npackages.\n\n### Evidence\n\n- `cargo nextest run -p mess-index -p mess-store`: 384 passed, 10 skipped.\n- `cargo check --workspace --all-targets`: clean.\n- `just fmt-check`: clean.\n- `mess-bench run --mode full`: **PASS, all 14 floor-gated metrics** within\n  tolerance (load 4.89 at start, 3.03 at end; scratch\n  `~/.cache/mess-bench-bn-dcr` so as not to collide with siblings).\n  sealed.global_scan 1.96e9 ev/s, sealed.stream_replay 1.46e9 ev/s,\n  engine.sealed_replay 5.82e6 ev/s.\n- Commit e4009d8e in workspace bn-dcr. Review cr-21ujmm requested\n  (mess-reviewer) — not self-approved. No push, no merge.\n\n### Follow-up worth a bone (not done here)\n\n`crates/mess-index/src/active.rs:199` `streams: RwLock<HashMap<u64, Vec<StreamEntry>>>`\nis the same shape — u64 interned stream ids, SipHash, hot lookup path — and is\nsharded, so it is touched on every active-index read. Same argument applies;\ndifferent territory (sibling workers active nearby), so left alone."}	blake3:uSraHHOw6nIafzuqtKtdDJ6KPP2BHiaPM1YcoVsE7xU
1785600082686655	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx4RE		item.move	bn-dcr	{"state":"done"}	blake3:cnhd9VuYXtZJ-lKbwjcpRUC5d3Mgzl587ZWsY3ubfVQ
1785600098551330	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx4hE		item.comment	bn-dcr	{"body":"Merged to trunk (cr-21ujmm approved). Framing correction recorded: DIRCODEC_BITRANK is serialization-only; the converted HashMap is the in-memory directory for every sealed segment. Follow-up filed for the active.rs streams map with a composed-engine-visibility gate (bn-3hm4 measured ActiveIndex at 0.5% of engine time — the microbench win must survive that filter)."}	blake3:POOpoiVIviYFikjg0m4w8W3Rdd_5jO8xzh6E1bcRh3M
1785600106491296	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx4xE		item.create	bn-3odg	{"description":"bn-dcr's worker flagged crates/mess-index/src/active.rs:199 — streams: RwLock<HashMap<u64, Vec<StreamEntry>>> — as the identical shape to the sealed DirMap it converted: interned u64 keys, SipHash, hot lookup on every active-index read. Same measure-first design as bn-dcr: reproduce a >=20% lookup win in the active index's REAL access pattern (per-batch apply_committed inserts + read-path probes under the Book RwLock — note bn-3hm4 measured ActiveIndex at 0.5% of engine time, so even a large map-level win may be invisible end-to-end; the premise check should measure BOTH the microbench and whether reader_contention/mess-bench moves at all). Adopt only if the composed engine can see it; otherwise DECLINE with the table, same as bn-2ig9's precedent. The DoS argument transfers verbatim (interned ids, attacker picks names).","kind":"task","title":"Measure foldhash for the active-index streams map (same shape as bn-dcr, more traffic)"}	blake3:q7x1TuAF5JDx3_wuRDJBi2qYwENy6pWMmE0LFitO30M
1785600592643389	mess-reseal	itc:v3:AWKZyTkzOZJJ5JEAAAAAQbdXW3aq9UAAAAEzAw		item.comment	bn-30u	{"body":"Implementation complete — workspace bn-30u, commit cc93da00, review cr-jn03mj (requested @mess-review, NOT self-approved). Part 1/2: design.\n\n## Lifecycle\n\nClassification runs once per open (load_sealed, then recover):\n\n  ABSENT --(seal writes durably: temp->fsync->rename->dir fsync)--> CANDIDATE\n  CANDIDATE --parses? no--> REFUTED(unparsable)                       [durable: quarantine rename]\n  CANDIDATE --footer cross-checks? yes--> ADMITTED                    [durable: the footer fsync]\n  CANDIDATE --footer cross-checks? no--> PENDING                      [in-memory only]\n  PENDING --base_pos mismatch--> REFUTED(identity-mismatch)           [durable: quarantine rename]\n  PENDING --scan next_pos < coverage end--> REFUTED(coverage-unproven)[durable: quarantine rename]\n  PENDING --segment never scanned--> REFUTED(orphan)                  [durable: quarantine rename]\n  PENDING --scan reaches coverage--> CONFIRMED                        [in-memory install; still owed a footer]\n  REFUTED --> ABSENT + a durable *.refuted marker\n  ABSENT+marker, not footer-verified, not the head --> re-seal enqueued --> CANDIDATE ...\n\nThe ONLY durable state changes are (a) the quarantine rename + directory fsync and (b) the sealer's own sidecar write and footer fsync. ADMITTED/CONFIRMED/PENDING are in-memory classifications recomputed from disk every open.\n\n## Quarantine vs delete: quarantine, bounded by naming\n\nRename seg-<id>.pidx -> seg-<id>.pidx.refuted.\n- A refuted candidate is the only witness to whichever crash window or sealer bug produced it. 'The sealer wrote an unadmittable sidecar' is exactly the bug class you cannot debug without the bytes.\n- The suffix makes Path::extension() 'refuted', so every existing structural name parse (engine load_sealed, CLI discover_sidecars) skips it for free — the same mechanism that already hides *.pidx.tmp husks. No new is-this-quarantined predicate anywhere.\n- Unbounded growth does not apply: the name is DERIVED, not unique. One slot per (segment, artifact kind); a second refutation renames over the first (POSIX rename replaces atomically). On-disk cost is O(segments) — the same order as the sidecars themselves — and cannot grow with reopens, which was the growth that mattered. No reaper, no policy knob.\n- Family: a refuted .pidx takes its derived .filter/.pcol/.reg (built from the very index being discarded; a stale .filter surviving into a later re-seal is cross-checked only by segment_id and could wrongly exclude a stream). .par stays — it is parity over the .log. A .seal pack is self-contained so its family is itself, which also stops a refuted pack dragging away a good .pidx's siblings mid-migration.\n- Rename order is siblings-first, primary-last, so a crash midway leaves the candidate in place and the next open re-refutes and finishes the move.\n\n## Re-enqueue rides the existing scheduler\n\nopen_with clones the committer's mpsc::Sender<SegmentSummary> before it goes into Roller and pushes owed segments onto the SAME background roll-sealer thread. No second scheduler and no second failure semantics: that thread already re-reads the durable prefix, writes the sidecars, finalizes the footer, installs, and evicts. The summary comes off the recovery scan (epoch/base_pos from the header, end_pos = next_pos, batch_count = accepted.len(), content_len = safe_offset). Its readiness gate (applied_end >= end && published >= end) is already satisfied at open because recovery seeded both.\n\nTrigger = durable *.refuted marker AND not footer-verified-admitted AND not the live head. Keyed on the marker rather than on 'this open refuted something' deliberately: the quarantine erases the candidate, so a refutation-keyed rule would strand the segment forever if the process died between the rename and the enqueue. The head is excluded because the sealer's finalize step writes a footer and the head is still being appended to; a refuted head candidate is still quarantined and the next roll seals it normally.\n\n## Crash convergence\n\n  before the quarantine            -> same candidate; refute + quarantine again (identical work, no oscillation)\n  midway through the quarantine    -> primary still a candidate; re-refute, rename the rest (NotFound skipped)\n  after quarantine, before enqueue -> marker, no candidate; enqueued off the marker\n  after sidecar, before the footer -> footerless candidate the scan CONFIRMS (served cold) + marker still present\n                                      and still not footer-verified -> enqueued once more, the footer lands\n  after the footer                 -> admitted; the marker is inert forever\n\nEach open makes progress or is a no-op; none oscillates. The one non-converging state left is a quarantine rename that FAILS (read-only/full fs): the candidate is re-evaluated next open — correct but unconverged — and that is counted and logged as such.\n\n## Observability (matches the seals_skipped shape)\n\nEngineMetrics gains sealed_candidates_refuted, sealed_candidates_quarantined, sealed_candidate_quarantine_failures, sealed_reseals_enqueued. LogEngine::sealed_candidate_health() carries per-candidate {segment_id, reason, quarantined}, the pending-reseal segment ids, and last_refutation() as a reason string. One loud '!!! mess SEALED CANDIDATE REFUTED: segment N: reason' line per refutation, deliberately not rate-limited: the quarantine is what bounds the volume (at most once per candidate, ever)."}	blake3:GOyAoqa6HU3wcVlE9POF9M8CSoATo3p7Dyi5i2d1mbU
1785600622393854	mess-reseal	itc:v3:AWKZyTkzOZJJ5JEAAAAAQbdXW3aq9UAAAAEzBA		item.comment	bn-30u	{"body":"bn-30u part 2/2: evidence, gates, territory, deferred.\n\n## Tests (all green)\n\nUnit — crates/mess-store/src/sealed_candidate.rs, 10:\nquarantine name leaves the candidate namespace for both primary extensions; .seal/.pidx slots never collide; family contents and rename order; .seal family self-contained; family move then repeat is a no-op; resumes after a partial family move (interrupted cleanup); 5 rounds of refutation occupy exactly ONE slot and the newest bytes win; structural segment-id name parse; health summarisation; stable reason tokens.\n\nIntegration — crates/mess-store/tests/sealed_candidate_lifecycle.rs, 12, against a real rolled multi-segment store:\n- a_corrupt_candidate_is_refuted_quarantined_and_resealed  (end to end: refuted -> quarantined -> re-sealed -> served from the sealed path)\n- a_candidate_claiming_undurable_coverage_is_refuted\n- an_identity_mismatched_candidate_is_refuted\n- an_orphan_candidate_is_refuted_and_queues_no_reseal\n- a_crash_between_quarantine_and_reseal_still_converges          (kill point)\n- a_crash_before_the_footer_converges_to_a_footer_verified_segment (kill point)\n- a_crash_midway_through_the_quarantine_finishes_it              (kill point)\n- repeated_reopen_judges_a_candidate_once_and_then_converges     (5 reopens: refuted [1,0,0,0,0], enqueued [1,0,0,0,0], quarantine slot count constant and > 0)\n- several_refuted_candidates_in_one_open_all_converge\n- a_refuted_head_candidate_is_quarantined_but_never_footer_finalized\n- a_corrupt_seal_pack_candidate_takes_the_same_lifecycle\n- a_healthy_store_refutes_nothing_and_enqueues_nothing\n\nEvery one of the 12 re-asserts the full read surface (global + per-stream + head) byte-exact against a pre-damage baseline — the whole justification for discarding a candidate is that the raw log is authority, so each test re-proves it rather than assuming it.\n\nMutation checks, to show the suite is not vacuous: quarantine stubbed out -> 5 integration tests fail; the enqueue stubbed out -> 8 fail (the 3 that still pass are precisely the three that correctly expect NO enqueue: orphan, healthy store, head candidate).\n\nFlakiness: the new suite was run 4x plus once with -j 8; 12/12 every time. The background re-seal is awaited with a bounded poll (10 s cap, normally satisfied on the first poll because the sealer's readiness gate is already met at open).\n\n## Gates\n\n- cargo nextest run -p mess-index -p mess-store: 405 passed, 10 skipped (393 before this bone; +12 integration — the 10 unit tests were already in the 393 count).\n- cargo check --workspace --all-targets: clean.\n- just fmt-check: clean.\n- cargo clippy -p mess-store --all-targets: no new warnings (the ones shown are pre-existing in mess-index pack.rs/driver.rs and engine.rs type_complexity).\n- cargo nextest run -p mess-cli: 40 passed. Run on purpose because a *.refuted file now lands in sealed/; discover_sidecars is extension-keyed so it skips them exactly like *.tmp, and verify's orphan-sidecar check is unaffected.\n- cargo doc -p mess-store --no-deps: no new warnings from the added module or items.\n- NOT run per instruction (lead gates): full just test, mess-bench.\n\nBench-floor risk: I do not believe this moves any floor. A healthy store has no *.refuted markers, so the enqueue set is empty and open does strictly one extra HashSet lookup per SCANNED segment plus one Vec<SegmentSummary> push — and a healthy store's rolled segments are trust-skipped, so they are not scanned at all. No new I/O on the healthy path; no change to the append, read, or seal hot paths. The a_healthy_store_refutes_nothing_and_enqueues_nothing test pins that claim.\n\n## Territory\n\nZERO changes under crates/mess-index — no contact with bn-dbz (SealPack open/read) or bn-dcr (sparse directory chooser). Everything is in mess-store: the new sealed_candidate module, engine.rs classification + scheduling, lib.rs re-exports. Files touched: crates/mess-store/src/sealed_candidate.rs (new), crates/mess-store/tests/sealed_candidate_lifecycle.rs (new), crates/mess-store/src/engine.rs, crates/mess-store/src/lib.rs.\n\n## Deferred (noted, not done)\n\n1. *.pidx.tmp / *.seal.tmp husks are still never cleaned up. They do not have this bug — they are already outside the candidate namespace and never parsed — so removing them is disk hygiene, not correctness.\n2. No mess-cli doctor finding for quarantined artifacts. The bone allows metrics OR doctor; EngineMetrics is documented in-tree as the authoritative surface and already carries the analogous seals_skipped counter. A doctor finding needs SegmentFile.has_quarantine plumbed through store.rs/doctor.rs/inspect.rs — a small follow-up if the lead wants the offline surface too. Note doctor already emits a Warn 'sidecar-missing' for a segment whose .pidx was quarantined, which is correct if terse.\n3. A rolled segment that lost its sidecar with NO marker (an operator deleting sealed/ by hand) is still not re-sealed. Pre-existing behavior, preserved deliberately: engine_roll::crash_mid_roll_seal_is_served_from_log_after_reopen asserts exactly that invariant ('no sidecars => nothing served cold'), and widening the trigger to 'any unsealed rolled segment' would both make that assertion racy and give this change a real blast radius on bench stores. If the lead wants automatic repair of hand-deleted sidecars it should be its own bone with its own floor evidence.\n4. A store mid-format-migration holding BOTH a .seal and a .pidx for one segment: if the .seal is refuted, the .pidx is only classified on the NEXT open (the pack short-circuits it at load). That is two-open convergence, not a loop, and it is documented at the short-circuit site."}	blake3:AJ2_ZDIDmy4EPvZfcgx0EKdaf2iqDdeEjX3BEs-1L1A
1785600798403162	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx5BE		item.move	bn-30u	{"state":"done"}	blake3:2eyWbW0whsvcxC2tGkI6eoETUvfgIKUzj1rBzQ7A_6o
1785601152838251	mess-pack-rss	itc:v3:AWKck8knJOZM5kQAAAAAQbq9V1drdoAAAAEyAw		item.comment	bn-dbz	{"body":"bn-dbz done: workspace bn-dbz, commit f22cb7e5, review cr-2bf96m (mess-dev requested, NOT self-approved). Bone stays `doing`, claims held.\n\nWHAT CHANGED. `SealedSegmentIndex::open_pack` no longer `fs::read`s the whole `.seal`. It preads header(64) + trailer(40) + directory(n_sections*48), verifies the trailer blake3 over header+directory, then reads ONLY the sections sized by STREAM COUNT — the three mandatory ones (STREAM_DIRECTORY, POINTER_BLOCKS, POINTER_SKIPS) and STREAM_FILTER — each checked against its directory-committed crc32c + content_hash_prefix. Retained bytes are POINTER_BLOCKS ++ POINTER_SKIPS with the directory offsets rebased onto that concatenation, so resolve/stream_entries/global_entries are untouched. STATS is never read (informational, no reader consults it). Sections sized by segment CONTENT stay on disk behind ONE retained handle: PAYLOAD_COLUMNS via new `SealedPayloadIndex::attach_at(Arc<File>, base, len)` (bn-bka2's machinery, now offset-relative so an embedded image reads exactly like a standalone .pcol), EVENT_TYPE_IDS via a new file-backed `EventTypeColumn` (dictionary + CRC table resident, index blocks pread on touch). fd cost is 1/segment and only when a lazy section exists — parity with the .pcol path since bn-bka2, not new.\n\nFORMAT CHANGE (flagged, old packs readable). EVENT_TYPE_IDS bodies now carry a trailing per-block crc32c table, announced by ETFLAG_BLOCK_CRC (0x01) in the header's formerly-reserved byte 9, with events-per-block in the formerly-reserved u16 at byte 10. crcs[0] covers the prologue (header+dict, verified once at attach); crcs[1+b] covers index block b, verified before its bytes are used. The section's whole-body CRC covers exactly what you would have to read to check it, which is the cost being removed — same reasoning as bn-bka2's FLAG_SPLIT_CRC. LEGACY RULING (same as bka2/cr-377lcv): a body with flags==0 has no per-block checksums, so it attaches EAGERLY, whole-body, section-CRC verified — byte-for-byte what open does today. Old packs lose no check. FORWARD compat is real too: the header layout is unchanged and the table is appended after the index region, so a pre-bn-dbz `decode_event_types` decodes a new body correctly and ignores the trailing bytes — VERIFIED by reopening a bn-dbz-written 2M corpus with the trunk binary (identical total_events, identical probe results).\n\nBLOCK SIZE, justified: ET_BLOCK_SHIFT=10 (1024 events, 1 KiB of index at width 1). Measured on the new `pack_read_bench` (1M-event pack): random point read p50 741ns at 4096/block, 310ns at 1024, 201ns at 256 — the crc32c dominates until ~1 KiB, then the pread syscall floor (~200ns) caps it — while the resident CRC table grows 4x per step down. At 1024 the table is 4 B/1024 events (1.5 MB at 385M) against the 4 B/event (1.5 GB) of decoded column it replaces.\n\nRSS, ABBA (trunk,dbz,dbz,trunk), 1000 streams / 100-ev batches / 200 B payloads, production 256 MiB segments, seal_pack=true, warm cache, load 2.7-4.8, 2.4T free. Per-rep post-open VmRSS delta / process VmHWM:\n\n  2M events (1 sealed pack, 59.9 MiB)\n    trunk  +128.3 / +124.5 / +121.3 MB   peak 313 / 364 / 368 MB   open 148/145/140 ms\n    dbz     +2.6  /  +0.04 /   +0.0 MB   peak 190 / 241 / 241 MB   open  96/ 89/ 94 ms\n    trunk  +128.5 / +124.8 / +121.3 MB   peak 314 / 367 / 370 MB   open 147/142/142 ms\n    dbz     +2.7  /  +0.03 /   +0.0 MB   peak 189 / 242 / 244 MB   open  97/ 89/ 91 ms\n  10M events (8 sealed packs, 478.8 MiB)\n    trunk  +1012 / +1004 / +1007 MB      peak 1151/1201/1201 MB    open 460/461/452 ms\n    dbz      +6.5 /  +0.07 /  +0.0 MB    peak  146/ 195/ 195 MB    open  66/ 65/ 68 ms\n    dbz      +6.6 /  +0.02 /  +0.0 MB    peak  147/ 195/ 195 MB    open  64/ 68/ 66 ms\n    trunk  +1012 / +1005 / +1007 MB      peak 1149/1199/1199 MB    open 463/437/410 ms\n\n  10M: 155x less post-open residency, 6.1x lower peak, 6.6x faster open. The residency that remains is the mandatory pointer sections (what the legacy .pidx costs too) plus the ET dict + CRC table, i.e. it now tracks stream count, not sealed bytes. A trunk-WRITTEN 2M corpus read by the dbz binary gives +3.7 MB (vs +2.6 new-format): the payload columns are still lazy, only EVENT_TYPE_IDS falls back to the eager legacy body — the expected and bounded legacy penalty."}	blake3:dGEOPp3Doykhi7laq7UWEookN3XSp51CJEVJZGAiJdw
1785601152865537	mess-pack-rss	itc:v3:AWKck8knJOZM5kQAAAAAQbq9V1drdoAAAAEyBA		item.comment	bn-dbz	{"body":"bn-dbz evidence (2/2), commit f22cb7e5, review cr-2bf96m.\n\nREAD LATENCY (the AC's \"no unacceptable regression\" concern). Two instruments, since mess-bench cannot cover this — SAYING SO PLAINLY: the floors run with DEFAULT EngineOptions and its engine workloads use 2 GiB segments, so nothing seals and no `.seal` is ever produced; the sealed.* workloads drive mess-index directly on loose sidecars. Floors guard the loose paths I did not touch; they do not test packs.\n (1) Engine level, in the ABBA above: 2001 `read_global` probes spread across the whole global sequence, 24.3-28.7 us/probe (2M) and 31.3-40.0 us/probe (10M) in EVERY cell, trunk and dbz interleaved — indistinguishable.\n (2) Index level, `pack_read_bench` (new, #[ignore]d, 1M-event pack, 20k random probes), eager (== trunk residency) vs lazy:\n      resolve         p50 150ns -> 150ns   p99 370ns -> 490ns     (mandatory sections stay resident)\n      payload point   p50 5.50us -> 5.80us p99 8.4us -> 8.7us     (already lazy in trunk via .pcol)\n      event_type_id   p50  30ns -> 310ns   p99 120ns -> 531ns     (the one real cost: 1 pread + 1 KiB crc32c)\n      type range x10  p50  30ns -> 310ns                          (10 events for the price of one)\n    The only caller, engine `decode_capsule`, now asks for the batch's whole frame run at once through the new `event_type_ids_range` instead of one call per frame, so it pays ~310ns per BATCH, next to a payload reassembly that is ~5.8us per event. Against not having the section at all (raw-batch decode), 310ns is still a large win.\n (3) INFORMATIONAL, seal_pack forced ON in the two engine bench workloads (64 MiB segments; patch NOT committed, reverted), ABBA: engine.sealed_replay 7.426/7.424 (trunk) vs 7.527/7.506 M ev/s (dbz); head_reads_per_s and resolve_per_s within noise. No regression with packs on.\n\nINTEGRITY. Nothing weakened, timing moved: trailer hash still binds header+directory (so every section checksum a lazy read leans on is itself covered); torn mandatory section still rejects the whole pack -> raw scan; torn optional section still dropped, just at the read that touches it, as the typed None/Corrupt every caller already degrades on (D1/I5). `open_pack_eager` retains the whole-image, every-section-verified read for offline verification / differential tests.\n\nTESTS (11 new, all green). pack.rs: legacy body attaches eagerly + reads exact; torn legacy body dropped AT ATTACH; lazy column == eager column across CRC-block boundaries (points, 6 ranges incl. straddling and short tail, out-of-range); torn index block surfaces at read and stays local (blocks 0 and 2 exact, block 1 refuses, a range touching it refuses); torn prologue caught at attach; header/section-length disagreement refused. segment.rs: lazy vs eager pack parity on every query (resolve/head/range/entries/global/filter/type-ids/payloads) + laziness observable `sections_resident()`; THE DIRECT LAZINESS PROOF — a pack with BOTH optional bodies torn still opens with both sections attached and resolves every pointer identically to a clean pack, while `open_pack_eager` on the same file drops both at open; corrupt mandatory section (all three kinds) rejects; header/directory flips and tail truncation reject; concurrent readers of one Arc'd lazy pack.\n\nGATES. mess-index 162/162, mess-store 232/232 (incl. the engine_seal_pack suite 4/4), `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean, `just fmt-check` clean, full `mess-bench run --mode full --floors crates/mess-bench/floors.json` PASS 14/14. Did NOT run full `just test` (lead gates the merge). No stray processes; corpora and the temporary trunk worktree/driver deleted; the measurement driver (`examples/packrss.rs`) is NOT in the commit.\n\nTOUCHED: mess-index/src/sealed/{pack,payload,segment}.rs and ONE line of mess-store/src/engine.rs `decode_capsule` (point loop -> range call). Did NOT touch driver.rs refutation/lifecycle (bn-30u) or the directory chooser (bn-dcr).\n\nDEFERRED / FOR THE LEAD:\n1. SEAL-PATH residency is unchanged and is the remaining whole-pack copy: `seal_consolidated` installs `from_pack(bytes.clone())`, so a segment sealed IN THIS PROCESS keeps its whole pack resident until eviction. That is parity with the loose path (which likewise keeps the whole `.pcol` resident via `from_bytes` after seal), it is not the reopen path this bone gates, and the fix would touch driver.rs where bn-30u is working — so I left it. It IS a ~3-line change (`open_pack(&path).unwrap_or(index)` after the durable write) and would make a long-running writer bounded too; worth a follow-up bone before bn-ccx1 flips the default on write-heavy stores. My change already cuts that path 4x (the eager ET column is now the 1 B/event body, not a 4 B/event decoded Vec).\n2. `archive_reblock` upgrades old `.pcol`s but there is no equivalent rewriter for a pre-bn-dbz `.seal` (its ET section stays eagerly attached forever). Bounded at 1 B/event and self-healing on re-seal; a `.seal` re-writer would be the general fix if Phase 8 wants it.\n3. bn-26pp's REGISTRY_DELTA note on bn-35fy is still open and is NOT mine — packs still emit no reserved REGISTRY_DELTA section, so a pack-sealed store keeps the O(#names) pread path at open."}	blake3:bBZX7kRkwGiKeanZZazVRPLh2sDqpkl_7LkE6BhRWlw
1785601572422757	mess-pack-rss	itc:v3:AWKck8knJOZM5kQAAAAAQbq9V1drdoAAAAEyBQ		item.comment	bn-dbz	{"body":"bn-dbz update: rebased onto the current epoch fe13ac86 (bn-dcr foldhash + bn-30u candidate lifecycle both landed while I worked) and RE-MEASURED there. Review re-anchored: cr-2bf96m ABANDONED (0 threads, never opened), new review cr-319tc5 @mess-dev, anchored a0bb9602. Workspace head 5849407d; commits 37a05688 (the change) + f927d0ef/5849407d (review records) + a0bb9602 (rebase fix).\n\nREBASE was textually clean but did NOT compile: bn-dcr changed `SealedSegmentIndex::dir` to `DirMap = HashMap<u64, DirEntry, foldhash::fast::RandomState>` and bn-dbz adds a THIRD construction site (`attach_pack`). Fixed in a0bb9602 — `attach_pack` now builds the same `DirMap::with_capacity_and_hasher` the two existing sites do. Worth flagging to whoever runs the post_sync hook: this is exactly the case the hook doc describes (clean replay, broken compile).\n\nCONFIRMATION ABBA at 10M events on the CURRENT epoch (trunk = fe13ac86, dbz = 5849407d), 1000 streams / 100-ev batches / 200 B, seal_pack=true, 8 sealed packs (478.8 MiB), load 8.7-9.3 (siblings busy), 2.4T free:\n\n  trunk  REP0 +1012.4 MB / peak 1150 MB / open 434 ms | REP1 +1005.4 MB / peak 1199 MB / open 423 ms\n  dbz    REP0    +6.6 MB / peak  146 MB / open  64 ms | REP1    +0.01 MB / peak  197 MB / open  66 ms\n  dbz    REP0    +6.6 MB / peak  146 MB / open  74 ms | REP1    +0.03 MB / peak  196 MB / open  71 ms\n  trunk  REP0 +1012.3 MB / peak 1149 MB / open 470 ms | REP1 +1004.2 MB / peak 1195 MB / open 462 ms\n\nSame as the pre-rebase numbers: 153x less post-open residency, 7.9x lower peak, 6.6x faster open. Read probes 34.0-40.3 us/probe in EVERY cell (trunk and dbz interleaved) — no latency shift from either sibling's changes or mine.\n\nGATES RE-RUN post-rebase: mess-index + mess-store 417/417 (up from 394 — bn-30u's new suites included), mess-cli 40/40, `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` clean, `just fmt-check` clean, full mess-bench floors 14/14 PASS. Corpora, temporary trunk worktree, and the measurement driver all deleted; no stray processes; the driver is NOT in any commit.\n\nBone stays `doing`, claims held, not self-approved, not pushed, not merged."}	blake3:h4bTZIjrA9RSz-ePx-gBxOHJE5xygXpuYXpKpV9zrQI
1785601746810837	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx5RE		item.move	bn-dbz	{"state":"done"}	blake3:dJP66BQn9LLweH2o7O_0M27VMQtYqy4_fpzo1lakLS8
1785601756934715	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx5hE		item.create	bn-2t2e	{"description":"Deferred from bn-dbz (its reopen fix deliberately avoided bn-30u's driver.rs territory): seal_consolidated installs from_pack(bytes.clone()), so a segment sealed in-process keeps its whole pack resident until reopen — parity with the legacy loose path, but now strictly worse than the lazy reopen path bn-dbz shipped. ~3-line change per the bn-dbz worker: install via the lazy attach (SealedSegmentIndex::attach_pack / open_pack path) instead of the eager from_pack. Matters for long-running write-heavy stores; should land before bn-ccx1 flips seal_pack default. Guard: sealed read tests + the bn-dbz laziness proofs already cover the attach path; add one test that a just-sealed in-process segment does not retain pack-sized bytes.","kind":"task","title":"Seal-path pack residency: seal_consolidated keeps the just-sealed pack resident in-process"}	blake3:picdRnknpgM4CSGNjsbvvxvygu_WGuCmp_KEIXvYB-Q
