1785603637420672	mess-chooser	itc:v3:AWLJJ58ySZknPQAAAAAAQdV77VbV3gAAAAEvAw		item.comment	bn-we9x	{"body":"## bn-we9x findings — chooser validated, found suboptimal, fixed + instrumented\n\nWorkspace `bn-we9x`, commit `08a18c0c`, review `cr-2m08m5` (requested from mess-dev, NOT self-approved).\n\n### 1. The chooser as found\n\n`crates/mess-index/src/sealed/pack.rs::should_use_bitrank`, one call site\n(`encode_stream_directory` <- `encode_pack`). Inputs: only `(n, min, max)` of the\nascending `DirEntryRaw` slice. Rule was:\n\n    n >= 8 && U <= 8n     (U = max - min + 1)  ->  BITRANK, else SORTED\n\nThat is Spike H's **lookup** crossover. bn-dcr established the codec is\nserialization-only (both codecs rehydrate into the identical foldhash DirMap), so\nlookup speed cannot be the criterion. design.md 12.6 prescribes a **byte-cost\ncomparison**; the implementation never was one.\n\nOn-disk costs: sorted = 56n. bitrank = 16 + 8*ceil(U/64) + 48n. Bitrank drops the\n8 B stream_id per record and spends it on the bitvector, so it is smaller exactly\nwhen `16 + 8*ceil(U/64) < 8n`, i.e. **U <= 64*(n-3)** — a break-even at U/n ~ 64,\neight times looser than the shipped rule.\n\n### 2. Validation on the 39 real engine-written segment directories\n\n`~/.cache/mess-bench-scratch/directory_tournament_store{,_big}` (the bn-dcr / Spike H\ncorpus). Directory bytes recomputed for both codecs from each segment's real\nstream-id set; decode timed with an ABBA-ordered paired harness (median of 41,\nload1 3.8, serial).\n\n    store   seg      n        U    U/n  sorted_B bitrank_B     old      new opt?  sorted_us bitrank_us  b/s\n    dts       1   2022     2022   1.00    113232     97328 BITRANK  BITRANK yes        3.75       4.26 1.14\n    dts       2   2070     3272   1.58    115920     99792 BITRANK  BITRANK yes        3.84       4.39 1.14\n    dts       3   2017     4143   2.05    112952     97352 BITRANK  BITRANK yes        3.74       4.31 1.15\n    dts       4   2007     4825   2.40    112392     96960 BITRANK  BITRANK yes        3.73       4.30 1.15\n    dts       5   2011     5441   2.71    112616     97232 BITRANK  BITRANK yes        3.48       4.14 1.19\n    dts       6   2006     5991   2.99    112336     97056 BITRANK  BITRANK yes        3.48       4.34 1.25\n    dts       7   2070     6488   3.13    115920    100192 BITRANK  BITRANK yes        3.59       4.40 1.22\n    dts       8   1976     6880   3.48    110656     95728 BITRANK  BITRANK yes        3.44       4.06 1.18\n    dts       9   2015     7306   3.63    112840     97656 BITRANK  BITRANK yes        3.51       4.14 1.18\n    dts      10   2046     7644   3.74    114576     99184 BITRANK  BITRANK yes        3.62       4.24 1.17\n    dts      11   2046     7966   3.89    114576     99224 BITRANK  BITRANK yes        3.62       4.25 1.17\n    dts      12   2035     8261   4.06    113960     98736 BITRANK  BITRANK yes        7.59*      8.93* 1.18\n    dts      13   2008     8515   4.24    112448     97472 BITRANK  BITRANK yes        3.60       4.22 1.17\n    dts      14   2033     8731   4.29    113848     98696 BITRANK  BITRANK yes        3.64       4.27 1.17\n    dts      15   1900     8940   4.71    106400     92336 BITRANK  BITRANK yes        3.27       3.88 1.18\n    dts      16   1659     9139   5.51     92904     80792 BITRANK  BITRANK yes        2.87       3.40 1.18\n    dts      17   1628     9327   5.73     91168     79328 BITRANK  BITRANK yes        2.81       3.35 1.19\n    dts      18   1254     9491   7.57     70224     61400 BITRANK  BITRANK yes        2.18       2.62 1.20\n    dts      19    994     9628   9.69     55664     48936  SORTED  BITRANK  NO        1.74       2.15 1.24\n    dts      20   1048     9741   9.29     58688     51544  SORTED  BITRANK  NO        1.83       2.25 1.23\n    dts      21   1045     9851   9.43     58520     51408  SORTED  BITRANK  NO        1.82       2.24 1.23\n    dts      22   1021     9951   9.75     57176     50272  SORTED  BITRANK  NO        1.78       2.20 1.24\n    dts      23    794    10046  12.65     44464     39384  SORTED  BITRANK  NO        1.41       1.78 1.26\n    dts      24    529    10104  19.10     29624     26672  SORTED  BITRANK  NO        0.95       1.26 1.33\n    dts      25    554    10162  18.34     31024     27880  SORTED  BITRANK  NO        0.99       1.31 1.32\n    dts      26    109    10169  93.29      6104      6520  SORTED   SORTED yes        0.21       0.44 2.09\n    dts_big   1   7839     7839   1.00    438984    377272 BITRANK  BITRANK yes       13.97      15.87 1.14\n    dts_big   2   7947    12369   1.56    445032    383024 BITRANK  BITRANK yes       14.12      16.12 1.14\n    dts_big   3   8056    15973   1.98    451136    388704 BITRANK  BITRANK yes       14.28      16.50 1.16\n    dts_big   4   7983    18733   2.35    447048    385544 BITRANK  BITRANK yes       14.11      16.35 1.16\n    dts_big   5   7877    21010   2.67    441112    380744 BITRANK  BITRANK yes       13.75      16.08 1.17\n    dts_big   6   8003    22991   2.87    448168    387040 BITRANK  BITRANK yes       14.13      16.46 1.16\n    dts_big   7   7976    24602   3.08    446656    385944 BITRANK  BITRANK yes       14.09      16.42 1.16\n    dts_big   8   7963    26019   3.27    445928    385496 BITRANK  BITRANK yes       14.00      16.48 1.18\n    dts_big   9   7839    27341   3.49    438984    379712 BITRANK  BITRANK yes       13.75      17.00 1.24\n    dts_big  10   6927    28468   4.11    387912    336072 BITRANK  BITRANK yes       12.12      14.33 1.18\n    dts_big  11   5281    29348   5.56    295736    257176 BITRANK  BITRANK yes        9.25      11.06 1.19\n    dts_big  12   4217    30088   7.13    236152    206200 BITRANK  BITRANK yes        7.37       9.32 1.26\n    dts_big  13   1704    30344  17.81     95424     85608  SORTED  BITRANK  NO        3.00       4.13 1.38\n\n    (* dts seg 12 caught an ambient scheduling blip; the RATIO, which is what the\n       ABBA pairing protects, is in line with its neighbours.)\n\n    old rule (U<=8n):   6,376,080 directory bytes, 8/39 segments SUBOPTIMAL\n    byte-optimal rule:  6,327,200 directory bytes, 39/39 optimal\n    excess removed:        48,880 B (0.77% of directory bytes; 6-14% on the 8\n                                     affected segments; 4.7% of dts seg 20's\n                                     whole sealed artifact)\n    decode cost:         273.2 us -> 277.0 us TOTAL across all 39 segments (+3.8 us)\n\n**Verdict: the chooser was safe but not optimal.** It never picked bitrank when\nbitrank was larger (the 8n cap sits well inside the 64(n-3) break-even), so\nnothing shipped was wrong — it just left the whole band 8 < U/n <= 64 on the\ntable, which is where 21% of real segments live. Trading +3.8 us of one-time\ndecode across the corpus for -48.9 KB of disk (and of pread) is a clear win, and\nthe byte rule is the one 12.6 already specifies.\n\nAlso corrected: the old doc claimed `U <= 8n` \"bounds the bitvector at n/8 bytes\".\nIt is n bytes (U bits = 8n bits). The byte rule needs no such claim — the chosen\nimage is by construction never larger than the sorted image."}	blake3:LkzXp9M8JNdTLFyvulDIi6w2ggy5TV_BmdUdcUCLJFQ
1785603637448758	mess-chooser	itc:v3:AWLJJ58ySZknPQAAAAAAQdV77VbV3gAAAAEvBA		item.comment	bn-we9x	{"body":"## bn-we9x findings (2/2) — determinism, exactness, instrumentation, evidence\n\n### 3. Determinism / re-seal byte stability (relevant to bn-11g)\n\nThe chooser is a pure integer function of `(n, min, max)`: no float, no hasher\nseed, no iteration order, no clock, and `<` (not `<=`) so ties are unambiguous\nand go to SORTED. Same directory content => same codec => byte-identical section\n=> same section crc32c + content_hash_prefix => same trailer hash. Pinned by\n`chooser_is_deterministic_and_re_encode_is_byte_identical` and\n`pack_bytes_are_stable_across_threshold_edge_reseals`.\n\nNo pathological flip exists. `chooser_flips_once_at_the_byte_break_even` walks U\none id at a time across +/-130 around `64*(n-3)` for n in {8,64,109,994} and pins\na single monotone step. And because the rule is \"emit the smaller image\", the\nregret at the flip is at most the 8-byte tie — under the old rule, crossing the\nthreshold jumped the section 6-14% larger, a real size discontinuity.\n\n**One-time byte change:** segments in 8 < U/n <= 64 now re-seal to different\nbytes than they would have before. FORMAT_VERSION is unchanged and both codecs\nwere always decodable, so existing packs open untouched; only a fresh seal of\nsuch a segment differs.\n\n### 4. Exactness (AC)\n\n`decode(encode(x)) == x` for BOTH codecs — including the declined one — over\nn in {1,2,3,4,5,7,8,9,16,63,64,65,109,994,2022} x U/n in {1,2,4,8,10,13,19,32,48,61,64,67,93}\nx two id bases, with clustered (seeded splitmix64) ids, plus the empty segment,\nplus every U in a +/-130 window around each break-even. bn-dcr's\nhasher-independence test gains strides 61 and 62 — the two shapes one id of span\napart that straddle the flip — with an added assertion that they really do land\non opposite arms.\n\n### 5. Instrumentation (AC \"visible in engine/doctor metrics\")\n\n- `SealedSegmentIndex::dir_codec() -> u16`, set from the section's `codec_id` by\n  all three readers (`from_bytes` legacy -> SORTED, `from_pack`, `attach_pack`).\n- `mess_index::sealed::dir_codec_of(path)` — answers off disk with no open:\n  header + section directory + trailer only, no section body, pack hash still\n  verified. Accepts `.seal` or legacy `.pidx`.\n- `mess_index::sealed::dircodec_name(u16) -> \"sorted\"|\"bitrank\"|\"unknown\"`.\n- `mess inspect` per-segment row: `\"dir_codec\": \"bitrank\"|\"sorted\"|null`\n  (`.seal` preferred over `.pidx`, matching the engine's dual-read).\n\n### 6. Evidence\n\n- `cargo nextest run -p mess-index -p mess-store` — 424/424 PASS\n- `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` — clean\n- `just fmt-check` — clean\n- floors: **14/14 PASS** (`mess-bench run --mode full`, load1 3.4 -> 2.9). Note the\n  bench workloads seal via `encode_sidecar` / default `EngineOptions`\n  (`seal_pack: false`), so they do **not** exercise the pack directory chooser —\n  run as diligence, not as evidence for this change.\n\n### 7. Observations out of scope (not fixed)\n\n- `EngineOptions::seal_pack` still defaults to `false`, so the chooser is behind\n  an opt-in; this bone did not flip that.\n- mess-cli is `.seal`-blind everywhere except the new `dir_codec` field:\n  `store::discover_segments` only knows `.pidx`, so `doctor` reports\n  \"sealed but no .pidx sidecar\" for a pack-sealed store, and\n  `backup`/`retention`/`verify` do not see `.seal` files. Pre-existing, worth a\n  bone."}	blake3:27536i7C8uBNl_BpoYxUToowYmEqiHVQ8kREOFAr5Cg
1785603827225300	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx5xE		item.move	bn-we9x	{"state":"done"}	blake3:sxfPN9ga08VTPh_hdMghMl3S4cmBAbBI59PETSaBQaI
1785603846085944	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx6BE		item.create	bn-1w4h	{"description":"Found by bn-we9x (pre-existing, out of its scope): store::discover_segments only recognizes .pidx, so on a pack-sealed store  reports 'sealed but no .pidx sidecar' for every segment, and backup/retention/verify do not see .seal files at all. Latent while seal_pack defaults off, load-bearing the moment bn-ccx1 flips it — an operator toolset that misdiagnoses every healthy segment cannot front a default-on storage mode. Scope: teach discover_segments/doctor/verify/backup/retention the .seal extension (and bn-30u's .refuted quarantine names), with the identity/fallback reporting bn-11g adds. BLOCKS bn-ccx1.","kind":"bug","title":"mess-cli is .seal-blind: doctor/backup/retention/verify only know loose .pidx sidecars"}	blake3:eCYH671FGsGkhnXmwLG1bBQY5b35tgyckobYGJxz20M
1785603867894051	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx6RE		item.comment	bn-1w4h	{"body":"Description note (fish ate a backtick during creation): the doctor symptom reads 'mess doctor reports sealed-but-no-.pidx-sidecar for every segment of a pack-sealed store'. Full scope: teach store::discover_segments, doctor, verify, backup, and retention the .seal extension AND bn-30u's .refuted quarantine names, with the identity/fallback reporting bn-11g adds. This bone BLOCKS bn-ccx1 — an operator toolset that misdiagnoses every healthy segment cannot front a default-on storage mode."}	blake3:fc2ZvkKMoe3kqM5Qsg79MWN1yQGgHuIbIFl4JUsW1qg
