1785608469484562	mess-pack-reg	itc:v3:AWLJM5JMkmTMnkxAAAAAQdW6rVa2vWAAAAE0Aw		item.comment	bn-3h64	{"body":"bn-3h64 complete in ws bn-3h64 (2 commits, HEAD 1175e3e0). Review cr-ygzyk4 requested from @mess-dev, NOT self-approved. Bone stays `doing`, claims held.\n\n## 1. Emission site + format decision\n\n`seal_consolidated` (crates/mess-index/src/sealed/driver.rs) now calls the SAME\n`encode_registry_delta(&input, REGISTRY_STREAM_ID)` the loose path calls and hands the bytes to\n`pack::encode_pack` through a new `PackInput::registry_delta: Option<&[u8]>`. The section body IS a\n`.reg` file image, byte for byte — no fork.\n\nWhy reuse rather than a pack-native layout: the body needs its own `crc32c` + magic regardless,\nbecause it is also a standalone file format on the other container, so a \"native\" variant would have\nbought nothing and doubled the corruption-test surface for one accelerator. One encoder\n(`encode_registry_delta`), one parser (`RegistryDelta::from_bytes`), one set of single-byte-flip and\ntruncation tests, and recovery folds the identical records whichever container it found.\n\nPlacement: staged after `STREAM_FILTER`, before `EVENT_TYPE_IDS` and `PAYLOAD_COLUMNS`. The two\ncontent-sized sections are hundreds of MiB; keeping the delta with the stream-count-sized ones puts\nit inside the front-of-file region a cold open already touches, instead of behind a fresh seek.\n\nEmitted only when the segment carries a registration (`encode_registry_delta` returns `None`\notherwise) — the same rule `.reg` follows, and the overwhelmingly common case once a store's names\nare established.\n\nIdentity: the section enters the directory with `crc32c` + `content_hash_prefix` like every other, so\nbn-11g covers it with no new code. Confirmed on a real corpus — `mess verify` on the 8 GiB/250k pack\nstore reports for all 32 packs `seal-pack-verified ... trailer hash and every section checksum` and\n`seal-pack-identity-verified ... footer names SealPack <hash>, matched`.\n\nSeal-time verify: `verify_pack` now requires that a staged section come back through the exact path\nrecovery takes (locate → verify committed checksums → parse → cross-check). An embedding bug is a\nseal failure rather than a silent fallback at every future open — which is precisely how a perf\nsection rots undetected.\n\n## 2. Consumption site + cross-check adaptation\n\n`SealedSegmentIndex` gains `reg_delta: Option<RegDeltaSection>` — **located, not read**: one 48-byte\n`SectionRef` plus, on the lazy path, a clone of the `Arc<File>` the pack's file-backed sections\nalready hold (a delta implies payloads implies `PAYLOAD_COLUMNS`, so this is never a new fd).\n`Image`/`File` variants mirror `EtSource` so the eager `from_pack` and the lazy `open_pack` share one\nreader.\n\n`read_registry_delta()` does the bounded pread (bn-dbz machinery), checks the directory-committed\n`crc32c` + `content_hash_prefix`, parses, and then runs `accepts_registry_delta` — **unchanged**. The\nadaptation is that for a pack the \"pointer sidecar that vouches for the delta\" and the \"container\nthat carries it\" are the same file: `accepts_registry_delta` compares the delta's\n`(first_global_pos, frame_count)` list against `self.stream_entries()`, which for a pack is its\nverified `STREAM_DIRECTORY` + `POINTER_BLOCKS`, under a trailer hash the footer names. So the delta\ncan still only ever supply the *payload bytes* of batches the same artifact already agrees exist at\nthe positions it agrees they occupy — the pack simply plays both roles `.reg` split between a sidecar\nand its sibling.\n\nEngine (`crates/mess-store/src/engine.rs`, sidecar-trusted recover branch): ask the index first, fall\nthrough to `.reg`, fall through to the preads. Each check is applied where it belongs — the stream-id\ncheck in mess-store (REG1's `$registry` id is mess-store's fact, mess-index cannot name it), the\nlayout check once, on the branch that has not already had it. Absent / unreadable / CRC-failing /\nlayout-disagreeing section → the pread path, per segment, unchanged.\n\nNot retained: the delta is moved to the caller, drained into the fold, dropped. Nothing added to\nsteady-state RSS.\n\n## 3. The `.reg`-alongside-a-pack rule\n\n**A pack-sealed segment gets a section and NO sibling `.reg`.** It already worked that way (`seal`\nreturns into `seal_consolidated` before reaching the `.reg` write); what was missing was that the\nsection did not exist, so the pack path had *neither*. Now it has exactly one.\n\nConfirmed nothing else consumes a `.reg` for a pack-sealed segment:\n- `sealed_candidate::PIDX_SIBLING_EXTENSIONS = [\"filter\",\"pcol\",\"reg\"]` — the `.reg` is a `.pidx`\n  family member only, and `seal_pack_family_is_self_contained` asserts a `.seal`'s quarantine family\n  is length 1.\n- `crates/mess-cli/src/store.rs`'s layout map files `.reg` under \"(.pidx family)\".\n- `backup.rs` explicitly leaves `.reg` out of the cut (bn-w5my's to settle).\n- engine recovery reaches `reg_path` only after the section came back `None`.\n\nDocumented at the `.reg` emission site in `SealDriver::seal` (the rule, why \"fewer files is SealPack's\npoint\", and the instruction that a future tool which rebuilds `.reg` for pack stores must be taught\nthe section first), plus in `regdelta.rs`'s module docs (\"One delta per segment, in whichever\ncontainer sealed it\") and the `sealed/mod.rs` module list.\n(continued in the next comment: the measurement, differential evidence, gates, deferred)"}	blake3:OPzouufeO3wYndNbLbDwxzXXFLPbOFYxTEIrulzhuvU
1785608469504236	mess-pack-reg	itc:v3:AWLJM5JMkmTMnkxAAAAAQdW6rVa2vWAAAAE0BA		item.comment	bn-3h64	{"body":"bn-3h64 evidence, part 2/2 (continues the previous comment).\n\n## 4. The measurement\n\n8 GiB / 250k streams, both containers built by one driver from one deterministic history (`seal_pack`\nchanges only the sealed artifact, never the log). Six cells, **one binary** — no cross-build\nconfound. `PS` pack+section, `PN` pack with every section body flipped (identity untouched, since the\ntrailer hash covers header+directory only, so the pack opens and only this accelerator drops — the\npre-bone state), `LR` loose+`.reg`, `LN` loose with `.reg` renamed away, plus `PS2`/`LR2` **null\ncontrols** (byte-identical re-measurements of `PS`/`LR`). `posix_fadvise(DONTNEED)` before every\ntimed open. Round order alternates FWD `[PS PS2 PN LN LR LR2]` / REV, 6 rounds, medians of 6.\n\n### Shape B — bn-26pp's regime (per_batch=32, payload=1 KiB; ~1 batch/stream, registrations scattered across the whole store; 33 segments, 8.1M events, 32 packs / 32 `.reg`)\n\n| cell | what | median | min | max |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| PS  | pack + REGISTRY_DELTA | **265.7 ms** | 240.4 | 272.9 |\n| PS2 | null control (= PS)   | 255.2 ms | 244.6 | 281.1 |\n| LR  | loose + `.reg`        | **265.5 ms** | 236.9 | 277.4 |\n| LR2 | null control (= LR)   | 272.9 ms | 245.2 | 276.3 |\n| PN  | pack, section dropped | 11 915.5 ms | 11 740.2 | 12 101.6 |\n| LN  | loose, `.reg` dropped | 9 843.1 ms | 9 520.9 | 9 948.6 |\n\n- **Target met: PS vs LR = +0.1%**, against a null-control floor of 2.8% (LR/LR2) to 4.0% (PS/PS2).\n  Pack-sealed cold open is indistinguishable from the `.reg` path at the same shape.\n- Section vs no section: **44.8x**. `.reg` vs no `.reg`: 37.1x (reproduces bn-26pp's 38.5x, so the\n  corpus is in their regime).\n- **The gap this bone existed to close:** pre-bone, pack cold open was 11 915 ms against the loose\n  path's 265 ms — **44.9x slower**. Now 1.001x.\n- `PN` is an *upper bound* on true pre-bone pack behaviour: it still pays the section pread + verify\n  before failing, and that whole machinery is bounded by the entire PS cell (≤266 ms, ≤2.2%).\n- All four arms recover `total_events=8360081` identically. Post-open RSS is *lower* with the section\n  (+129.1 MB vs +148.6 MB, −13%) — the same effect bn-26pp saw, fewer log pages faulted in.\n- Confirmed with `sync` between toggle and open (ruling out a writeback confound from rewriting 4 GiB\n  of packs): PN 11 987 / 12 408 / 12 411, LN 10 018 / 9 994 / 10 065, LR 286 / 287 / 281, PS 283 /\n  275 / 276 ms.\n\n### Shape A — the same 8 GiB/250k with per_batch=4, payload=256 B (28 batches/stream, so ~every stream is in ~every segment; only 2 segments carry registrations)\n\n| cell | median | | cell | median |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| PS  | 1673.8 ms | | LR  | 1666.2 ms |\n| PS2 | 1679.2 ms | | LR2 | 1723.6 ms |\n| PN  | 2050.6 ms | | LN  | 2058.2 ms |\n\nPS vs LR **+0.5%** (null floor 0.3–3.4%) — target met here too. The absolute win is only 1.22x\nbecause this shape's open is directory-decode bound (8.25M directory entries; warm open is 1.5 s), and\nthe registrations sit contiguously at the head of the log where readahead makes the preads cheap. Both\ncontainers' deltas remove the same ~380 ms, as they must. Reported because it is the shape I measured\nfirst and it shows where the delta does *not* dominate.\n\n### Cost\n\nSections total 12.16 MiB across 32 packs — exactly the `.reg` total for the same history (same bytes),\ni.e. +0.30% of sealed bytes, +0.10% of the store. Seal cost is the identical `encode_registry_delta`\ncall plus a section append inside a file already being written — strictly cheaper than the extra file,\n`fsync` and dir-`fsync` the loose path pays. bn-26pp measured that call's drain cost at +2.4% (inside\nnoise).\n\n### Observation, not this bone's problem\n\n`PN` (11 915 ms) is 21% slower than `LN` (9 843 ms): at this shape the pack container's *no-delta*\ncold open costs ~2 s more than the loose one's, reproducibly and with caches sync-settled. It vanishes\nentirely once the delta lands (PS ≡ LR), so it only ever showed in the state this bone removes. Left\nuninvestigated; happy to open a bone if the lead wants it chased.\n\n## 5. Differential evidence\n\n`crates/mess-store/tests/registry_delta_differential.rs` gains the pack arm (suite now 6 tests):\n\n- `pack_sections_are_byte_identical_to_the_log` — 6 seeded random histories; every pack's\n  `REGISTRY_DELTA` yields *exactly* the `(first_global_pos, payloads)` list a raw scan of that\n  segment's log produces, and any pack without a usable section is proved to have no registrations in\n  its log. Also asserts a pack-sealed store writes **zero** `.reg` files.\n- `pack_section_reg_file_and_log_all_fold_one_registry` — the bone's differential. Per seed, a packed\n  and a loose store from the same history; their raw-log oracles must agree first, then\n  section-accelerated ≡ `.reg`-accelerated, then every section damaged (→ preads) ≡ half damaged\n  (mixed within one open) ≡ both, on the full bijection fingerprint (both high-water marks, every\n  `id→name`, every `name→id`, category, head).\n- `a_store_with_both_containers_folds_one_registry` — the migration shape: sealed loose, reopened\n  with `seal_pack` on, new segments seal as packs. One open then folds from `.reg` files, pack\n  sections and raw preads at once; all of {`.reg` present/absent} × {section valid/damaged} fold the\n  same registry. Asserts the pack phase adds no `.reg`.\n\nUnit tests in `sealed/driver.rs`:\n- `pack_seal_carries_the_registry_delta_as_a_section` — just-sealed index, lazy reopen and eager\n  reopen all yield the same records, and those records equal the `.reg` the loose path writes for the\n  identical input; no sibling `.reg` exists.\n- `pack_seal_without_registrations_emits_no_section`.\n- `a_damaged_registry_delta_section_degrades_locally` — flips the first/middle/last byte of the\n  section body (located by walking the directory, so it survives a reordering): pack still opens, pack\n  identity unchanged, pointers still exact, only the delta drops.\n- `a_pack_section_layout_mismatch_is_refused` — a fully CRC-consistent pack carrying a delta whose\n  batch layout disagrees with its own pointer directory; only the cross-check can catch it, and does.\n\n## 6. Gates\n\n- `cargo nextest run -p mess-index -p mess-store` — **451/451 pass**, 11 skipped.\n- `cargo nextest run -p mess-cli -p mess-log` — 375/375 pass (I touched a mess-cli test's `PackInput`).\n- `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` — clean.\n- `just fmt-check` — clean.\n- mess-bench full: **PASS, all 14 floor-gated metrics within tolerance**.\n- `mess verify` on the 8 GiB pack corpus: verified, every section checksum and every footer identity.\n- Did NOT run full `just test` (lead gates).\n- Cleanup: measurement driver deleted (never committed), both 8 GiB corpora + the calibration corpus\n  removed, no stray processes.\n\n## 7. Deferred\n\n- The CLI does not enumerate pack sections (`mess inspect` reports `dir_codec` only), so there is no\n  operator-facing \"does this segment carry a registry delta\". Out of scope here; a one-liner if wanted.\n- The `PN` vs `LN` container asymmetry above.\n- `backup.rs`'s `.reg` policy (bn-w5my) is untouched and unaffected: a pack-sealed store has no `.reg`\n  to have a policy about, and the section travels with the pack that is already in the cut."}	blake3:iyQckW6Ig5pyu9UUB9Evl-orxJU8afCQ9FnLHWVwJSQ
1785608618794840	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx8hE		item.move	bn-3h64	{"state":"done"}	blake3:TfsxDSnR8cHNPyML4Dok9EscwpY7Pnwj3AoaJ4GfTSQ
