1785608670539817	mess-yor-matrix	itc:v3:AWLJnJOZJJJJmeUAAAAAQdbq7VVVt6AAAAEzAg		item.assign	bn-1yor	{"action":"assign","agent":"mess-yor-matrix"}	blake3:YurNok8qA9iND_0rNfn-2kX9xF1h5s-YHC7AyNb6bjM
1785610050992314	mess-yor-matrix	itc:v3:AWLJnJOZJJJJmeUAAAAAQdbq7VVVt6AAAAEzAw		item.comment	bn-1yor	{"body":"bn-1yor part 1/3 — METHOD + THE FOUR MISSING CELLS\n\nFraming per bn-35fy: decision evidence for bn-ccx1, not certification. Everything measured this week\nis CITED, not re-run. Workspace bn-1yor holds a MEASUREMENT-ONLY flip that NEVER MERGES:\n`EngineOptions::default()` returns `seal_pack: env(\"MESS_SEAL_PACK\") != \"0\"` (default TRUE), so ONE\nbinary runs both arms and `MESS_SEAL_PACK=0` reproduces trunk exactly — no cross-build confound. Host\nThreadripper 9970X/64c/ext4 NVMe, 2.3T free, loads noted per cell. ABBA everywhere. Corpora deleted,\nno stray processes. The unbounded snapshot_law was NOT run.\n\n== CELL 1 — full workspace suite with the default flipped (the high-value cell) ==\n\n`MESS_SNAPSHOT_LAW_ITERS=25 cargo nextest run --workspace --no-fail-fast`\n\n  pack default-on : 1153 run, 1136 passed, 17 FAILED, 28 skipped\n  MESS_SEAL_PACK=0: 1153 run, 1153 passed,  0 failed, 28 skipped   <- baseline: all 17 are the flip\n\nALL 17 ARE TEST DEBT. ZERO REAL BUGS. Every one fails on its own FIXTURE precondition (\"No such file\nor directory\", \"no rolled segment carries a .pidx\", \"fixture must corrupt at least one .pcol\"), never\non an engine assertion — they build a store through `..EngineOptions::default()` and then reach for\nloose-sidecar files that pack mode legitimately does not write.\n\n  mess-cli/tests/corruption_detection.rs  detects_pidx_crc_flip, detects_pcol_corruption\n  mess-cli/tests/rebuild_byte_equal.rs    rebuild_index_reproduces_pidx_byte_for_byte,\n                                          rebuild_is_deterministic\n  mess-cli/tests/retention_explain.rs     deletable_matches_decision_function,\n                                          blocked_matches_decision_function\n  mess-cli/tests/seal_namespace.rs        doctor_still_reports_a_pidx_sealed_store_as_healthy,\n                                          doctor_reports_a_shadowed_pidx_as_inert\n  mess-store/tests/sealed_candidate_lifecycle.rs (8)\n       a_candidate_claiming_undurable_coverage_is_refuted,\n       a_corrupt_candidate_is_refuted_quarantined_and_resealed,\n       a_crash_before_the_footer_converges_to_a_footer_verified_segment,\n       a_crash_between_quarantine_and_reseal_still_converges,\n       a_crash_midway_through_the_quarantine_finishes_it,\n       an_identity_mismatched_candidate_is_refuted,\n       repeated_reopen_judges_a_candidate_once_and_then_converges,\n       several_refuted_candidates_in_one_open_all_converge\n  mess-store/tests/engine_block_reads.rs  block_reads_are_byte_identical_live_reopened_and_cache_starved\n\nFixture sites: mess-cli/tests/common/mod.rs:49 `build_corpus`, sealed_candidate_lifecycle.rs:62\n`rolling_opts()`, engine_block_reads.rs `opts()`.\n\nTHE FIX bn-ccx1 SHOULD MAKE, and why it is not cosmetic: pin `seal_pack: false` in those LOOSE\nfixtures rather than re-point them at packs. Each already has a pack sibling\n(`build_pack_corpus`/`build_sealed_pack_store` in mess-cli/tests/common/mod.rs;\nsealed_candidate_lifecycle.rs:810 `EngineOptions { seal_pack: true, ..rolling_opts() }`;\nengine_seal_pack.rs `sidecar_opts`/`pack_opts`). Letting the new default carry them converts loose\ncoverage into a duplicate of the pack arm and silently DELETES the loose-sidecar tests — for a mode\nthat must keep working forever on existing stores. ~17 one-line fixture edits, no production change.\n\nTHE POSITIVE HALF, which is the real evidence: the other 1136 tests ran the PACK path (their fixtures\ntake the default) and passed. That includes mess-soak::smoke (soak_smoke_survives_crashes_with_all_\nprobes_on and soak_smoke_crashless_clean_pass — soak config.rs:143 uses `..EngineOptions::default()`),\ndifferential_model, snapshot_law@25, pack_snapshot, engine_reopen_cycles, engine_name_durability,\nbank_account, registry_delta_differential (incl. all three mixed-container tests), parity_seal,\nmess-log stateright, and social::projections.\n\n== CELL 2 — mess-bench full, ABBA (loose, pack, pack, loose), one session ==\n\n`mess-bench run --mode full --floors crates/mess-bench/floors.json`, loads 10.9 / 7.4 / 5.1 / 5.2.\nALL FOUR CELLS: \"PASS: all 14 floor-gated metrics within tolerance\". Deltas (B=pack mean, A=loose\nmean) with each arm's own two-run spread so the noise is visible:\n\n  metric                                      Aspread  Bspread     B/A\n  engine.sealed_replay.ev_per_s                  1.1%     6.2%   1.0517  <- only genuinely pack cell\n  engine.buffered.ev_per_s                       0.7%    18.0%   0.9341\n  phase5.sealed.bytes_per_event                  0.0%     0.0%   1.0000\n  phase5.sealed.columnar_replay.ev_per_s         2.9%     0.8%   0.9871\n  phase5.foldchain.full_chain.ev_per_s           1.0%     0.9%   1.0089\n  phase5.verify.load_verified.ev_per_s          15.6%    15.5%   0.9971\n  recovery.s_per_gib                            14.8%     2.9%   1.0539\n  mess_log.buffered.ev_per_s     NULL CTRL      15.0%     7.2%   0.9503\n  mess_log.durable.ev_per_s      NULL CTRL       0.7%     0.9%   1.0052\n  sealed.global_scan.ev_per_s    NULL CTRL       3.3%     9.9%   1.0324\n  sealed.stream_replay.ev_per_s  NULL CTRL       2.5%    10.3%   0.9801\n  engine.rc.head_reads_per_s     NULL CTRL      35.9%     4.7%   0.9010\n  engine.rc.resolve_per_s        NULL CTRL      25.4%     3.7%   0.8836\n\nNULL CONTROLS ARE LOAD-BEARING. mess_log.* and sealed.* drive mess-log/mess-index directly and never\nconstruct an EngineOptions — literal no-ops for this change, reading 0.95-1.03.\nengine.reader_contention.* DOES build an engine, but with 2 GiB segments and no seal_active call, so\nno seal ever happens — also a no-op, and it read 0.88-0.90 with a 25-36% within-arm spread. The\nresolvable band on this host today is ~+/-15% (tight metrics) to +/-35% (contention). Every cell,\nincluding engine.sealed_replay's +5.2%, sits inside it: NEUTRAL, mildly positive, and directionally\nconsistent with bn-dbz's informational 7.426/7.424 -> 7.527/7.506 M ev/s.\n\nCAVEAT bn-dbz stated and I confirm: with default options the bench's engine workloads use 2 GiB\nsegments, so only the single explicit `seal_active()` in the engine workload makes a pack at all.\nfloors.json guards the loose paths; it does not test packs. Hence cell 2b.\n\n== CELL 2b — mess-bench with seals FORCED (informational; NOT floor-comparable) ==\n\nFeasibility answer: NOT possible with stock EngineOptions or any env var — the segment size is\nhardcoded at crates/mess-bench/src/workloads/engine.rs:44. I added a workspace-only\n`MESS_BENCH_SEG_MIB` knob (16 MiB) so the engine workload rolls ~4x and the BACKGROUND roll-sealer\nruns. Changing the workload invalidates the floors comparison, so this is a delta only. ABBA, loads\n4.1/2.5/3.0/3.2:\n\n  engine.buffered.ev_per_s       Aspread  2.6%  Bspread 0.6%   B/A 1.0845   pack appends FASTER\n  engine.sealed_replay.ev_per_s  Aspread  1.4%  Bspread 0.8%   B/A 1.0233\n  engine.rc.head_reads_per_s     Aspread 22.3%  Bspread 4.9%   B/A 0.8051   (still 2 GiB: NULL CTRL)\n\nWith the sealer actually running, pack is +8.5% on append throughput against 0.6-2.6% within-arm\nspread — the first cell where an effect clears its own noise, and it points the right way (see the\nfsync count in cell 3). One benign artifact appeared IDENTICALLY in all four cells (same segment 4,\nsame positions): \"SEAL SKIPPED ... re-read for seal: No such file or directory\" — the workload's own\nend-of-run `remove_dir_all` racing a queued roll. Mode-independent, absent at 2 GiB, caused by my\nknob, not by pack."}	blake3:TDC1Pru1qE6sBTUZ9tVRntkiFjtP640iYJFjiYzB-Gs
1785610051019496	mess-yor-matrix	itc:v3:AWLJnJOZJJJJmeUAAAAAQdbq7VVVt6AAAAEzBA		item.comment	bn-1yor	{"body":"bn-1yor part 2/3 — CELLS 3 + 4, then THE DECISION TABLE\n\n== CELLS 3 + 4 — seal cost and file/byte layout, one ABBA on one corpus ==\n\nDriver: bn-2u01's `openscale` build path recovered from commit 5ce00b1a (`maw ws recover` shows\nbn-26pp/bn-3h64 as head_only, so the driver came from git), trimmed of the probe module and given a\nsettable segment size plus a dump of `LogEngine::metrics()`'s seal counters. Lives as an uncommitted\nexample in ws bn-1yor; NEVER MERGES. Shape deliberately equals bn-26pp's seal-path cell so the\nnumbers are comparable: 4 GiB of log, 125,000 streams, 100-event batches, 250 B JSON-ish payloads,\nproduction 256 MiB segments, Durability::Process. ABBA loose/pack/pack/loose, loads 3.1/3.1/2.9/4.1,\n`sync` + 3 s between cells. Both arms produced byte-for-byte the same history: 15,340,800 events /\n153,408 batches / 17 segments (16 sealed + live head) in all four cells.\n\nCELL 3 — SEAL COST\n  drain wall (last append -> engine dropped, i.e. every queued roll durable)\n      loose  25.444 s / 24.564 s   mean 25.004  within-arm spread 3.5%\n      pack   25.392 s / 24.851 s   mean 25.121  within-arm spread 2.2%\n      pack/loose = 1.0047  (+0.5%, INSIDE the 3.5% loose-vs-loose spread)\n  append wall (seal is off the append path, so this should not move)\n      loose 9.007 / 9.216 s   pack 8.997 / 9.152 s   pack/loose 0.9959\n  SEAL-PATH DURABILITY BARRIERS — the one structural, non-noisy difference\n      loose  38 seal fsyncs for 4 in-process seals  = 9.5 barriers per sealed segment\n      pack    8 seal fsyncs for 3-4 in-process seals = 2.0-2.7 barriers per sealed segment\n      ~4x fewer fsyncs per sealed segment. This is the mechanism behind cell 2b's +8.5% append.\n  per-seal duration p50: loose 1.845/1.913 s, pack 2.047/1.980 s (+7%) — REPORTED AS UNRESOLVED:\n      n=3-4 samples per cell (most seals drain inside drop, after the metrics snapshot) and p99 is\n      identical (2.114 s) in three of the four cells. The drain wall above is the load-bearing number.\n  VERDICT: seal cost is NEUTRAL, matching bn-26pp's loose `.reg` result (+2.4% inside a 4.7% spread)\n  and bn-3h64's structural argument (\"a section append inside a file already being written is\n  strictly cheaper than the extra file, fsync and dir-fsync the loose path pays\").\n\nCELL 4 — FILES AND BYTES PER SEALED SEGMENT (16 sealed segments, parity OFF = default)\n      loose  62 files = pidx 16 (10.55 MiB) + pcol 16 (1170.90 MiB) + filter 16 (0.38 MiB)\n                        + reg 14 (6.08 MiB)                    = 3.88 files / sealed segment\n      pack   16 files = seal 16 (1201.66 MiB)                  = 1.00 file  / sealed segment\n      -> 3.88x FEWER FILES. (With the opt-in parity sidecar on: 4.88 vs 2.00, still 2.4x.)\n      -> `.reg` count is 14 not 16 because two segments carried no registration; under pack the\n         delta is a section inside the pack and there is no `.reg` at all (bn-3h64).\n  BYTES\n      sealed  loose 1,245,603,142 B   pack 1,260,031,035 B   = +14,427,893 B, +1.16%\n      log     loose 4,296,619,874 B   pack 4,296,620,898 B   = +1,024 B = exactly 64 B/sealed segment,\n              which is bn-11g's SealPackIdentity footer extension. Quantified, tiny, expected.\n      whole store  5.1616 GiB -> 5.1750 GiB = +0.26%\n  WHERE THE +14.4 MB COMES FROM — this is a real cost, not noise: the pack carries a per-event\n  EVENT_TYPE_IDS section (driver.rs seal_consolidated doc step 1: \"plus the new per-event\n  event_type_ids\") that the loose trio has NO equivalent for. 14,427,893 / 15,340,800 = 0.94 B/event,\n  which is that section (bn-dbz put it at ~1 B/event plus a 4 B/1024-event CRC table). So pack trades\n  +1.16% of sealed bytes for a lazily-read type-id column; bn-dbz measured that column at ~310 ns per\n  BATCH against a payload reassembly of ~5.8 us per EVENT, and against not having it at all the\n  alternative is raw-batch decode.\n\n== THE DECISION TABLE ==\n(source: MINE = measured in this bone; otherwise the cited bone)\n\nDIM 1  reopen residency, 10M events / 8 sealed artifacts\n  loose  n/a (the comparable is trunk-pack: +1012 MB post-open, peak 1151 MB, open 460 ms)\n  pack   +6.6 MB -> +0.0 MB, peak 146 MB, open 64-68 ms\n  src    bn-dbz (ABBA, re-confirmed on the current epoch)     VERDICT  PACK WINS DECISIVELY (155x)\n\nDIM 2  in-process seal residency (a long-running writer)\n  loose whole `.pcol` retained after seal      pack 33.9 MB -> 1008 B; process RSS +76.4 -> +5.9 MiB\n  src    bn-2t2e                                              VERDICT  PACK WINS (13x)\n\nDIM 3  cold open, 8 GiB / 250k streams\n  loose 265.5 ms      pack 265.7 ms  (+0.1%, null-control floor 2.8-4.0%)\n  src    bn-3h64                                              VERDICT  PARITY (was 44.9x worse pre-3h64)\n\nDIM 4  post-open RSS at 250k streams\n  loose +148.6 MB     pack +129.1 MB (-13%)\n  src    bn-3h64                                              VERDICT  PACK SLIGHTLY BETTER\n\nDIM 5  composed bench, default config, floors\n  loose PASS 14/14    pack PASS 14/14; every delta inside a null-control band of 0.88-1.03\n  src    MINE cell 2                                          VERDICT  NEUTRAL\n\nDIM 6  composed bench with the sealer actually running (16 MiB segments)\n  loose 17.29 M ev/s append   pack 18.76 M ev/s (+8.5%, spreads 0.6-2.6%)\n  src    MINE cell 2b                                         VERDICT  PACK BETTER\n\nDIM 7  seal drain wall, 4 GiB / 125k streams / 16 seals\n  loose 25.00 s       pack 25.12 s (+0.5%, inside a 3.5% within-arm spread)\n  src    MINE cell 3                                          VERDICT  NEUTRAL\n\nDIM 8  seal-path fsync barriers per sealed segment\n  loose 9.5           pack 2.0-2.7\n  src    MINE cell 3                                          VERDICT  PACK WINS (~4x)\n\nDIM 9  files per sealed segment (SealPack's actual point)\n  loose 3.88 (pidx+pcol+filter+reg; 4.88 with parity)   pack 1.00 (2.00 with parity)\n  src    MINE cell 4                                          VERDICT  PACK WINS (3.88x)\n\nDIM 10 sealed bytes\n  loose 1,245.6 MB    pack 1,260.0 MB (+1.16% sealed, +0.26% of store) for EVENT_TYPE_IDS\n  src    MINE cell 4                                          VERDICT  PACK SLIGHTLY WORSE, accepted\n\nDIM 11 whole-suite behavioural contracts under the flip\n  loose 1153/1153     pack 1136/1153; 17 failures ALL loose-layout fixtures, 0 real bugs\n  src    MINE cell 1                                          VERDICT  PACK OK, 17 fixture edits owed\n\nDIM 12 trust chain / identity\n  loose footer names nothing; a stand-in sidecar is undetectable\n  pack  footer binds blake3(header++directory); 7-row validation matrix; every refusal raw-falls-back\n  src    bn-11g                                               VERDICT  PACK STRICTLY STRONGER\n\nDIM 13 refutation + re-seal convergence\n  loose quarantine takes the .pidx family (filter/pcol/reg)\n  pack  self-contained family of one; crash-midway converges; identity mismatch refutes\n  src    bn-30u, bn-11g                                       VERDICT  PARITY, pack cleaner\n\nDIM 14 directory codec choice\n  byte-optimal chooser is container-independent (both codecs rehydrate one foldhash DirMap)\n  src    bn-we9x, bn-dcr                                      VERDICT  NEUTRAL\n\nDIM 15 operator surface (doctor / verify / retention / inspect / backup+restore)\n  loose full          pack full since bn-1w4h, incl. identity-complete backup\n  src    bn-1w4h                                              VERDICT  PARITY — bn-1w4h was the blocker\n\nDIM 16 offline sidecar rebuild (`mess rebuild-index`)\n  loose rebuilds .pidx byte-for-byte from bare .log\n  pack  NOT REBUILDABLE OFFLINE — reports `pack-sealed-segment-skipped` and writes nothing\n  src    bn-1w4h gap 1                                        VERDICT  PACK WORSE — the one real regression\n\nDIM 17 registry cold-open acceleration\n  loose `.reg` sibling (38.5x)   pack REGISTRY_DELTA section (44.8x), no `.reg` written\n  src    bn-26pp, bn-3h64                                     VERDICT  PARITY (pack marginally better)"}	blake3:t1gQncDgrNh0p6L_i7LZx8qMmxHde0DqvUA2E3QdcU8
1785610051036802	mess-yor-matrix	itc:v3:AWLJnJOZJJJJmeUAAAAAQdbq7VVVt6AAAAEzBQ		item.comment	bn-1yor	{"body":"bn-1yor part 3/3 — MIGRATION, RECOMMENDATION, WHAT WOULD CHANGE THE ANSWER\n\n== MIGRATION: is \"old stores stay loose forever\" fine? YES, EXPLICITLY ==\n\nThree facts, each already tested:\n\n1. MIXED STORES WORK, and it is the exact shape a default flip produces.\n   - mess-store/tests/engine_seal_pack.rs:392 `legacy_sidecars_and_packs_coexist` — seal loose,\n     reopen with the pack flag, old `.pidx` history dual-reads byte-identically, new rolls seal as\n     `.seal`, both families coexist under sealed/, final reopen still exact.\n   - mess-store/tests/registry_delta_differential.rs:756 `a_store_with_both_containers_folds_one_\n     registry` — one open folding `.reg` files, pack sections and raw preads at once, across\n     {`.reg` present/absent} x {section valid/damaged}.\n   - registry_delta_differential.rs:481 `a_legacy_store_upgrades_in_place`.\n   - All three passed in my flipped run (cell 1).\n\n2. A LOOSE SEGMENT IS NEVER SPONTANEOUSLY RE-SEALED. bn-30u's enqueue rule (engine.rs:1150) requires\n   a durable `*.refuted` quarantine marker AND no footer-verified sidecar AND not the live head. An\n   ADMITTED loose sidecar satisfies none of that, so it is never re-queued. Old stores therefore stay\n   loose indefinitely and only converge to pack for segments that were independently damaged (where\n   the re-seal is happening anyway). A shadowed `.pidx` next to a pack is INERT, not an error —\n   `pidx-shadowed-by-pack` at Info severity (bn-1w4h).\n\n3. THE DOWNGRADE DIRECTION IS ALSO SAFE. bn-11g's `re_sealing_with_packs_off_leaves_the_trailer_at_\n   eof` covers a pack-mode store re-sealed with `seal_pack: false` (the footer shrinks). So an\n   operator who flips back is not stranded.\n\nWHY \"STAY LOOSE FOREVER\" IS ACCEPTABLE RATHER THAN MERELY TOLERATED: every pack win in the table is a\nwin on NEWLY SEALED bytes. A store that never rolls again never had the problem; a store that keeps\nrolling converts its hot tail to packs as it goes and its RSS/file-count profile improves\nmonotonically without a migration tool, a rewrite pass, or a downtime window. The one thing that is\ngenuinely lost on legacy segments is the pack's reopen-residency win (DIM 1) — which is exactly the\nproperty they have today, so nothing regresses. NO MIGRATION TOOL IS NEEDED. Do not build one.\n\n== RECOMMENDATION: FLIP — WITH CAVEATS ==\n\nThe evidence is one-sided on the dimensions that motivated SealPack (files 3.88x, reopen residency\n155x, seal residency 13x, seal barriers 4x) and neutral-or-better everywhere it could have hurt\n(cold open parity, seal drain +0.5% inside noise, floors 14/14 in all four ABBA cells, 1136 tests\ninc. the crash-probe soak green on the pack path). The remaining cost is +1.16% sealed bytes for a\nsection the loose layout simply lacks. No blocking finding.\n\nCAVEATS, enumerated — all four are for bn-ccx1's commit, none require new measurement:\n\nC1. FIXTURE DEBT, 17 tests. Pin `seal_pack: false` in the loose fixtures listed in part 1 — do NOT\n    let them drift onto the default. Loose is a permanently supported READ shape (every existing\n    store) and a supported WRITE shape (the flag stays public), so it must keep its own coverage.\n    This is the flip's real work and it is mechanical.\n\nC2. `mess rebuild-index` HAS NO PACK PATH (bn-1w4h gap 1). Today it reconstructs a `.pidx`\n    byte-for-byte from bare `.log` segments; for a pack-sealed segment it correctly refuses\n    (`pack-sealed-segment-skipped`) because a `.pidx` offered where the footer names a pack is a\n    `PackIdentityMismatch` that the next open would quarantine. Under default-on this command becomes\n    a no-op for every newly sealed segment — the ONE real capability regression in the table. The\n    replacement (engine re-seal) exists and is exercised, but it is not an offline tool. Do not block\n    the flip on it; DO file a bone for an offline pack encoder + footer-finalize path, and say so in\n    the release note, because \"rebuild-index no longer does anything on new stores\" is the kind of\n    surprise operators discover at the worst moment.\n\nC3. bn-dbz DEFERRED ITEM 2 — there is no rewriter for a pre-bn-dbz `.seal` (its EVENT_TYPE_IDS\n    section stays eagerly attached). Bounded at ~1 B/event and self-healing on re-seal. Under\n    default-on the population of such packs is anyone who ran with the flag on before this week,\n    i.e. effectively nobody. Accept; do not build the rewriter.\n\nC4. THE MEASUREMENT KNOB DOES NOT SHIP. `MESS_BENCH_SEG_MIB` and the env-driven default in this\n    workspace are instruments. bn-ccx1's commit should be a plain `seal_pack: true` with an updated\n    doc comment. Separately worth considering: mess-bench's engine workloads never seal at 2 GiB\n    segments, so after the flip the floors STILL will not exercise packs (bn-dbz said this first). If\n    the project wants a standing pack guard, the cheapest version is a floors-gated small-segment\n    engine cell — but that is a new bench workload, not a prerequisite.\n\nNOT A CAVEAT, deliberately: the `PN` vs `LN` container asymmetry bn-3h64 flagged (a pack with its\ndelta section dropped cold-opens 21% slower than loose with `.reg` dropped) exists only in the state\nbn-3h64 removed. With the section present the two are within 0.1%.\n\n== WHAT WOULD CHANGE THE ANSWER (i.e. what a DON'T-FLIP would need) ==\n\n- A real bug in the 17 — there was none; all 17 fail their own fixture precondition, and the\n  MESS_SEAL_PACK=0 control is 1153/1153.\n- A floors regression in any ABBA cell — all four passed 14/14.\n- Seal drain worse than the loose-vs-loose spread — it was +0.5% against 3.5%.\n- Sealed bytes materially larger — +1.16% of sealed, +0.26% of the store, fully attributed.\n- Loss of the loose read path — it is dual-read, tested, and the CLI reports a shadowed `.pidx` as\n  inert rather than as damage.\n\n== DISCIPLINE / HYGIENE ==\n\nWorkspace bn-1yor contains ONLY instruments (the env-driven default in mess-store/src/engine.rs, the\n`MESS_BENCH_SEG_MIB` knob in mess-bench, and examples/yormatrix.rs). NOTHING FROM IT MERGES — the\nflip is bn-ccx1's commit. Bone stays `doing`, claims held (bone:// and workspace://), lead destroys\nthe workspace. All corpora removed ($HOME/.cache/mess-yor deleted), no background processes left,\nloads recorded per cell, ABBA throughout, unbounded snapshot_law not run."}	blake3:TBzJ8WmyqK94Q51dXZcH5ucSE4lwm7rgMJX-QdeiqT8
1785610107274598	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx8xE		item.comment	bn-1yor	{"body":"Lead decision: ADMIT / FLIP accepted per the matrix. The 17 pack-on failures are all fixture-precondition debt (fix = pin seal_pack: false in loose fixtures, preserving loose coverage — NOT re-pointing at packs); zero engine assertions failed with 1136 tests running the pack path including the crash soak, differential model, and mixed-container suites. Perf: neutral at bench segment sizes; +8.5% buffered (vs 0.6-2.6% spread) with seals forced, from ~4x fewer seal barriers; seal drain +0.5% inside noise; cold open parity via bn-3h64. Layout: 3.88 -> 1.00 files/sealed segment at +0.26% store bytes. Migration: none needed, old stores stay loose and are monotonically upgraded by natural re-seals only. bn-ccx1 executes the flip with the four caveats; rebuild-index pack path filed as follow-up."}	blake3:Qf7baCs8t31QSnNA9vSzEHUh_ZWLcVAPw6GRkRTZIEQ
1785610107302217	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx9BE		item.move	bn-1yor	{"state":"done"}	blake3:wlK_u11G7tzJLT6Z_yFEQeRKKQOEaKUFol0fRsMMnsE
1785610108260614	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAEx9RE		item.create	bn-3qh0	{"description":"bn-1w4h made rebuild-index refuse to write a refutable .pidx over a pack-sealed segment (correct), which with seal_pack default-on (bn-ccx1) means rebuild-index is a no-op for newly sealed segments — the one real capability regression the bn-1yor matrix identified. Needs an offline pack encoder path: rebuild the pack from the raw log the way the seal driver does, honoring bn-11g identity (the rebuilt pack gets a NEW identity; the footer must be re-finalized to name it — decide whether offline refinalization is admissible or whether rebuild should quarantine + let the engine re-seal at next open, which may be the safer and much smaller design). Release-note this gap when ccx1 lands.","kind":"task","title":"mess rebuild-index: pack path (offline pack encoder) so rebuild works on pack-sealed segments"}	blake3:GYwuzNMXN7Ot6HMTNV-ubKjSybZYvD2PJE2s-HxFngg
1785610147370368	mess-flip	itc:v3:AWLMkmZJOSeSSeUAAAAAQdqtqur1V6AAAAEzAQ		item.move	bn-ccx1	{"state":"doing"}	blake3:BGU5boN2Cd-tjfFv6W5tUKWO6t4avq519KxMIrOQ7pc
1785610147383716	mess-flip	itc:v3:AWLMkmZJOSeSSeUAAAAAQdqtqur1V6AAAAEzAg		item.assign	bn-ccx1	{"action":"assign","agent":"mess-flip"}	blake3:6sddtlX3DApTzbPdxKJglI5KF9mNBYFLFuMO7MpKaVU
1785610927304665	mess-flip	itc:v3:AWLMkmZJOSeSSeUAAAAAQdqtqur1V6AAAAEzAw		item.comment	bn-ccx1	{"body":"bn-ccx1 EXECUTED — the flip is committed in ws bn-ccx1 (a2f7a20), review cr-3idjop requested from\n@mess-security (risk:high checklist), NOT self-approved. Bone stays doing, claims held, nothing pushed\nor merged.\n\n== THE FLIP ==\ncrates/mess-store/src/engine.rs — `EngineOptions::default().seal_pack` false -> true, one field. Its\nrustdoc now describes pack sealing as the default (install protocol + bn-11g footer identity), names\n`seal_pack: false` the COMPATIBILITY MODE and states why it is permanent (reopen dual-reads, both\nfamilies coexist, legacy segments are never spontaneously re-sealed), records the rebuild-index gap\ninline, and points at bn-1yor/bn-ccx1 as the decision record. The Default impl comment carries the\none-line evidence summary (3.88->1.00 files, 155x reopen residency, ~4x fewer seal barriers, floors\n14/14, +0.26% store bytes). No other production logic touched — bn-1yor caveat C4 honoured: no\nenv-driven default, no MESS_BENCH_SEG_MIB, no instruments in this commit.\n\n== FAILURE LIST REPRODUCED BEFORE PINNING ANYTHING ==\nFlipped, then `MESS_SNAPSHOT_LAW_ITERS=25 cargo nextest run --workspace --no-fail-fast`:\n  1153 run, 1136 passed, 17 FAILED, 28 skipped\nDiffed against bn-1yor cell 1: EXACT MATCH, all 17, nothing beyond. (8 mess-cli:\ncorruption_detection detects_pidx_crc_flip + detects_pcol_corruption; rebuild_byte_equal\nrebuild_index_reproduces_pidx_byte_for_byte + rebuild_is_deterministic; retention_explain\ndeletable_ + blocked_matches_decision_function; seal_namespace\ndoctor_still_reports_a_pidx_sealed_store_as_healthy + doctor_reports_a_shadowed_pidx_as_inert.\n9 mess-store: the 8 sealed_candidate_lifecycle tests bn-1yor listed, plus engine_block_reads\nblock_reads_are_byte_identical_live_reopened_and_cache_starved.) No stop-and-report condition hit.\n\n== PIN INVENTORY — 3 fixture sites, 17 tests, one line each ==\nThe 17 failing tests own only 3 EngineOptions construction sites between them; the mess-cli 8 do not\nconstruct options at all (the corpus builder owns them), so per-test pinning was not even expressible\nwithout changing helper signatures. Each site already had a pack SIBLING, which is what makes the pin\nthe designed pairing rather than a workaround:\n\n1. crates/mess-cli/tests/common/mod.rs::build_corpus  (+ a docstring line naming it the loose builder)\n   tests: corruption_detection detects_pidx_crc_flip, detects_pcol_corruption; rebuild_byte_equal\n     rebuild_index_reproduces_pidx_byte_for_byte, rebuild_is_deterministic; retention_explain\n     deletable_matches_decision_function, blocked_matches_decision_function; seal_namespace\n     doctor_still_reports_a_pidx_sealed_store_as_healthy, doctor_reports_a_shadowed_pidx_as_inert\n   why loose: every one asserts on the loose ARTIFACTS themselves — flip a CRC inside a .pidx,\n     corrupt a .pcol, rebuild a .pidx byte-for-byte, explain retention over sidecar-bearing segments,\n     and doctor's two verdicts about a .pidx (healthy alone / inert when shadowed by a pack). These\n     are the operator-facing guarantees for the millions of segments already on disk in loose form.\n   sibling: common::build_pack_corpus, used by seal_namespace's 6 pack tests and mirrored locally in\n     seal_pack_identity_verify.rs.\n   side effect, stated plainly: build_corpus also feeds backup_restore and repair, which passed on\n     the pack default. Pinning returns them to loose, i.e. exactly their pre-flip arm. Their pack\n     coverage lives in seal_namespace (pack backup incl. quarantine-marker exclusion and\n     seal-pack-identity-verified) and seal_pack_identity_verify.\n\n2. crates/mess-store/tests/sealed_candidate_lifecycle.rs::rolling_opts\n   tests: a_candidate_claiming_undurable_coverage_is_refuted,\n     a_corrupt_candidate_is_refuted_quarantined_and_resealed,\n     a_crash_before_the_footer_converges_to_a_footer_verified_segment,\n     a_crash_between_quarantine_and_reseal_still_converges,\n     a_crash_midway_through_the_quarantine_finishes_it,\n     an_identity_mismatched_candidate_is_refuted,\n     repeated_reopen_judges_a_candidate_once_and_then_converges,\n     several_refuted_candidates_in_one_open_all_converge\n   why loose: bn-30u's refutation/quarantine/re-seal convergence over the LOOSE family, whose\n     quarantine has to move .pidx + .filter + .pcol together — a materially different failure\n     surface from the pack's family-of-one. Three more tests in the file share the helper\n     (an_orphan_candidate..., a_healthy_store..., a_refuted_head_candidate...) and also return to\n     the loose arm, keeping the file's loose lifecycle coverage whole.\n   sibling: the inline pack_opts() at a_corrupt_seal_pack_candidate_takes_the_same_lifecycle\n     (EngineOptions { seal_pack: true, ..rolling_opts() }) — untouched, still the pack arm.\n\n3. crates/mess-store/tests/engine_block_reads.rs::opts\n   test: block_reads_are_byte_identical_live_reopened_and_cache_starved\n   why loose: bn-2ib's byte-identity differential for sidecar-served block reads — live vs reopened\n     vs cache-starved vs corrupt/truncated .pcol falling back to the raw log. The module doc is\n     written entirely in terms of .pidx/.pcol. The file's other 4 tests share the helper and return\n     to loose with it.\n   sibling: engine_seal_pack.rs's pack_opts suite — differential_pack_vs_sidecar_is_byte_identical,\n     pack_reopen_serves_cold_tier_byte_identical, pack_injection_matrix_reopens_byte_identical.\n\nZero production behaviour rides on the pins. The pack path keeps its broad default coverage: the\nother ~1136 tests take the default, including mess-soak::smoke (both crash-probe and clean),\ndifferential_model, snapshot_law@25, pack_snapshot, engine_reopen_cycles, engine_name_durability,\nbank_account, registry_delta_differential (all three mixed-container tests), parity_seal, and\nsocial::projections."}	blake3:EEqGZ6BaBlbFzKKc04es_bYZXgVxCkhIRK5I7jB6Otc
1785610927326187	mess-flip	itc:v3:AWLMkmZJOSeSSeUAAAAAQdqtqur1V6AAAAEzBA		item.comment	bn-ccx1	{"body":"bn-ccx1 part 2/2 — DOCS, RELEASE NOTE, SUITE + GATES\n\n== DOCS ==\n- EngineOptions::seal_pack rustdoc: default/compat-mode framing, gap, decision-record pointer (above).\n- crates/mess-cli/src/main.rs RebuildIndex --help + crates/mess-cli/src/rebuild.rs module doc: the\n  command is now a no-op on every newly sealed segment; still works on legacy/compat stores; cites\n  bn-1yor's \"one real capability regression\" finding and bn-3qh0.\n- docs/spec/07-backup.md: the cut now names `.seal` alongside the legacy trio in §1 and in the layout\n  block, and distinguishes them correctly — copying LOOSE sidecars is a restore-speed optimisation,\n  copying a NAMED pack is mandatory (the footer inside the copied .log names it; leaving it behind\n  restores a store that fails its own verify with seal-pack-missing). Manifest `role` prose notes the\n  token stays `sidecar` for both shapes (bn-1w4h).\n- README.md mess-index row: one `.seal` per sealed segment by default, loose sidecars still read and\n  writable.\n- crates/mess-store/examples/owb_bench.rs: killed the now-false \"seal_pack is OFF by default\" comment;\n  the example pins both arms explicitly so a measurement never inherits the default.\n- Checked and deliberately NOT changed: docs/spec/01-log-format.md §3.3.3 and 02-recovery.md §8.3 are\n  format-normative (\"a segment MAY have a SealPack installed\") and stay correct under either default;\n  spikes/*/REPORT.md are dated records of what was true at the time; examples/social never seals\n  (256 MiB segments, no seal_active) and says nothing about seal_pack.\n\n== RELEASE NOTE (in the commit message; there is no CHANGELOG in this repo, so the commit is the\nrecord) ==\n\"mess rebuild-index is now a NO-OP on pack-sealed segments, i.e. on every segment a current engine\nseals. It reports pack-sealed-segment-skipped per segment and writes nothing, because there is no\noffline pack encoder and a .pidx offered where the footer names a pack is exactly the\nPackIdentityMismatch the next open would quarantine (bn-1w4h gap 1). It still rebuilds legacy\nloose-sidecar stores and stores run with seal_pack: false. The replacement today is an engine re-seal,\nwhich is not an offline tool. bn-3qh0 tracks the offline pack encoder + the footer-refinalize decision\nthat would close the gap. This is the one real capability regression in bn-1yor's table and it was\nadmitted knowingly.\"\n\n== SUITE + GATES ==\n- MESS_SNAPSHOT_LAW_ITERS=25 cargo nextest run --workspace --no-fail-fast, post-pin:\n  1153 run, 1153 PASSED, 28 skipped.\n  One intervening run showed a single failure, mess-log::alloc\n  zero_allocs_per_event_on_the_hot_path — reran alone (PASS) and reran the whole workspace (PASS\n  1153/1153). It is an allocation-count assertion sensitive to parallel load, unrelated to this\n  change (mess-log does not know EngineOptions). Recording it rather than hiding it.\n- cargo check --workspace --all-targets: clean.\n- just fmt-check: clean.\n- mess-bench run --mode full --floors crates/mess-bench/floors.json (release, MESS_BENCH_DIR on\n  real fs, host load 7.4 at start): \"PASS: all 14 floor-gated metrics within tolerance\". FLOORS\n  UNMOVED, as bn-1yor cell 2 predicted: the bench's engine workloads use 2 GiB segments and never\n  seal, so seal_pack is a literal no-op for them; mess_log.* and sealed.* never construct an\n  EngineOptions at all.\n  Single-run cross-session comparison against ledger-3h64.json (trunk at this epoch, run ~45 min\n  earlier at a different load) is load-dominated and informational only: engine.buffered 1.118,\n  engine.sealed_replay 1.018, sealed.global_scan 0.767 (a NULL CONTROL measuring 1.88 BILLION ev/s\n  against a 25M floor — 75x headroom), reader_contention 0.87-1.15. Everything sits inside bn-1yor's\n  established resolvable band (~+/-15% tight, ~+/-35% contention). No paired ABBA was run here\n  because bn-1yor already ran four ABBA cells on this exact question and all four passed 14/14.\n  Ledger: /tmp/.../scratchpad/ledger-pack.json (scratch, not committed).\n\n== HYGIENE ==\nEdited only in ws bn-ccx1. No sibling workers. Nothing pushed, nothing merged, bone stays doing,\nbone:// and workspace:// claims held. No background processes left; the bench corpus lives under\n$HOME/.cache/mess-bench where mess-bench manages it."}	blake3:4DSXIoKLyQXa1OHIjL-NPQCuevIUhq2GYh-b3_ol2zg
