1785694546841411	mess-w-1r9c	itc:v3:AWLMmTP-ZJzJJEAAAAAAQdrW_7V2qgAAAAEwAw		item.comment	bn-1r9c	{"body":"Start: staked claims, bone doing, workspace bn-1r9c created from main (epoch ef02c03ca90e). Plan: new crates/mess-bench/src/workloads/live_tail.rs mirroring bn-1dlb reader_contention structure. Three passes over one primed corpus: (A) catch-up, solo vs N concurrent readers paging read_global_page from position 0 to the watermark; (B) live tail, N readers started at the pre-burst watermark following a concurrent writer, parking on await_watermark_past when caught up; (C) hot multi-stream read_stream paging. Cursor advance strictly via GlobalPage.frontier per mess-store/src/subscription.rs and spec 06 SUB2 - no dense-position assumption, no watermark jump. Will seed floors loose at ~half measured with loadavg noted. Also adding an --only filter to mess-bench run so a single workload can be measured without a full-suite pass on this shared host."}	blake3:A4piNuIYhQUBWYwiTG3geqMnI4PnD_-Ixyz5pEwKWu0
1785694689869340	mess-w-3qh0	itc:v3:AWLOZknnkyfzEAAAAAAAQd21e9a_sAAAAAEtAw		item.comment	bn-3qh0	{"body":"Started (mess-w-3qh0). Claims staked on bone and workspace. Workspace bn-3qh0 created from main at epoch ef02c03ca90e. Reading the required surfaces now: sealed_candidate.rs quarantine family, engine open owed_reseal merge, rebuild.rs plus SealedArtifact classification, sealed pack identity (bn-11g), the seal driver pack path, and docs/spec/07-backup.md. Design decision comment to follow before any code."}	blake3:vUcSxDYNVDClglBkBO1TrfyRBhiCz9t8R1xzMfip4EU
1785694815603758	mess-w-3qh0	itc:v3:AWLOZknnkyfzEAAAAAAAQd21e9a_sAAAAAEtBA		item.comment	bn-3qh0	{"body":"DESIGN DECISION: Design B (quarantine plus engine re-seal). Chosen after two premise-check experiments run against a real pack-sealed rolled store BEFORE writing product code, both of which passed.\n\nWHY B, in one paragraph. B needs zero new format code and zero offline mutation of the log: the pack is produced by the one encoder the engine already uses, and the footer is re-finalized by the one finalize_footer the sealer already uses, so the rebuilt pack is by construction exactly what a fresh seal produces and is identity-bound the same way. Design A would have to duplicate the pack encoder plus the footer-extension encoder outside the engine, keep both in lockstep with PACK_FORMAT_VERSION forever, and write into the sealed log file (the sole authority) from an offline tool - and A does not even escape B: A crashing between the pack rename and the footer rewrite leaves a pack whose identity the footer does not name, which is PackIdentityMismatch, which converges only through bn-30u quarantine plus re-seal. A therefore needs B's machinery for its own crash windows while adding a second copy of the most safety-critical byte surface in the project. No disqualifier for B was found (see Q-payload below).\n\nQ1. Does the bn-30u machinery extend to pack artifacts, or is it pidx-specific? It extends fully. PRIMARY_EXTENSIONS is seal plus pidx; quarantine_family already knows a pack is self-contained (family of one, no derived siblings); load_sealed already calls refute_unparsable on a bad pack; check_named_pack already refutes an identity mismatch; and engine open already merges refutations plus on-disk refuted markers into owed_reseal. What is MISSING is not pack support - it is the case where there is no artifact to rename. That is exactly the regression case: the operator deleted or lost sealed/, so there is no candidate, so nothing is refuted, so no marker is written, so owed_reseal is empty and the segment is never re-queued. Premise check 1 proves it: build a pack-sealed 3-segment store, delete one rolled segment's .seal, reopen three times - refuted=0, pending_reseal=[] every time, pack never comes back, reads correct throughout. That is the bn-1w4h/bn-ccx1 capability regression in one line.\n\nSo the only thing that must be added is an OFFLINE WITHDRAWAL entry point that can record re-seal intent when there is nothing to rename. It does not need a new marker kind. bn-30u's trigger is a structural name parse (extension refuted plus segment_id_from_name), never file content, so writing the marker directly into the segment's own quarantine slot (seg-ID.seal.refuted) is the same trigger with the same bounded one-slot-per-segment-per-kind property. Premise check 2 proves it end to end: delete the pack, drop a bare marker in the slot, reopen - pending_reseal=[victim], reads byte-correct while the pack is absent, and on drop the sealer writes a fresh .seal and re-finalizes the footer to name it (the new identity differs from the old one, so it really is a fresh pack, and named_pack_identity equals pack_identity_on_disk afterwards). A further reopen shows refuted=0 and pending_reseal=[] - the marker is inert once the segment is admitted again, exactly as bn-30u documents. Net engine.rs code change required: ZERO. Only a doc line.\n\nQ2. What does the footer say after quarantine? It still names the old pack identity, and that is safe because MISSING and CORRUPT take different paths. Corrupt or substituted: the pack is opened as a candidate, check_named_pack refutes it, fail-closed per bn-11g. Missing: there is no candidate at all, so check_named_pack is never reached, the segment is simply absent from ids and pending, recovery scans it from the log and serves it. Verified empirically in both premise checks - reads byte-exact with the footer naming a pack that does not exist. Named-but-missing is a miss and a fallback, never an error, per the discardable-acceleration law. One consequence I will document rather than hide: mess verify reports seal-pack-missing at Error severity in that window (verify.rs, bn-1w4h), which is pre-existing behaviour and is also the state a lost pack already puts the store in before rebuild-index runs - but it is why the converge-now flag matters.\n\nQ3. Does the store still work between withdrawal and next open? Yes, and it is tested: recovery scans the segment through the acceptance kernel and every read comes off the raw log. The withdrawal touches nothing but the sealed/ directory - never a .log byte, never the footer, never .par.\n\nQ4. Offline vs at-open UX. Default stays offline and honest: rebuild-index writes the durable re-seal request and reports reseal-pending, saying in the finding that the rebuild completes at the next engine open. New optional flag --reseal opens a LogEngine once at the store and drops it, which drains the sealer and leaves a real footer-bound pack on disk before the command exits - so the traditional leaves-an-index-behind UX is available in one command, and the artifact it leaves is strictly better than an offline encoder's because it is the engine's own. rebuild-index already requires exclusive access, so taking the lock is not a new constraint.\n\nFLAG SEMANTICS (bn-1w4h refusal preserved). A HEALTHY pack (opens, and its identity matches what the footer names, or the footer names none) is still refused by default with pack-sealed-segment-skipped and nothing is written. --force withdraws a healthy pack too, for an operator who wants it rebuilt from the log. A BROKEN pack path - footer names a pack that is absent, pack unreadable, identity mismatch, identity unresolvable - is repaired without --force, because that is precisely what a repair tool is for and because the engine would quarantine those itself at the next open anyway (the absent case being the one it cannot).\n\nBuilding now: mess-store gets a public withdraw_sealed_index in sealed_candidate (marker written first with create_new so it can never clobber preserved evidence, then the quarantine rename, which replaces it with the real bytes when there are any), rebuild.rs gets the pack classification plus the two flags, main.rs gets the flags and the doc, engine.rs gets one doc line retiring the known-gap note."}	blake3:TBq-bYDDfwCxBi_8t3hkSZT1AzeK_o8iU-DkSPFJdHQ
1785695587787625	mess-w-1r9c	itc:v3:AWLMmTP-ZJzJJEAAAAAAQdrW_7V2qgAAAAEwBA		item.comment	bn-1r9c	{"body":"Done, review cr-susd4q requested from mess-dev. Workspace bn-1r9c, 1 commit (e8f9ba5), 7 files, +1088/-23.\n\nWORKLOAD: live_tail, registered in run_selected as slug live_tail, label 'live tail / global read'. Full params: 32 streams, 1,000,000-record history in 250-record appends, 8 gap streams punching registry holes through the history, 4 readers, page_size 256, two 1,000,000-record write bursts, 300 pings. Smoke params scaled down. Runtime about 2s at full size.\n\nENGINE API EXERCISED: Backend::read_global_page (the whole point - previously zero calls suite-wide, so ActiveIndex::global_range had zero coverage), Backend::read_stream paged over hot unsealed streams (ActiveIndex::stream_entries_from), SubscribeBackend::watermark, SubscribeBackend::await_watermark_past, Backend::head, Backend::append_batch. Deliberately NOT exercised: the sealed tier - segment_size is 2 GiB so nothing seals, because sealed reads already have coverage in sealed_pointer and engine.sealed_replay and letting a seal land mid-run would silently mix the two. Also not exercised: EventStore::subscribe itself. This drives the Backend seam directly, same as reader_contention, so the numbers are the engine's and not the facade's.\n\nMEASURED (3 runs, --only live_tail, release, loadavg 1.39-1.59 one-minute with two sibling workers active):\ncatchup_solo 7.603 / 7.602 / 7.635 M ev/s\ncatchup_loaded 14.82 / 15.90 / 15.90 M ev/s\ncatchup_scaling 1.95 / 2.09 / 2.08\nwriter_tailed 12.60 / 9.86 / 12.17 M ev/s\nwriter_retention 0.69 / 0.48 / 0.60\ntail_reader 13.16 / 11.58 / 11.97 M ev/s\nappend_to_delivery 16.71 / 22.03 / 16.56 us\nstream_paging 14.82 / 13.63 / 14.54 M ev/s\n\nFLOORS SEEDED (floors.json, all tolerance 0.10, all provenance-noted with date, trunk ef02c03c, the 3-run cluster, and the loadavg):\nengine.live_tail.catchup_solo.ev_per_s min 3,800,000\nengine.live_tail.catchup_loaded.ev_per_s min 7,400,000\nengine.live_tail.writer_tailed.ev_per_s min 4,900,000\nengine.live_tail.tail_reader.ev_per_s min 5,700,000\nengine.live_tail.stream_paging.ev_per_s min 6,800,000\nengine.live_tail.append_to_delivery_us max 66.0\nNot gated: catchup_scaling and writer_retention, both derived ratios with visible run-to-run swing - same call bn-1dlb made leaving reader_contention.writer_retention ungated. Nothing added to floors-ci.json, also following bn-1dlb: nobody has characterised a GitHub runner with four contending readers.\n\nTWO FINDINGS WORTH FLAGGING:\n1. Live tailing costs the writer roughly 30-50 percent of its throughput (retention 0.48-0.72), against reader_contention's 0.98 for head/stream_id_of lookups. A global scan competing with the committer is a materially different animal from an index lookup competing with it, and the suite could not see that before.\n2. This engine commits far faster than the read path materialises, so a flooding writer never lets a tailer catch up - the first cut of this workload logged 4 parks total across 4 tailers and left the caught-up wake path effectively untested. That is why pass 3 exists as a separate paced pass rather than a metric bolted onto pass 2.\n\nCLI: added run --only <names> and list-workloads so a single workload can be measured without a ten-workload pass on this shared host. run_all is now a wrapper over run_selected. A narrowed run is a measurement not a gate - compare skips floors whose metric is absent from the ledger, so the binary prints a NOTE and the README states the nightly gate must never pass --only.\n\nGATES: cargo test -p mess-bench 4 passed 0 failed. cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean, exit 0. just fmt-check clean, exit 0. One full-mode gated run: PASS, all 20 floor-gated metrics within tolerance.\n\nFOLLOW-UPS: (a) read_stream paging is covered only on the hot path; a sealed-stream paging variant is uncovered and would need its own corpus. (b) Latency is reported as a mean only; a p99 would need per-sample retention. (c) These floors are seeded from three runs under ambient load and should be tightened once several quiet runs exist - each source field says so."}	blake3:hHwW4gEvitl6n3yKJOZRtZY8qoHd7c2MpD96FBL9uAM
1785695907046259	mess-w-1r9c	itc:v3:AWLMmTP-ZJzJJEAAAAAAQdrW_7V2qgAAAAEwBQ		item.comment	bn-1r9c	{"body":"Review finding on cr-susd4q fixed in commit 335626a (4 files, +194/-21).\n\nThe finding was correct and it was mine to catch: run_compare counted the floors FILE while compare() skips floors whose metric is absent from the ledger. Unreachable in the gate flow before --only, reachable after it, and it faked the exact line the gate greps for.\n\ncompare_report() now returns a Comparison carrying matched/total alongside the regressions. compare() is a thin wrapper over it so no existing caller changed. run_compare prints two different strings for two different claims, verified against the ledgers already on disk from the earlier runs, no benchmark re-run:\n\nFull ledger, 20 of 20 floors matched, exit 0, byte-identical to the historical string:\nPASS: all 20 floor-gated metrics within tolerance of crates/mess-bench/floors.json\n\nNarrowed ledger from run --only live_tail, 6 of 20 floors matched, exit 0:\nPASS (narrowed): 6 of 20 floor-gated metrics checked; 14 absent from this ledger - NOT a full gate.\n\n(The narrowed line uses an em dash before NOT, as specified.)\n\nTest: compare_pass_line_never_claims_a_full_gate_a_narrowed_ledger_did_not_run in tests/smoke.rs pins both forms through the real binary via CARGO_BIN_EXE_mess-bench with a crafted floors file and two ledgers, rather than through an internal helper, because the observable stderr is what gates read. It asserts the full form appears for a complete ledger, the narrowed form appears for an incomplete one, and that the narrowed output never contains PASS: all.\n\nAlso updated: the --only NOTE now tells the operator which line to expect, the run_selected doc caveat names both strings, and the README --only section shows both verbatim with the guidance that anything grepping for a green gate must match 'PASS: all ' and never a bare PASS.\n\nGates re-run: cargo test -p mess-bench 5 passed 0 failed. cargo check --workspace --all-targets exit 0. just fmt-check exit 0. Full mode not re-run - nothing in this fix touches measurement.\n\nWorkspace bn-1r9c now at 3 commits: e8f9ba5 feat, bb9c75f chore review metadata, 335626a fix."}	blake3:-ot68flDCU_ADXghgkmRo-c3ahVAreBVO6POO21K_Pw
1785695977139423	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExjBI		item.comment	bn-1r9c	{"body":"Merged to trunk. Review cr-susd4q approved after one finding fixed in 335626a: the floors PASS line counted the floors file, not the floors checked, so a run with the new only flag could print the full-gate PASS string while checking a subset. Full runs keep the historical string byte-identical; narrowed runs print PASS (narrowed) M of N, pinned by a binary-level test. Floors seeded loose at half of measured under ambient load with provenance; tighten after quiet runs accumulate. Follow-ups noted on the bone: sealed-stream paging variant, p99 latency."}	blake3:D1e4CspySk_920FNYI08HLAHFlWMTTRj8m0tyu2bSyc
1785695977168006	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExjRI		item.move	bn-1r9c	{"state":"done"}	blake3:T6UjHX5V9DLYvwfcVixc01Hx6EJiF4Y6gQwNAOuGJ1s
1785696064751500	mess-w-11ba	itc:v3:AWKST8kknkmScklAAAAAQar9VXq1dVAAAAE0BA		item.comment	bn-11ba	{"body":"Ready for review: cr-2b0zoc, reviewer mess-dev, 1 commit in workspace bn-11ba.\n\nSURFACE THAT EXISTS NOW\n\nIn-process (crates/mess-store):\n  observability.rs (new) holds AUTHORITY_MODEL, the ADR 0002 classification as data: nine artifact classes, each with a Role of canonical or discardable-accelerator plus a one-line on_loss. LogEngine::observability() returns EngineObservability with seven sections: authority, owner, durability, state, accelerators, fallbacks, backlog.\n  owner: durability mode, queue slots and bytes in use with their capacities and saturation ratios, group width (intents) and group wait (nanos), ack latency, commit latency, conflicts, cancellations, committer throughput, append-input counters.\n  durability: mode, commit and seal barrier histograms, both degradation flags with trip counts and thresholds, the poisoned flag, fold-chain on/off.\n  state: v3 format version, three watermarks (durable, published, active-index applied end), sealed segment count and install generation, summed sealed-index resident bytes, block cache, four registry high-water marks, recovery payload decodes.\n  accelerators: seal_pack_enabled plus one row per installed sealed segment with representation (seal-pack or loose-sidecar), pack identity hex, pack format version, dir codec, resident bytes, and which sub-accelerators are attached.\n  fallbacks: candidates refuted, quarantined, quarantine failures, the per-candidate refutation reasons, registry-delta admitted vs fallback, seals skipped.\n  backlog: reseals owed at open, the pending segment ids, live seal queue depth, jobs dequeued, seals completed and skipped, seal duration.\n\nOffline (crates/mess-cli): mess doctor now emits an authority section from authority.rs, rendering the SAME AUTHORITY_MODEL plus a summary and one row per segment naming which shape serves it (seal-pack, pidx, log-scan), the fallback the engine would take, and a four-way per-artifact state (present, absent, degraded, shadowed). Findings kind authority-accelerators at ok, info, or warn; never error, so the exit code is unchanged. Advisory authority-scope states the offline vs in-process split. No new flags: the section is always present in text, pretty, and json.\n\ndocs/observability.md documents names, units, and the cardinality bound (segment count; no stream, type, or batch id is ever a key or label), and names four things the engine honestly cannot answer.\n\nTESTS: engine_observability 11/11, doctor_authority 9/9, full mess-store 280 passed 5 skipped, full mess-cli 78 passed 3 skipped, mess-log alloc 1 passed. just fmt-check clean, cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean."}	blake3:pL6wkIHRSwBGPrz29fb-qozzhU2f1t3wkzFF4a-go8E
1785696115650763	mess-w-3qh0	itc:v3:AWLOZknnkyfzEAAAAAAAQd21e9a_sAAAAAEtBQ		item.comment	bn-3qh0	{"body":"IMPLEMENTED. Design B, as decided. Review cr-2nh0m5 requested from mess-dev. Workspace bn-3qh0, one commit 9a1105a, 11 files, plus 1673 minus 77.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED\nmess-store sealed_candidate gains withdraw_sealed_index plus the Withdrawal type and WITHDRAWAL_MARKER_BANNER, re-exported from the crate root. It is the offline counterpart to SealedCandidateHealth::refute: same slot, same trigger, same bound. Marker first with create_new plus fsync plus dir fsync, then quarantine of the primary, whose rename replaces the note with the real bytes atomically. engine.rs is DOC-ONLY (one paragraph retiring the known-gap note) - the engine needed no code because bn-30u triggers on a structural name parse, never on content, so the request is just the segment's own quarantine slot occupied.\n\nmess-cli rebuild.rs gains a full pack path: classify_pack mirrors load_sealed's admission rules (healthy, absent, unreadable, identity mismatch, identity unresolvable), reusing verify's read_named_identity rather than a second implementation. Healthy plus no --force keeps bn-1w4h's pack-sealed-segment-skipped exactly. Everything else withdraws, plus the shadowed .pidx if any. --reseal opens a LogEngine once and drops it, which joins the sealer, then reports each rebuilt pack's fresh identity. Two new flags on the subcommand, plus rewritten long help.\n\nTHE ONE THING THE BONE DID NOT ANTICIPATE\nbn-30u already covers pack artifacts completely. What it cannot cover is the artifact that is not there, and that is the whole regression. Premise check 1 pins it as a test: delete a rolled segment's .seal, reopen three times, refuted=0 and pending_reseal empty every time, pack never returns, reads correct throughout. never_reseals_a_deleted_pack_on_its_own now guards that baseline permanently so nobody deletes the API thinking the engine handles it.\n\nA SECOND FINDING WORTH THE LEAD'S ATTENTION\nWithdrawing a pack while a sibling .pidx exists is not merely untidy - it is a correctness trap. The moment the pack leaves the candidate namespace the sidecar becomes the primary candidate. If the footer names a pack the sidecar is refuted at the next open (noise). If the footer names NONE - a legacy D-FMT-10 store mid-migration - the sidecar is ADMITTED, which puts the segment in sealed_ids, which is exactly the condition that suppresses the re-seal: the withdrawal would silently accomplish nothing. So the pack path quarantines the sidecar family too, and a_shadowed_sidecar_is_withdrawn_with_its_pack pins it.\n\nCRASH SAFETY, STEP BY STEP\n1 marker create_new plus fsync plus dir fsync - if we die here the slot is occupied, the pack is untouched, the re-seal is already owed; at open the healthy pack is admitted and an admitted segment is never re-queued, so the request goes inert with no oscillation (a_request_over_a_still_healthy_pack_is_inert), and re-running finishes the job. 2 quarantine of the pack - NotFound tolerant, renames onto the slot, replaces atomically, dir fsync best effort; a crash here is bn-30u's own documented mid-quarantine row. 3 quarantine of the sidecar family - a crash here leaves the request durable and either the sidecar is refuted at open (converges) or admitted (segment keeps a valid legacy index and re-running converges). Repeats are bounded: exactly one slot per segment per kind across three full cycles.\n\nEVIDENCE\nmess-store lib sealed_candidate 15 of 15 (5 new). mess-store offline_reseal_request 5 of 5 new. mess-cli rebuild_pack_path 8 of 8 new. mess-cli rebuild_byte_equal 3 of 3 unchanged and passing, including bn-1w4h's refusal test. Full workspace just test: 1171 tests run, 1171 passed, 28 skipped. cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean. just fmt-check clean. clippy on mess-store and mess-cli adds no new warnings (the few reported are pre-existing in mess-index and two mess-store test files I did not touch).\n\nNOTES FOR THE LEAD\n1 mess verify reports seal-pack-missing at Error severity between a withdrawal and the re-seal. That is pre-existing verify behaviour and is also the state a lost pack already puts the store in BEFORE rebuild-index runs, so nothing new is created - but it is the reason --reseal exists, and bn-11ba may want to word doctor's view of a withdrawn segment to match.\n2 Pre-existing and left alone: on a pack-default store the unsealed live head still gets a legacy .pidx from the non-pack branch. It is footerless, valid, idempotent, and superseded when the segment rolls and seals. Out of scope for this bone but worth a follow-up bone if the shape purity matters.\n3 The rebuilt pack is NOT byte-identical to the original even from identical log bytes - the identity really is fresh, as bn-11g's design implies. The footer is re-finalized to name it, so the pair stays coherent; asserted in both new suites."}	blake3:QbfkYJSeMao0PzMdYH2c_Ickd178jVZyX3ECTL4i7wU
1785696278902459	mess-w-11ba	itc:v3:AWKST8kknkmScklAAAAAQar9VXq1dVAAAAE0BQ		item.comment	bn-11ba	{"body":"Replayed from workspace-local bones store (worker ran bn inside the workspace; event never reached trunk): Progress: mess-store side landed. New module crates/mess-store/src/observability.rs holds AUTHORITY_MODEL (the ADR 0002 canonical vs discardable-accelerator table as data) plus the composed report types; LogEngine::observability() composes them from existing counters plus new owner conflict/cancellation/ack-latency/group-width/group-wait/commit-latency histograms, a live seal backlog gauge, and registry-delta admitted/fallback counts. crates/mess-cli/src/authority.rs renders the same AUTHORITY_MODEL offline as a doctor section with a per-segment accelerator inventory and fallback text. engine_observability suite: 11 tests, 11 passed."}	blake3:ZqOEfF20ZcHlyaSYfCsXJJNlS4gQ0arPRL1GNdyax7s
1785696305393962	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExjhI		item.comment	bn-11ba	{"body":"Merged to trunk at 57c64374 after review cr-2b0zoc. Surface: LogEngine::observability() (authority/owner/durability/state/accelerators/fallbacks/backlog) + mess doctor authority section rendering AUTHORITY_MODEL with per-segment serving mode and artifact states. Verified in review: all new counters off the per-event path, seal backlog gauge is a Drop guard immune to the four continue exits, doctor never exceeds Warn. Honest gaps recorded by the worker stand as follow-up context: active-tier byte accounting (relates to bn-3odg), write-half latency needs a mess-log committer change. Note for bn-2wso/bn-37jh/bn-hvp1: this is the surface those evidence bones should read."}	blake3:Yv2VmuTgEDT4_hU1kJazIifl_eNR98-1gI5fCgXbO-Q
1785696305410842	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExjxI		item.move	bn-11ba	{"state":"done"}	blake3:VTCTSgoaUX-viFsskseenAeqSqeawAOcLPELQFLq-jg
1785696405790403	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExkBI		item.comment	bn-3qh0	{"body":"Merged to trunk at 315b0b5e after review cr-2nh0m5. Design B (quarantine + engine re-seal) ratified: Design A needed B for its own crash windows while duplicating the most safety-critical encoders offline. Review verified: write-order law (marker before destruction, create_new never clobbers evidence), the shadowed-sidecar promotion trap fix with test, inert-when-admitted convergence, never-writes-log assertion, bn-1w4h refusal preserved by default. Worker follow-up notes on record: verify reports seal-pack-missing at Error severity between withdrawal and re-seal (pre-existing; doctor wording alignment candidate), and pack-default stores still write a legacy .pidx for the live head segment (separate bone to be filed)."}	blake3:luv7IOw5h5cidp9BoKGxgha9loTbZjJ1qQdn6yhK_v4
