1785701468416414	mess-w-33l3	itc:v3:AWKSSSczJnnkmTQAAAAAQaqrtre9WsAAAAEyAw		item.comment	bn-33l3	{"body":"mess-w-33l3 starting. Workspace .maw/workspaces/bn-33l3 created from main, claims staked. Plan: (1) read the alloc test + its global_allocator shim, (2) determine nextest process isolation for this binary, (3) instrument a per-thread-partitioned counting allocator to identify WHICH thread allocates inside the measurement window, (4) reproduce under contrived parallel load, then fix per the named mechanism while keeping the assertion's teeth."}	blake3:DHRhr5JuR87geVoKzzObh6EJiIINwOBqCoGgcsG6lNw
1785701916909052	mess-w-3m62	itc:v3:AWLOZJJz-SSTmUAAAAAAQd2qu_qq7QAAAAEwBA		item.comment	bn-3m62	{"body":"FINDING AND DECISIONS, both premise-checked on disk before any product code.\n\nPART A. THE PREMISE IS REFUTED. On trunk (315b0b5e) a pack-default store writes NO artifact at all for the live head - not a .pidx, not anything. bn-3qh0 follow-up note 2 was mistaken, and the 1.00 files per sealed segment claim from bn-1yor is intact and now measured.\n\nStatic evidence - there are exactly two .pidx writers in the tree. One: crates/mess-index/src/sealed/driver.rs:456-459 in SealDriver::seal, which is unreachable when pack is set because driver.rs:447-449 returns into seal_consolidated first, and seal_consolidated writes one .seal and nothing else. Two: the offline mess rebuild-index at crates/mess-cli/src/rebuild.rs:174. Both engine SealDriver constructions pass with_pack(opts.seal_pack) - engine.rs:3013 for the background roll-sealer and engine.rs:4616 for on-demand seal_active - and EngineOptions default is seal_pack true at engine.rs:2811. There is no third path.\n\nEmpirical evidence - probe 1, a pack-default store, 32 KiB segments, 400 batches over 4 streams, real live rolls. Result: 5 rolled segments sealed, 1 live head, and sealed/ holds exactly 5 files, every one a .seal. No .pidx, no .filter, no .pcol, no .reg, in the live store, after close, after reopen, and after a second close. Probe 2 - seal_active over a live head on a pack-default store writes seg-N.seal, footerless, not a .pidx.\n\nSo question A as posed has no object: nothing reads the head .pidx before roll because there is none to read. Suppression is a no-op and classify-the-head-sidecar has no sidecar to classify. When a head DOES deliberately carry an artifact (an explicit seal_active) every surface already handles it correctly: doctor runs check_sealed_artifact for a footerless segment that has one (doctor.rs:424-427) and verify_seal_identity returns early with no trailer (verify.rs:476-478).\n\nBUT THE INVENTORY COMPLAINT IS REAL WITH A DIFFERENT CAUSE, and it is worth fixing. authority.rs:297-301 has only three Serving values, so the live head falls into Serving::LogScan, whose operator-facing text (authority.rs:250-254) says no usable sealed index and the background sealer is owed a seal. For an unsealed head that is FALSE - sealed_candidate.rs:112-116 documents that the live head is excluded from re-seal unconditionally, and engine.rs:3068-3071 implements that. Two consequences, both measured on the probe store: a perfectly healthy pack-default store never reports the authority finding at Ok, it reports Info saying 1 segment has no sealed index; and the served_by_log_scan counter conflates the always-present head with the case an operator actually needs to see, a ROLLED segment that lost its accelerator and is owed a re-seal. The head permanently masks the signal.\n\nDECISION A. Suppress nothing (nothing is written). Classify distinctly instead, on the honest axis: is the segment SEALED. Add a fourth Serving variant for a segment whose .log carries no footer trailer, token unsealed, with fallback text saying it is not sealed yet so it has no sealed index by design and the roll-seal will index it. served_by_log_scan then counts only sealed-but-unaccelerated segments, and a healthy pack store goes back to reporting Ok. The discriminator is mess_log::sealer::read_trailer, one 100-byte pread per segment (sealer.rs:259-279), the same fact doctor's own segment pass already uses as scan.trailer - so authority cannot disagree with doctor about which segments are sealed.\n\nPART B. DECISION: split seal-pack-missing on the durable quarantine slot. Owed-reseal becomes Warn, genuinely lost stays Error, and the split is provably safe for the backup gate.\n\nOwed-reseal -> new kind seal-pack-reseal-pending at Warn. Trigger: footer names a pack, .seal absent, AND the segment has a quarantine marker under sealed/. The predicate mirrors the engine's own trigger exactly - engine.rs:4039-4045 inserts a segment id into the owed set for ANY *.refuted whose name parses to it, not only .seal.refuted - so the CLI cannot invent a second notion of owed. Justification: verify's own contract for this section states that every Error here corresponds to a candidate the next open would refuse and quarantine (verify.rs:461-462). With the pack withdrawn there IS no candidate, the next open refuses nothing, and bn-3qh0 Q2/Q3 plus its offline_reseal_request suite prove the segment converges to a fresh footer-bound pack at that open. Warn also matches what doctor already says about the identical store: quarantined-candidate at Warn with state pending-reseal and queued for a fresh seal at the next open (doctor.rs:610-645). Today verify says Error and doctor says Warn about the same bytes - that is the contradiction, and this closes it.\n\nGenuinely lost stays kind seal-pack-missing at Error, message extended to say that nothing on disk requests a re-seal so the engine will not restore it on its own, and to name mess rebuild-index. Justification, and this is the one that settles it: the Error is LOAD-BEARING for backup and restore. docs/spec/07-backup.md section 1 item 1 requires the cut to be identity-complete and cites seal-pack-missing as an Error rather than a slow path; section 1.2 says a restored store whose footer names an absent pack fails restore's own verify gate; crates/mess-cli/src/backup.rs:222-233 is that reasoning as code; restore.rs:185 gates on verify worst below Error. Downgrading the missing case wholesale would let a torn backup pass silently. The split cannot weaken that gate, because a torn cut can never produce the Warn state: backup.rs:236-241 deliberately excludes *.refuted markers from the cut, so a restored store has no quarantine slot and lands in the Error arm by construction. I will pin that as a test.\n\nNote the shape of the residual severity ladder this produces, which is the real justification: every other Error in the seal-pack family is bytes-exist-and-are-wrong (unreadable, identity-mismatch) or canonical-footer-bytes-are-damaged (identity-unresolvable). seal-pack-missing was the only one where no byte that exists is wrong, and it now splits into the arm that self-heals (Warn) and the arm that needs an operator and guards the backup contract (Error).\n\nDOCTOR ALIGNMENT, same signal, same words. doctor.rs:382-398 tells the operator that ANY footer-sealed segment with no artifact is served from the raw log and is owed a re-seal. bn-3qh0's own premise check 1, now the merged test never_reseals_a_deleted_pack_on_its_own, proves that is false when there is no marker: refuted 0 and pending_reseal empty on three consecutive reopens, the pack never returns. So doctor is currently telling operators a repair is coming that will never come. sidecar-missing gets the same two-state split and the same wording as verify.\n\nTERRITORY. verify.rs change is surgical - inside verify_seal_identity only, no restructuring, so bn-w5my's additive verify_reg pass merges cleanly. Building now: authority.rs Serving variant plus summary counter, verify.rs split, doctor.rs sidecar-missing wording, tests in mess-cli and mess-store. No engine.rs code change is needed for A, which is the whole point of the finding."}	blake3:Gsbm0N9d2anYocMctYOn4zVC60pD7z9nT3_rNbo29Is
1785702222649474	mess-w-w5my	itc:v3:AWLPPMkzmSZmdAAAAAAAQd72rdq23AAAAAEuBA		item.comment	bn-w5my	{"body":"Done and up for review. Review cr-1m715l in workspace bn-w5my (.maw/workspaces/bn-w5my), commit 3a048f4e, 1 commit, 8 files, 792 insertions. BACKUP: sealed/seg-x.reg joins the cut list beside .pcol and .filter with the same sidecar role and discardable classification; .par stays out. VERIFY: a verify_reg pass mirroring verify_pcol, using RegistryDelta::open for magic, version, flags and crc32c, then accepts_registry_delta against the serving pointer directory (pack over shadowed pidx). Kinds reg-io, reg-corrupt, reg-layout-mismatch at Error (matching pcol-corrupt) and reg-verified at Ok; absence silent everywhere including beside a pack. Spec 07 updated in three places. Tests: backup round trip proves the file rides the cut and the manifest and that the rebuilt store reports registry_delta_admitted 1 with fallback 0, versus 0 and 1 with the delta deleted and identical event counts; ten cases in the new tests/registry_delta_verify.rs; corruption class 6 added to corruption_detection.rs. All mess-cli tests pass, cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean, just fmt-check clean, clippy clean for mess-cli. mess-bench not run."}	blake3:sJGgZZapouErEVXTB053pIQzDV9ksdhiKjusba9Y5Vc
1785702330833814	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExlRI		item.comment	bn-w5my	{"body":"Merged to trunk after review cr-1m715l. Review verified: absence-silent/corruption-reported semantics with tests, pack-segment handling aligned to actual engine fallthrough behavior (a reg beside a pack is read when the pack has no delta section - cross-checked, not skipped), severity matches pcol-corrupt exactly, backup inclusion rationale documented in the policy note that had reserved the decision for this bone. Independent re-run of the two new suites: 17/17."}	blake3:0IWOF2dwpZGJkwPIytP-F-tSQVfTVxtuz6jTwJqVkG4
1785702330850940	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExlhI		item.move	bn-w5my	{"state":"done"}	blake3:wcmoc9mDrD6OdzMvGKtYBmAo8axnkdhic_GCqJor8LM
1785702693297161	mess-w-33l3	itc:v3:AWKSSSczJnnkmTQAAAAAQaqrtre9WsAAAAEyBA		item.comment	bn-33l3	{"body":"MECHANISM NAMED AND REPRODUCED (details in the next comment); writing the fix now. Short version: nextest DOES process-isolate (argv is 'BIN --exact NAME --nocapture', no --test-threads=1), so cross-test pollution is impossible — but process isolation is not THREAD isolation. libtest runs the test on a SPAWNED thread and its harness main thread stays alive in the same process and keeps allocating. /proc/self/task shows exactly 2 threads. Caught it live under memory pressure: phase1 encode delta=1, allocation made by thread name=main, backtrace ending at library/test/src/lib.rs:462 (running_tests.insert, the post-spawn bookkeeping) landing 5.3ms INSIDE the measured window. The counter is a process-global static, so it books the harness allocation against the hot path."}	blake3:CCOfRSdTD-b2dd9Ha-ItJ8OBYdcDGNal1ZYbj4dAq7k
1785702861183874	mess-w-2lir	itc:v3:AWLJ5mSZ55OZJEAAAAAAQde2re9dqgAAAAEwBA		item.comment	bn-2lir	{"body":"MECHANISM NAMED (measured, not argued). Verdict: real cross-process path collision. The bone's 2^-64 premise is wrong by ~16 orders of magnitude.\n\nChain: ident::Id::new() = (31.25ms bucket shifted left 64) OR (fastrand::u128 AND 2^64-1) [ident 2.0.0 src/lib.rs:71-78]. fastrand 2.5.0 seeds its thread-local RNG from random_seed() = DefaultHasher::new() -- SipHash-1-3 with FIXED all-zero keys, NOT RandomState -- hashing exactly Instant::now() (CLOCK_MONOTONIC) and thread::current().id() [src/global_rng.rs:31 and 203-213]. In a freshly exec'd nextest child the ThreadId is a constant (measured ThreadId(1)), so the entire 64-bit random half of the Id is a pure function of ONE monotonic-clock nanosecond reading. Two processes that read the same nanosecond mint the byte-identical Id, and being in the same nanosecond they are necessarily in the same 31.25ms bucket, so the same full /tmp path.\n\nMeasurements on this host: (1) burst-spawn 400,000 processes -> 14 duplicate Ids, and dup-Ids == dup-fastrand-seeds == 14, i.e. every Id collision was a seed collision; (2) matched to this suite, 4000 bursts of 29 simultaneous processes (29 = the tests here that mint a DB) -> 2 bursts collided, about 5e-4 per suite run. Rare enough to be seen once and never reproduced; nowhere near 2^-64 (5.4e-20).\n\nLOCK-TYPE FINDING (the bone asked for this explicitly): rocksdb 8.1.1 uses fcntl(F_SETLK, F_WRLCK), NOT flock and NOT F_OFD_SETLK -- env/fs_posix.cc:95-106, called at :785, and the EAGAIN path formats exactly 'While lock file: PATH: Resource temporarily unavailable' at :786. RocksDB's in-process guard IS real (a path-keyed static std::map locked_files at fs_posix.cc:92, checked at :761-773) but it returns a DIFFERENT error: ENOLCK with 'lock hold by current process, acquire time N acquiring thread M'. Confirmed by direct experiment that POSIX record locks are per-(process,inode) and never self-conflict: a second fd on the same inode in the SAME process takes F_SETLK F_WRLCK successfully, while a child process gets errno 11 EAGAIN = 'Resource temporarily unavailable'. Therefore the observed message PROVES the conflicting holder was a different process.\n\nCandidates disposed of: (1) rstest case expansion sharing a process -- dead: rstest cases do expand into separate #[test]s (see paged_reads...::case_1/2/3 in nextest list) and nextest runs each in its own process; the failing test is not even parameterized. (2) drop ordering -- dead: db.rs drops the DB handle (releasing the fcntl lock) before DB::destroy touches the dir; ordering was already correct. (3) in-process lockedFiles table returning busy -- dead by error message, see above. (4) same-process ident collision -- dead: a same-process second Id::new() advances the same RNG state, and the error message would be ENOLCK anyway.\n\nLANDED (option (a) fix + option (b) instrumentation): rocks::db::test::tmp_db_path() names dirs mess_db-PID-SEQ-Id and creates them with exclusive create_dir. Uniqueness is structural: no two live processes share a pid, no two calls in a process share the seq. The Id is kept only so a recycled pid cannot land on a dir leaked by a dead run. No sleep, no retry anywhere. svc::test_actor::TmpHandle used the same unsafe naming and now shares the helper (29 minting tests total: 22 rocks + 7 svc). Instrumentation: tmp_db_report() renders path, pid, thread, dir contents, open-fd count and this pid's live tmp dirs into any failure message; SelfDestructingDB::drop no longer .unwrap()s DB::destroy (panic-in-drop during unwind aborts the process and discards the original failure) and now reclaims the dir unconditionally.\n\nRegression tests pin both halves of the mechanism. NEGATIVE CONTROL: with the pre-bn-2lir naming restored, tmp_db_paths_differ_under_a_pinned_rng fails with left and right both = /tmp/333rrg-36yctttg-tfxq5d.\n\nBOUNDED REPRO: 200 foreground iterations of the mess_db rocks+svc suite against unfixed trunk, 0 failures, 4m43s. Honest negative and a weak instrument: at 5e-4/run the expectation over 200 runs is 0.1 events, so this does not argue against the mechanism.\n\nTEST EVIDENCE: mess_db 46 tests run: 46 passed, 0 skipped. Full suite 1196 tests run: 1196 passed, 28 skipped. cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean. cargo clippy -p mess_db --all-targets clean. just fmt-check clean.\n\nReview: cr-36v65r (mess-dev). 1 commit: 3eed41a7."}	blake3:K-PKi7T8arC2ZJ_TsSCvOFpGtoYA5DkY3Vp98V705TY
1785702960199541	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExlxI		item.comment	bn-2lir	{"body":"Merged to trunk after review cr-36v65r. CORRECTION TO THIS BONE PREMISE, for the record: the 2^-64 estimate assumed fastrand seeds from RandomState. It does not - fastrand 2.5.0 random_seed() is DefaultHasher (fixed-key SipHash) over Instant::now() plus ThreadId, and ThreadId is constant in fresh nextest children, making the collision rate a function of clock resolution: measured ~5e-4 per suite run on this host. The RocksDB error string (fcntl EAGAIN, not the in-process table ENOLCK message) independently proves a cross-process holder. Fix verified in review at source: pid+seq structural uniqueness, exclusive create_dir, negative-control test. Lead theory from the dispatch brief (in-process table) experimentally refuted by the worker - correctly."}	blake3:HdtMXJ1si4MzHZZIxM_GQ9jJ0GMyLmX6bZyHcxu-yyI
1785702960225414	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExmBI		item.move	bn-2lir	{"state":"done"}	blake3:_-sq1gd64mCsoo4MYafiDTmcvVjduMfYmsCT_AI23cY
1785703153418837	mess-w-3m62	itc:v3:AWLOZJJz-SSTmUAAAAAAQd2qu_qq7QAAAAEwBQ		item.comment	bn-3m62	{"body":"IMPLEMENTED. Review cr-3a6ldz requested from mess-dev. Workspace bn-3m62, one commit (8c192865 after two rebases onto bn-w5my and bn-2lir), 8 files, plus 776 minus 53.\n\nDECISION A AS BUILT. Premise refuted, so nothing was suppressed - there is nothing to suppress. The evidence is in my previous comment; the standing proof is now four tests in mess-store/tests/pack_default_head_artifacts.rs. What DID need fixing was the classification, and it was a live defect rather than cosmetics: authority.rs had three Serving values, so the head fell into LogScan, whose text tells the operator the background sealer is owed a seal - false for a head by design. Added Serving::Unsealed (token unsealed), a per-row sealed boolean, and an unsealed_segments summary counter, discriminated by one 100-byte read_trailer pread per segment. served_by_log_scan now counts only SEALED segments with no usable index, and a healthy pack-default store reports the authority finding at Ok again instead of a permanent Info that masked the real signal.\n\nDECISION B AS BUILT. The exact table now:\n\nseal-pack-reseal-pending, Warn, fields state=pending-reseal converges=next-open expected_identity observed_identity=null fallback=raw-segment-scan segment_id path. Fires when the footer names a pack, the .seal is absent, and the segment has a quarantine slot. Message: footer names SealPack X and the file does not exist, but the segment's quarantine slot under sealed/ is occupied - the re-seal is durably owed (bn-30u/bn-3qh0). The next engine open rebuilds the pack from the log and re-finalizes the footer to name the fresh one; until then the segment is served from the raw log, the only authority (D1). No operator action is required.\n\nseal-pack-missing, Error, unchanged kind and severity, fields state=lost remedy=mess rebuild-index plus the same four. Fires when the footer names a pack, the .seal is absent, and there is NO slot. Message: footer names SealPack X but the file does not exist, and no quarantine slot under sealed/ requests a re-seal - the engine will NOT restore it on its own. Reads stay correct off the raw log (D1); run mess rebuild-index on the store to re-seal the segment.\n\ndoctor sidecar-missing, Warn in both arms, now carries the same state field and either converges=next-open or remedy=mess rebuild-index, with wording matched to verify. It previously told EVERY artifact-less sealed segment it was owed a re-seal, which bn-3qh0's own never_reseals_a_deleted_pack_on_its_own proves false with no marker.\n\nThe owed predicate mirrors engine.rs:4039-4045 exactly - ANY *.refuted parsing to the segment id, not only .seal.refuted - so a .pidx.refuted dragged along by the pack path's shadowed-sidecar withdrawal counts, and the CLI cannot invent a second notion of owed.\n\nWHY THE LOST ARM KEEPS ERROR. It is load-bearing outside verify: restore.rs gates on verify.worst() below Error, spec 07 sections 1 and 1.2 name seal-pack-missing as the Error that makes a cut identity-complete rather than merely data-complete, and backup.rs includes the pack for that reason. Downgrading wholesale would let a torn backup pass silently. The split cannot weaken the gate BY CONSTRUCTION: *.refuted markers are deliberately excluded from a cut, so a restored store never has a slot and a torn cut always lands in the Error arm. a_torn_cut_still_errors pins that, and it withdraws the SOURCE pack first so the test fails loudly if a cut ever starts carrying a marker. Spec 07 section 1.2 now documents the split and that argument.\n\nLAWS. No engine code changed. Nothing suppressed, but the head's empty artifact set is justified by a_cold_open_of_an_unrolled_store_serves_every_event_from_the_log: an unrolled store with an empty sealed/ cold-opens, serves every event and every payload byte off the log, and writes no artifact of its own. Every new message names fallback raw-segment-scan and neither claims data loss. Checked doctor, inspect, verify, backup and restore for a dependence on a head artifact - none exists; doctor and verify already handle the one deliberate case (a seal_active pack over a footerless head) correctly, and both still do.\n\nTESTS. mess-store/tests/pack_default_head_artifacts.rs, 4 new: the live head carries no .pidx/.filter/.pcol/.reg/.seal/.par across live, close and reopen while sealed/ holds exactly one .seal per rolled segment; the head gains exactly one .seal when it rolls and the new head starts empty; cold open of an unrolled store; seal_active writes a pack not a pidx. mess-cli/tests/head_inventory_reseal_severity.rs, 10 new: head classified unsealed not log-scan with its fallback text asserted, healthy store reports authority Ok with the head counted apart, the log-scan signal still fires at Info for a SEALED segment that lost its pack (proving the head was hiding a real signal rather than the signal being removed), both severity arms with severity plus message plus every field pinned, the sibling-slot rule, the backup gate, doctor/verify agreement on an owed re-seal and on a lost pack, and a healthy store silent in both.\n\nTwo tests in doctor_authority.rs now seal the footer before injecting the fault, so they exercise log-scan rather than the new unsealed state. Intent preserved, comments say why.\n\nEVIDENCE. Full workspace nextest after rebasing onto bn-w5my and then bn-2lir: 1222 tests run, 1222 passed, 28 skipped. cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean. cargo +nightly fmt --all --check clean. clippy on mess-cli and mess-store adds no warning in any file I touched.\n\nSURPRISE WORTH RECORDING. verify is completely silent when a loose .pidx disappears - no finding at all - while a disappearing .seal was an Error. The asymmetry is defensible (the footer names the pack and nothing names the .pidx) but it is worth knowing that verify's coverage of missing loose accelerators is zero, if anyone later wants symmetry."}	blake3:sBS1p94LmQE3jIYyKj3_T4RnklEIlFAOQ8TIqx4woqc
1785703262796248	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExmRI		item.comment	bn-3m62	{"body":"Merged to trunk after review cr-3a6ldz. Both flagged judgment calls ratified by lead: (1) the lost arm keeps Error - consistent with pcol-corrupt/reg-corrupt severity and load-bearing for the restore gate at restore.rs:185; (2) Unsealed beats a degraded artifact on a footerless head, with the debris still counted in artifacts_degraded at Warn. Also on record from the worker: verify is silent when a loose .pidx disappears (zero findings) while a lost .seal is Error - defensible under the discardable law since only the pack is footer-named, and doctor now reports sidecar-missing for sealed segments either way; noted here in case anyone later wants verify-side symmetry."}	blake3:UR8nzAkC8AXOQppZ1gKwaJJMDOnB_A3QvxuQRXFTZ3A
1785703262815750	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExmhI		item.comment	bn-3qh0	{"body":"Record correction from bn-3m62: follow-up note 2 on this bone (pack-default stores still write a legacy .pidx for the live head) was WRONG - the pack branch returns into seal_consolidated before the sidecar writer is reachable, and measurement confirms zero head artifacts on pack-default stores. The genuine issue behind the observation was doctor misclassifying heads as LogScan with owed-a-seal text; fixed in bn-3m62."}	blake3:Wnh1OSycbGJrUrScn-hav2ICBQLZrkX7EqUVZLvZxeY
1785703262832470	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExmxI		item.move	bn-3m62	{"state":"done"}	blake3:-A7WP-yCW7YElXN-5usJWdyVOxHFo7830WZTpJAPVrk
1785703304228870	mess-w-33l3	itc:v3:AWKSSSczJnnkmTQAAAAAQaqrtre9WsAAAAEyBQ		item.comment	bn-33l3	{"body":"EVIDENCE 1/2 — MECHANISM. Review cr-cuz0ay to @mess-dev, 1 commit (3d3d25c) in ws bn-33l3. Not merged, not closed.\n\n== 1. IS THE COUNTER PROCESS-GLOBAL OR THREAD-SCOPED? ==\nProcess-global, before this change. crates/mess-log/tests/alloc.rs:28 'static ALLOCS: AtomicUsize', bumped in the GlobalAlloc impl at lines 33-58 (alloc, alloc_zeroed, realloc-grow). One counter for every thread in the process; the test read it with a plain load at lines 95/128/183.\n\n== 2. DOES NEXTEST PROCESS-ISOLATION APPLY? YES — AND IT IS NOT ENOUGH ==\nNo .config/nextest.toml anywhere in the repo, no test-group or threads-required config, so the default profile applies. I printed std::env::args() from inside the test under nextest 0.9.140. The child argv is exactly:\n  [.../target/debug/deps/alloc-f127f621deea1274, --exact, zero_allocs_per_event_on_the_hot_path, --nocapture]\nOne test per process, so CROSS-TEST POLLUTION IS RULED OUT, as the bone predicted. But note what is NOT in that argv: --test-threads=1. libtest therefore runs at concurrency = available_parallelism (64 here) and takes the Concurrent::Yes path, which runs the test body on a SPAWNED thread. Confirmed twice: std::thread::current().name() inside the test is Some(\"zero_allocs_per_event_on_the_hot_path\"), not \"main\"; and /proc/self/task read from inside the test lists exactly two live threads for the whole run — the harness (comm alloc-f127f621d, state S) and the test thread (state R). PROCESS ISOLATION IS NOT THREAD ISOLATION.\n\n== 3. BACKGROUND THREADS FROM THE ENGINE? NO — THE FOREIGN THREAD IS LIBTEST ITSELF ==\nRuled out by reading the constructors, then confirmed by /proc/self/task showing only 2 threads. RealRuntime::new (crates/mess-log/src/runtime/real.rs:113) is a struct literal; RealRuntime only ever creates threads via Runtime::spawn (real.rs:190), which this test never calls. DirectCommitter::with_roll_chained (crates/mess-log/src/committer.rs:2031) is a struct literal too and commit_ordered_group is fully synchronous; the Roller only sends on an std::sync::mpsc channel and owns no thread. mess-testkit contains no thread::spawn at all. Durability::Process maps to Policy { barrier: false } (committer.rs:839), so phase 3 does no fdatasync and never reaches the DegradationAlarm rate-limited eprintln (metrics.rs:351) — that time-dependent log was my leading suspect and it is unreachable here. So there is no tokio runtime, no committer thread, no roll-sealer in this process.\n\nThe only other thread is libtest's harness main thread, and it allocates inside our window. From library/test/src/lib.rs, concurrency-greater-than-1 branch:\n  461  let join_handle = run_test(...)           <- SPAWNS the test thread\n  462  running_tests.insert(id, RunningTest {})  <- first HashMap insert: ALLOCATES\n  463  timeout_queue.push_back(TimeoutEntry {})  <- first VecDeque push: ALLOCATES\nBoth come AFTER the spawn. Measured margin between the harness's last allocation and the test body's first instruction (timestamp taken as the literal first statement of the body), idle host, n=200: min 20.5us, p1 24.4us, p50 45.2us, p90 67.1us, max 102.9us. The harness wins that race by tens of microseconds — until it does not.\n\n== 4. DIRECT REPRODUCTION (the proof, not the theory) ==\nAn instrumented twin of the test (per-thread-partitioned counter plus backtrace capture on any foreign allocation), 300 runs under stress-ng --cpu 40 --vm 8 --vm-bytes 1G --hdd 4 alongside concurrent mess-log suite runs. Iteration 33:\n  diag: mark phase1-window-open t+22us\n  diag: mark phase1-window-close t+28183us\n  diag: phase1 encode delta=1 own=0 foreign=1\n  --- FOREIGN-THREAD alloc at t+5346us from thread id=1 name=main\n        ... hashbrown-0.17.1/src/raw.rs ... std/src/collections/hash/map.rs:1334:19\n        at library/test/src/lib.rs:462:31\n        at library/test/src/console.rs:328:5\ndelta=1 in the phase-1 encoder window — the exact shape of the reported failure — caused by libtest's running_tests.insert landing 5.3ms inside the window. main_allocs_before_body=584 vs main_allocs_total=588: four harness allocations landed after the test body started.\n\nSecond, independent instance of the same class, also captured with a backtrace: hold the counter armed for 65s and at t+59.999s the harness thread allocates 3 more times to print \"has been running for over 60 seconds\" (TEST_WARN_TIMEOUT_S; library/test/src/lib.rs:409/416 and 472). That one cannot be designed around, which is why quiescing the harness before opening the window is not a sound fix.\n\nNEGATIVE RESULT WORTH RECORDING: pure CPU load does not reproduce it and makes it LESS likely. 200 runs at 6150% CPU (128 busy-loop burners) gave 0 foreign allocations, and the margin distribution WIDENS under CPU oversubscription (p50 45us idle to p50 851us at 160 burners) because the spawned thread's startup is delayed more than the harness's two lines are. MEMORY pressure is the discriminating ingredient — stress-ng --vm is what flipped it. That fits the original occurrence: a full 1153-test workspace run with 64 concurrent engine/RocksDB test processes."}	blake3:VJOxxsFoXdO3CO73hDnNrRBH0YeSn9f-N2C7ChEz9WY
1785703328321062	mess-w-33l3	itc:v3:AWKSSSczJnnkmTQAAAAAQaqrtre9WsAAAAEyBg		item.comment	bn-33l3	{"body":"EVIDENCE 2/2 — FIX, TEETH, STATS.\n\n== 5. THE FIX AND WHY THE TEST KEEPS ITS TEETH ==\nCount into a const-initialised, destructor-free thread_local Cell<usize> and assert on the measuring thread's delta. const-init matters: a lazily initialised TLS would allocate on first touch and recurse through the allocator, and Cell<usize> has no Drop so no TLS destructor is registered. The process-wide count is KEPT but never asserted on — it is reported in every failure message so the next reader can separate a real hot-path regression (per-thread delta greater than 0) from harness noise.\n\nTeeth demonstrated by mutation, not claimed:\n- Add one Vec::push per encoded batch to BatchEncoder::encode. FAILS: 'encoder allocated 19999 times over 20000 batches (want 0)'.\n- Disable the thread-local bump so the counter silently returns zero. The new Part 0 canary FAILS: 'the counting allocator did not observe a deliberate allocation on the measuring thread (0 counted) — every zero-alloc assertion in this test would be vacuous'. That is the guard against my own fix hollowing the test out.\n- Revert allocs() to the process-global counter. BOTH tests FAIL, the hot-path one with the original signature: 'encoder allocated 1021 times over 20000 batches (want 0)'.\n\nPlus a new deterministic guard, a_foreign_threads_allocations_are_not_charged_to_this_thread: a helper thread churns 1000 allocations across an open window while this thread does allocation-free work; asserts this thread's delta is 0 AND the process delta is at least 1000 (so the guard cannot pass vacuously either). It converts the flake into a per-run invariant and fails immediately if anyone reverts to a process-global counter.\n\nNot weakened where it matters: the hot paths here are synchronous on the calling thread (SegmentWriter::append is a plain call; DirectCommitter is by construction the owner-only direct seam), and handing per-event work to another thread would itself allocate on THIS thread (the spawn, or the boxed message), so that regression is still caught. All of this is written into the module docs with the libtest line numbers, so the next person does not have to rediscover it.\n\n== 6. VERIFICATION AND ITS HONEST STATISTICAL LIMIT ==\n- 700 runs of the fixed binary with BOTH tests running concurrently in ONE process (--test-threads=2, deliberately harsher than nextest's one-test-per-process isolation, because the guard's helper thread is churning allocations throughout) under the same stress-ng plus concurrent-suite pressure that reproduced the flake: 0 failures.\n- cargo test -p mess-log --test alloc -- --test-threads=2: both pass. Same command with the process-global mutation: both fail. That pair is the cleanest before/after.\n- cargo nextest run -p mess-log: 307 tests run, 307 passed, 11 skipped.\n- cargo check --workspace --all-targets: clean. cargo +nightly fmt --all --check: clean. No mess-bench run (out of scope).\n\nLIMIT, stated plainly. I also ran a 300-vs-300 ABBA A/B of the old binary against the new one under the same pressure and BOTH arms came back 0/300. That A/B proves nothing and I am not claiming it: at an event rate near 1e-3 per run, 300 runs has no power to separate the arms, and the one confirmed reproduction was caught by the higher-sensitivity instrumented twin (which detects a foreign allocation anywhere in the test, a strict superset of the window hits that actually fail the assertion). The load-bearing evidence is therefore the captured backtrace in section 4 — the mechanism identified at file:line while it was happening — and the mutation tests in section 5 showing the fix still bites. The 700-run soak bounds the residual rate at roughly under 1 in 700 under that pressure; it does not prove zero, and no finite soak could. What comes closer to proof is structural: with the counter scoped to one thread, another thread's allocation is not representable in the measured quantity at all.\n\n== SCOPE / HYGIENE ==\ncrates/mess-log/tests/alloc.rs only, 1 file, +170/-13. No production code touched — the hot path never allocated; the harness did. Both temporary diagnostic test files (alloc_diag.rs, alloc_old.rs) were deleted before the commit; encode.rs was restored from backup after the mutation run and git confirms it is unmodified. No retry, no tolerance, no #[ignore]. Load generators were double-bounded (timeout plus finite loop) and I verified 0 stray stress-ng or burner processes afterwards.\n\nHanding off to @mess-dev for review cr-cuz0ay. Bone stays doing, claims held, nothing merged or pushed."}	blake3:-SPa6UAb3OVcZLup89GRB32tVJAy7iuwCnwVd0Jt4NE
1785703419178427	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExnBI		item.comment	bn-33l3	{"body":"Merged to trunk after review cr-cuz0ay. Mechanism: libtest post-spawn bookkeeping observed by a process-global counter under memory pressure - named with a captured backtrace (test/src/lib.rs:462 via hash/map.rs), not inferred. Lead brief theory (engine background threads) correctly ruled out at source by the worker. Fix keeps the mechanism-test teeth: three mutations fail as required, and foreign-thread allocations are now structurally unrepresentable in the asserted quantity. Host verified clean of load generators after the soak."}	blake3:rZq9xg3TWyUlGQqWOF1K_GPSqzS5DCr281mi53T_4Wg
1785703419224222	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExnRI		item.move	bn-33l3	{"state":"done"}	blake3:HbvyHfAKA6M0AGLrWH-scuQVJwIDLNbpL427C4kYGlk
1785706684262482	mess-w-3odg	itc:v3:AWKeTJOTknJ8klAAAAAAQb1q66uvqoAAAAExAQ		item.move	bn-3odg	{"state":"doing"}	blake3:wjBjIBTlWsa4xtyACtI8Gjwm4ctAIC9Ykl7sn9k3dlM
1785706684265242	mess-w-3odg	itc:v3:AWKeTJOTknJ8klAAAAAAQb1q66uvqoAAAAExAg		item.assign	bn-3odg	{"action":"assign","agent":"mess-w-3odg"}	blake3:nMfVZng2vruoSQF5Scrl88hjfQ5MtWmLch1n4GPbqb4
1785706748660807	mess-w-3odg	itc:v3:AWKeTJOTknJ8klAAAAAAQb1q66uvqoAAAAExAw		item.comment	bn-3odg	{"body":"Start: bn-3odg premise check underway. Workspace .maw/workspaces/bn-3odg created from main. Plan per brief: (1) precondition grep for order-sensitive iteration of the ActiveIndex streams map, (2) microbench sip-vs-fold at 1k/32k/250k stream ids in the real access shape, (3) composed ABBA A/B on mess-bench --only live_tail and --only reader_contention with a trunk-vs-trunk null control, (4) decision table + verdict under the pre-registered rule (adopt only if the composed engine can see the win)."}	blake3:dTYRlF0TBak-J9VU9y5dY4zZvoPdwZH2kLA-dU8qlV0
1785707676753214	mess-w-3odg	itc:v3:AWKeTJOTknJ8klAAAAAAQb1q66uvqoAAAAExBA		item.comment	bn-3odg	{"body":"## bn-3odg premise measurement -- VERDICT: DECLINE\n\nPre-registered rule: adopt only if the composed engine can see the win.\nThe map-level win is real and large. The composed engine cannot see it, by\nabout a factor of 40 against the noise floor. No production conversion ships.\n\n### 0. Precondition check (run before any measurement) -- PASSES\n\nThe streams map is never iterated order-sensitively. Every access in\ncrates/mess-index/src/active.rs is a get/entry by key\n(stream_head:328, resolve:345, stream_entries:364, stream_entries_from:389,\napply_committed:290). The sole iteration is ActiveIndex::snapshot (active.rs\n459-467), which collects into a BTreeMap<u64, Vec<StreamEntry>> -- order\nindependent by construction, and the test\nsnapshot_is_deterministic_regardless_of_shards already pins that. Every\nordered product comes from the global Vec, not this map. The field is private\nto a private struct, so there is no out-of-module access at all (verified by\ngrep across crates/). The bn-dcr DoS argument transfers verbatim: interned\nu64 ids, attacker picks names and never ids.\n\nSo the swap is admissible. It is just not worth making.\n\n### 1. Framing finding: which composed metrics can even touch this map\n\nCounted before measuring, because it decides what the composed A/B can\npossibly show:\n\n  read_global_page -> ActiveIndex::global_range (engine.rs:5441) reads the\n    global Vec. The streams map is NOT touched. That is live_tail passes 1-3:\n    catchup_solo, catchup_loaded, catchup_scaling, writer_tailed,\n    tail_reader, append_to_delivery_us.\n  read_stream -> ActiveIndex::stream_entries_from (engine.rs:5350). ONE map\n    lookup per page of 256 events. That is live_tail pass 4, stream_paging.\n  reader_contention readers call Backend::head (engine.rs:5306 -> book.heads)\n    and stream_id_of (engine.rs:4559 -> book.registry). Neither touches the\n    active index at all. Its only exposure is the writer's apply_committed.\n\nAlso: live_tail Full runs 32 + 8 gap = 40 streams and reader_contention Full\nruns 64, over 64 shards. Per-shard maps hold 0-2 keys. The reference suite\nhas no workload that puts a meaningful number of keys in this map.\n\n### 2. MICRO -- map level, the real access pattern (the win is real)\n\nHarness: a faithful copy of the ActiveIndex hot code generic over the hasher\n(64 shards, fibonacci shard_for, parking_lot::RwLock per shard, the same\napply_committed insert path and partition_point reads), three arms in ONE\nbinary: A = std SipHash-1-3, F = foldhash::fast, N = a second independently\nmonomorphised SipHash arm as NULL CONTROL. Palindromic ABBA per rep\n(A F N N F A). Writes are per-group apply_committed bursts of 64 batches\n(frame_count 1..8, streams drawn uniformly); reads are stream_entries_from\nwith page 256 plus resolve plus stream_head, 200k probes. N=15 reps, p50 of\nper-rep ns/op, TWO independent runs. Scratch example binary, deleted.\n\napply_committed, ns per batch (p50):\n\n  shape                    sip R1/R2      fold R1/R2     fold%  R1/R2   null% R1/R2\n  40 streams (live_tail)   15.90/17.69    13.22/13.97    +16.8/+21.0    +1.4/+2.4\n  64 streams (reader_cont) 18.47/20.26    13.67/15.67    +26.0/+22.7    +3.8/-2.3\n  1k streams               27.05/29.36    20.43/22.41    +24.5/+23.7    -4.0/+2.2\n  7k streams (social lg)   33.48/40.19    22.21/27.47    +33.7/+31.6    -2.5/-0.2\n  32k streams              50.88/60.23    37.02/41.39    +27.2/+31.3    +2.2/+0.0\n  250k streams            127.17/129.64   82.35/84.83    +35.2/+34.6    +0.2/+1.3\n\nread probe (stream_entries_from + resolve + stream_head), ns per probe (p50):\n\n  shape                    sip R1/R2      fold R1/R2     fold%  R1/R2   null% R1/R2\n  40 streams (live_tail)  308.67/351.42  286.05/329.63    +7.3/+6.2     +2.3/+0.1\n  64 streams (reader_cont)299.49/350.12  276.34/325.89    +7.7/+6.9     +2.2/-0.7\n  1k streams              301.14/332.03  272.60/300.99    +9.5/+9.3     -0.3/+4.3\n  7k streams (social lg)  340.02/410.95  339.91/390.38    +0.0/+5.0     -3.0/-4.5\n  32k streams             227.19/271.43  197.70/243.55   +13.0/+10.3    -1.0/-2.0\n  250k streams            308.29/311.34  280.99/279.57    +8.9/+10.2    -0.0/-0.9\n\nMicro null band: +/-4.5%. Insert wins (+17..35%) clear it decisively in both\nruns and at every size. Read wins (+5..13%) clear it everywhere except the\n7k read cell in R1 (+0.0%, inside the band; R2 gave +5.0%). Direction is\nconsistent; magnitude grows with keys per shard, exactly as expected.\nMinimums track the p50s (250k insert min 113.68 sip vs 76.34 fold in R1), so\nthis is not a tail artefact.\n\nLoadavg(1m) at micro run boundaries: R1 5.49 -> 5.12, R2 3.87 -> 3.94."}	blake3:7zufW5sKdBE4NdzHXwTsUadqXCZqkaCqLfgqe1nA0sY
1785707676773882	mess-w-3odg	itc:v3:AWKeTJOTknJ8klAAAAAAQb1q66uvqoAAAAExBQ		item.comment	bn-3odg	{"body":"bn-3odg decision table, part 2 of 2 (composed A/B, arithmetic ceiling, decision).\n\n### 3. COMPOSED -- mess-bench, the suite's own instruments\n\nThree release binaries built BEFORE any measured cell, no builds during:\n  A  = trunk\n  B  = trunk + the foldhash conversion (type alias + DoS doc comment\n       mirroring bn-dcr, Shard::streams retyped)\n  A2 = trunk rebuilt in the other worktree = NULL CONTROL.\nA and A2 came out byte-identical (same sha256), so the null control is pure\nrun-to-run noise; it does not price code-layout luck, which is a caveat on\nthe band's lower edge, not on the verdict.\n\nSchedule per workload: 8 blocks; each block runs A B B A, then the null\ncontrol N1 N2 N2 N1 (N1 = A binary, N2 = A2 binary) in the SAME time window.\n16 samples per arm per metric. Corpus in ~/.cache/mess-bench/bn-3odg, deleted\nafter. flock held on the shared global-measurement.lock. No background load\ngenerators were started at any point.\n\nlive_tail (loadavg over the 512 cells: min 3.11, p50 4.59, max 5.57):\n\n  metric                          A p50        B p50     B vs A    null    verdict\n  catchup_solo.ev_per_s        7.591e6      7.587e6      -0.05%   -0.34%   in band\n  catchup_loaded.ev_per_s      1.520e7      1.512e7      -0.53%   +2.47%   in band\n  catchup_scaling                 2.01        1.986      -1.21%   +2.84%   in band\n  writer_tailed.ev_per_s       1.129e7      1.107e7      -1.96%   -5.62%   in band\n  writer_retention              0.5687       0.5585      -1.79%   -8.35%   in band\n  tail_reader.ev_per_s         1.256e7      1.215e7      -3.26%   -5.30%   in band\n  append_to_delivery_us          12.88        13.37      +3.81%   +4.35%   in band\n  stream_paging.ev_per_s       1.354e7      1.390e7      +2.64%   -0.96%   in band\n\nreader_contention (loadavg over the 320 cells: min 4.27, p50 4.27, max 4.41):\n\n  metric                          A p50        B p50     B vs A    null    verdict\n  writer_solo.ev_per_s         2.126e7      2.147e7      +0.97%   -4.49%   in band\n  writer_loaded.ev_per_s       2.232e7      2.110e7      -5.48%   -5.56%   in band\n  writer_retention               1.034        1.010      -2.39%   -4.94%   in band\n  head_reads_per_s             3.566e6      3.414e6      -4.26%   +4.24%   in band\n  resolve_per_s                3.569e6      3.407e6      -4.53%   +6.43%   in band\n\n13 of 13 metrics inside the null band. Per-arm coefficient of variation is\n6-11% on nearly everything (append_to_delivery_us is far worse, 22-102%). The\nband is +/-5..8%. Nothing here is a signal in either direction -- the largest\napparent B \"loss\", writer_loaded at -5.48%, is matched almost exactly by the\nnull control's -5.56% on a byte-identical binary.\n\nHonest caveat: 1-min loadavg sat at 3.1-5.6 during the composed cells,\nabove the ~3 ceiling the discipline sets (a neighbouring project was\nspawning governance checks; nothing of mine). That inflates the band. It\ndoes not change the verdict, because of the next section.\n\n### 4. Arithmetic ceiling -- why the verdict does not depend on the noise\n\nTake the micro savings at face value and divide by what the engine actually\nspends per unit of the same work, using the measured A p50s above:\n\n  path                      saving/op   engine cost/op   ceiling\n  apply_committed insert     4.80 ns    94.07 us/batch   0.0051%\n    (reader_contention: 2000 ev/batch at 2.126e7 ev/s)\n  apply_committed insert     3.72 ns    88.60 us/batch   0.0042%\n    (live_tail burst: 1000 ev/batch at 1.129e7 ev/s)\n  stream_entries_from       22.62 ns    18.91 us/page    0.1196%\n    (live_tail paging: 256 ev/page at 1.354e7 ev/s; the whole 3-lookup\n     probe delta charged to the ONE lookup a page does, i.e. generous)\n  global_range paths             0 ns   n/a              0%\n    (catchup, tail_reader, append_to_delivery: streams map not touched)\n  head / stream_id_of            0 ns   n/a              0%\n    (Book registry and heads, not the active index)\n\nBest case anywhere in the composed suite: 0.12%. The noise floor is 40x\nthat. Same number from the other direction as bn-3hm4's finding that the\nwhole ActiveIndex is 0.5% of engine time: a 25% win on 0.5% is 0.125%.\n\n### 5. Decision\n\nDECLINE, per the pre-registered rule and the bn-2ig9 precedent. The micro\nwin is real, reproducible and comfortably outside its own null band; the\ncomposed delta is inside the null band on every metric, and the arithmetic\nceiling says it always would be. Direction and magnitude of the composed\neffect: too close to call, and bounded above at 0.12% even if it were\nmeasurable.\n\nWhat shipped: nothing behavioural. The conversion was built, ran 172/172\nmess-index tests green, was measured, and was reverted. The commit is\ncomment-only -- a note on Shard::streams recording the numbers and the\nreopen condition (a workload that makes the map the cost: many more streams\nper shard, or a read path that probes far more often per materialised\nevent), so the next reader finds the measurement instead of redoing it.\n\nChecks: just fmt-check clean; cargo doc -p mess-index shows no new warnings.\nThe final diff is doc-comment-only, so per the brief the package tests and\nworkspace check were not re-run against it -- they were run against the\nfuller conversion state and were green (cargo test -p mess-index --release:\n172 passed, 0 failed).\n\nReview: cr-1mink3 (reviewer mess-dev), workspace bn-3odg, commit d53c79c2."}	blake3:Me-MmpomUggrIZlxd4zQiGDwUN1bIgIvjMnaX0_MoGo
1785707697399642	mess-w-3odg	itc:v3:AWKeTJOTknJ8klAAAAAAQb1q66uvqoAAAAExBg		item.comment	bn-3odg	{"body":"Review created: cr-1mink3 in workspace bn-3odg (.maw/workspaces/bn-3odg), commit d53c79c2, reviewer mess-dev. Diff is comment-only (28 added doc-comment lines in crates/mess-index/src/active.rs)."}	blake3:NxVUe15s3v0CidSn9s2cGN4mBssA2ZabBrJnnYCuC44
1785707832371336	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExnhI		item.comment	bn-3odg	{"body":"DECLINED and merged (measurement record only, no behavioral change). Fifth consecutive premise-check kill. Review verified the discipline: three prebuilt binaries, palindromic ABBA with trunk-rebuild null (byte-identical, so pure temporal noise), loadavg recorded per cell with the deviation honestly flagged (neighboring continuum governance checks, 3.1-5.6) and absorbed by the interleaved null; the arithmetic ceiling makes the verdict independent of the inflated band. Two brief corrections from the worker on record: reader_contention reads never touch this map (head and stream_id_of go through book.heads/book.registry), and the reference suite runs only 40-64 stream ids over 64 shards, so composed sensitivity to this map is structurally tiny - which is itself part of why DECLINE is right. Reopen condition lives in the active.rs doc comment."}	blake3:696PvPSPK-Af04vIo3Zf0BxiQ6OMWpUmmv4oVAmaoes
1785707832391753	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExnxI		item.move	bn-3odg	{"state":"done"}	blake3:EIm9eNzCRLOKhiofKyKmo7kgKmQRQtN_V6H6Pw5NiMY
1785708014939894	mess-w-2gns	itc:v3:AWLnJmZJn5nJMQAAAAAAQe621b9urAAAAAEvAQ		item.move	bn-2gns	{"state":"doing"}	blake3:sJXLwxCzZGQnLuJCFvBVN_20D3ItMev17llLT-2FfOs
1785708014945961	mess-w-2gns	itc:v3:AWLnJmZJn5nJMQAAAAAAQe621b9urAAAAAEvAg		item.assign	bn-2gns	{"action":"assign","agent":"mess-w-2gns"}	blake3:bEOuWlNteZg_EEtjG81EmvT4Khmc76UVk4OP247YRK4
1785708023631377	mess-w-2gns	itc:v3:AWLnJmZJn5nJMQAAAAAAQe621b9urAAAAAEvAw		item.comment	bn-2gns	{"body":"Starting bn-2gns as mess-w-2gns. Claims staked, workspace bn-2gns created from main. Reading ADR 0002 section 1, the bn-ozi5/bn-3l8n records incl. the cr-2ywo0t equal-coverage ruling, pack_snapshot/{format,sidecar,mod}.rs, and every Snapshottable/SnapshotStore implementor and caller before designing. Plan: stable aggregate/schema + codec identity on Snapshottable, public SnapshotCompatibility/SnapshotCoverage over the internal Coverage lattice, decide LWW vs ADR conflict rule explicitly, pinned-root bounded scan_snapshots, and check whether the fold-chain precondition for CertifiedSnapshotRef exists before building anything for it."}	blake3:yVgfZRQQenIkKC4FWjVHiLbeD9fE7dOqaVR_-plQQa4
1785710890606928	mess-w-2gns	itc:v3:AWLnJmZJn5nJMQAAAAAAQe621b9urAAAAAEvBA		item.comment	bn-2gns	{"body":"bn-2gns ready for review: cr-1o5uka (reviewer mess-dev, NOT self-approved), workspace bn-2gns, 1 commit 37e1e8e9. The five items and their decisions (part 1/2).\n\n1. STABLE IDENTITY (the break). SnapshotCompatibility { aggregate_schema_id, fold_version, codec_id, codec_version } over StableSnapshotId: a Copy, 1..=64-byte, lowercase-ASCII-plus-.-_:/ identifier with ONE canonical encoding (no case folding, no normalization), built by a const fn whose validation runs at compile time. Snapshottable declares AGGREGATE_SCHEMA_ID + FOLD_VERSION; CODEC_ID defaults to the schema id and CODEC_VERSION to 1. Stream name plus compatibility is the complete lookup key, so a fold bump no longer overwrites the old head, it writes a NEW one, and neither identity can hide or delete the other.\nCollision diagnosis: register_snapshot_identity runs first thing in every save and every accelerated load; a second Rust type claiming an identity gets StoreError::SnapshotIdentity naming both types. The key is the COMPLETE compatibility, not the schema id alone, deliberately: the harm is a shared head key, and two types sharing a schema id at different fold versions are exactly what a fold bump looks like from inside one process (Counter/CounterV2 in the deploy suites). Flagging those would make honest deploy modelling impossible while catching nothing. TypeId keys the registry as a runtime diagnostic, never persisted. The conflict carries two 64-byte ids, so StoreError boxes it (measured: StoreError hit 184 bytes unboxed).\n\n2. OLD-SNAPSHOT DISPOSITION: miss and rebuild, stated explicitly, no migration. RECORD_FORMAT and ROOT_FORMAT both bump to 2. An older sidecar's ROOTS do not decode, so it resolves to zero heads -- not a per-record miss, the whole thing is invisible. Tested three ways: frozen v1 record-body bytes never decode; frozen v1 root bytes never decode; and a_pre_identity_sidecar_misses_cleanly_and_is_never_touched plants a complete internally-consistent v1 sidecar (valid IDENTITY, pack header, commit-framed record, CRC-correct root) then asserts (a) a read-only open misses with Absent and leaves the directory byte-identical with no LOCK created, (b) a writer also misses and full-replays, (c) a new-scheme save then hits, (d) every planted artifact survives untouched.\n\n3. EQUAL COVERAGE: UPGRADED to the ADR rule, per the cr-2ywo0t ruling that transferred it here. publication_decision() is one shared function used by both the sidecar and MockBackend: higher supersedes, lower is CoverageRegressed, equal reads and fully validates the current record FIRST -> invalid = Repaired, identical = Idempotent, different = Conflict with the current record retained. Two reasons beyond the ruling: the seam can finally report it (save_snapshot returns SnapshotSaveOutcome -- the absence of that was bn-ozi5's stated reason for LWW), and LWW was hiding a real bug class (a non-deterministic fold, or a state encoding changed without a codec_version bump), which now surfaces as Conflict plus a SidecarMetrics::conflicts counter instead of two answers alternating. Nothing depended on LWW once heads are compat-keyed. The current record is read ONLY on an exact-coverage collision (tested)."}	blake3:h8XUH0Ar_UcHwkaromk6pf46_lnLpliIQenEBKdokTU
1785710919718426	mess-w-2gns	itc:v3:AWLnJmZJn5nJMQAAAAAAQe621b9urAAAAAEvBQ		item.comment	bn-2gns	{"body":"bn-2gns part 2/2.\n\n4. BOUNDED SCAN: Sidecar::pin_root() -> Option<PinnedSnapshotRoot> and Sidecar::scan(pin, cursor, limit) -> SnapshotScanPage. Deliberately NOT on the SnapshotStore trait: enumeration is a property of a store that HAS a discovery root, its only callers hold that store concretely, and putting it on the seam would force MockBackend to fake a root. The vocabulary (PinnedSnapshotRoot, SnapshotScanCursor/Key/Entry/Page/Diagnostic, MAX_SNAPSHOT_SCAN_LIMIT) is public in mess_store::snapshot so every implementation says the same thing. Cursors bind their root id and are Rejected against any other -- a foreign store, a foreign pin, or the SAME store one generation later -- never reinterpreted, which is the fail-closed signal a destructive caller needs. Pages are capped, every entry is validated (frame read, leaf binding checked, record decoded), and a head that does not resolve is skipped and counted as Partial; only Complete licenses destruction. Real consumer wired: mess_cli::metaread pages 512 at a time over one pinned root, re-pins on rejection with a bounded attempt count, and reports MetaFacts::degraded. Cursor serialization was deliberately NOT built (no consumer -- paging happens inside one command); the ADR's checksum arrives with the wire form, documented as such.\nHonest cost, in code and in docs/snapshots.md: pin_root is O(1), but the thing it pins is v1's FLAT root, so a pin borrows an O(N) resident list rather than walking an O(height) page path, and the sidecar now holds each head twice (HashMap for O(1) point lookup, sorted Vec for scanning). ADR 0002's COW tree removes both; no signature changes when it does.\n\n5. CertifiedSnapshotRef: DEFERRED. Precondition checked and absent. mess-log has the real fold chain and certificates::load_verified, but that module owns the algorithm over an in-memory StreamCert view and its own header says wiring mess-store's SnapshotRef and on-disk reads into it is a dependent bone. Concretely: nothing can produce the canonical event-prefix certificate h[v] for a (stream, coverage) at the snapshot layer -- Backend exposes no chain accessor and mess-store's only fold_chain contact is an observability boolean. A record claiming certification without both semantic hashes would be a lie; the variant with nothing able to populate it would be a stub. So SnapshotTrust ships with one variant and #[non_exhaustive], which is the whole forward-compatibility provision: adding CertifiedSnapshotRef { state_hash, event_prefix_hash } later is not a source break, so this deferral costs no second trait break. The record format already carries the trust byte and an unknown value is a miss.\n\nSOCIAL BEFORE/AFTER. before: const FOLD_VERSION: u32 = 1; (one line). after: AGGREGATE_SCHEMA_ID = StableSnapshotId::new(social.user), CODEC_ID = snapshot_codec::CODEC_ID, CODEC_VERSION = snapshot_codec::CODEC_VERSION, FOLD_VERSION = 1. The codec lines are not boilerplate, they are the feature: all four aggregates serialize through one shared hand-rolled format, so social names it once (social.length-prefixed) and can version its wire shape for all four without touching a single FOLD_VERSION, and vice versa. An aggregate that does not share a codec omits both lines and takes the defaults -- exactly one added line versus before.\n\nPUBLIC SURFACE ADDED (mess_store): StableSnapshotId(+Error), SnapshotCompatibility, SnapshotCoverage, SnapshotTrust, Hash256, SnapshotRef (reshaped to compatibility/coverage/trust/stream_id; BlobPtr and the two reserved Option<Hash256> fields deleted), SnapshotLookup<T>, SnapshotMiss, SnapshotSaveOutcome, CurrentHead, publication_decision, SnapshotIdentityConflict, register_snapshot_identity, SnapshotSave, SnapshotRootId, the five scan types, MAX_SNAPSHOT_SCAN_LIMIT, clamp_scan_limit. Every public item has a doc comment stating its law. pack_snapshot::Coverage is gone -- the public SnapshotCoverage replaced it in the format, the sidecar and the seam. No fjall vocabulary anywhere in the new surface.\n\nLAWS KEPT GREEN AND EXTENDED: read-only opens create nothing (byte-for-byte directory compare, the new pre-identity case, both golden v3/v4 steps); corruption/absence/unknown format = miss never error; snapshot+tail == full replay over 25x300 prefixes; fjall-era golden sidecars still miss safely. New: sequential AND concurrent Empty<->Through(0) both directions (64 rounds); equal-coverage repair/idempotent/conflict at the sidecar and at the seam; identity collision at registration and at first save/load; 2000-head paged scan with per-page bounds and strict key ordering; foreign and stale cursor rejection; corrupt-head Partial; pin-holds-its-view.\n\nEVIDENCE: cargo nextest -p mess-store (MESS_SNAPSHOT_LAW_ITERS=25) 328 passed 5 skipped; -p mess-cli 108 passed 3 skipped; -p social 122 passed 3 skipped; cargo test -p mess-store --doc 2 passed; cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean; just fmt-check clean; clippy on mess-store/mess-cli adds no warning from this change; cargo doc -p mess-store --no-deps has no warning in the touched files. No mess-bench runs.\n\nSURPRISES for the reviewer: (i) the deploy suites forced the registry criterion -- Counter/CounterV2 are one aggregate at two folds in ONE process, so a schema-id-only check would have been unimplementable alongside honest deploy tests; (ii) a byte-search corruption test flaked because the pack header's 16 random identity bytes can contain the search byte and a reader never validates that header -- both such tests now match the full state pattern; (iii) StoreError silently grew to 184 bytes before I boxed the conflict, worth watching if anyone adds another wide variant."}	blake3:4HsQOb-3E6Bz6arG1Hqr0CulrIscmGjWhMDQVSDe3xo
1785711048635964	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExoBI		item.comment	bn-2gns	{"body":"Merged to trunk after review cr-1o5uka. Lead rulings: (1) LWW upgrade to the ADR conflict rule RATIFIED - the cr-2ywo0t ruling was v1-scoped by its own terms and both named blockers (no outcome channel, fold bumps needing overwrite) are gone; the conflicts surface a real bug class (non-deterministic folds, codec changes without a version bump). (2) Collision keying on the COMPLETE compatibility rather than schema id alone RATIFIED - the guarded harm is the shared head key, cross-fold coexistence is what a deploy looks like, and heads keyed by complete compatibility make it safe by construction. (3) CertifiedSnapshotRef deferral confirmed - precondition verified absent, non_exhaustive trust enum means no second source break. Enforcement verified live at store.rs save and load entry points, not merely available."}	blake3:RquQden3UM9Fu7EIAoZYMXJfK08O_3LowqmVAhUQPEI
1785711048662835	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExoRI		item.move	bn-2gns	{"state":"done"}	blake3:u9yKS957COGA6BkZGJc_up4dusUbUJn11vHYoUX3Xqg
1785715622414518	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExohI		item.create	bn-3jqg	{"description":"Bob wants: (1) verify examples/ still work at main, (2) benchmark the social example at main, (3) benchmark the same cells at the last commit where Fjall was the live snapshot-discovery engine (29b23266, parent of the bn-3l8n adoption), (4) a comparison table. The delta spans every engine change since 29b23266 - pack snapshot sidecars, Fjall deletion, .reg registry deltas, the SealPack default flip, wave 1-3 - so frame it as engine evolution anchored at the fjall era, not an isolated fjall-vs-pack A/B; list the major perf merges between the two commits alongside the table. Measurement discipline per house memory: both binaries built before any measured cell, ABBA interleave, same-binary null control, loadavg recorded per cell, fresh store dir per run, corpora under ~/.cache/mess-bench cleaned after.","kind":"task","title":"Benchmark the social example: main vs the fjall-era engine (29b23266)"}	blake3:df5iO2mGJaspAZX7fufcQIXvjjoCyLCLb7gegAcjCrE
1785715684145092	mess-w-3jqg	itc:v3:AWKTJkkk5kk5OSSUAAAAQa1qq7VddVQAAAE2AQ		item.move	bn-3jqg	{"state":"doing"}	blake3:9zGAqExHiEgXCiTzucAP3su2To9K_urr0-eow_ZalSg
1785715684147858	mess-w-3jqg	itc:v3:AWKTJkkk5kk5OSSUAAAAQa1qq7VddVQAAAE2Ag		item.assign	bn-3jqg	{"action":"assign","agent":"mess-w-3jqg"}	blake3:jaFfauUIyBPiWTFEe2RXLCJqWvLh0bSw7qnZME6yg8I
1785715753193526	mess-w-3jqg	itc:v3:AWKTJkkk5kk5OSSUAAAAQa1qq7VddVQAAAE2Aw		item.comment	bn-3jqg	{"body":"START (mess-w-3jqg). Plan: Phase 1 verify examples at main (cargo test -p social, -p bank, build local_db + social bins release, social-seed demo smoke + social-web --rebuild proof). Phase 2 cells: seed-large (wall), rebuild-from-zero + warm-resume (both read out of social-web --rebuild, which reports from0_build and resume_build in one process), store-footprint (du + per-family file counts). Old arm built in a detached worktree at /home/bob/.cache/mess-bench/fjall-ab-wt (29b23266); trunk never leaves main. Verified the example is semantically identical across the two commits: only the backend type differs (FjallSnapshotBackend at .snapshots vs PackSnapshotBackend at .snapshots.packs) plus the bn-2gns snapshot-identity constants. Same CLI flags, same generator, same corpus."}	blake3:obFM3q7izWu3D8hi7abAI1aPWCGqo9LOCc_7rDWMe0Q
1785717022604483	mess-w-3jqg	itc:v3:AWKTJkkk5kk5OSSUAAAAQa1qq7VddVQAAAE2BA		item.comment	bn-3jqg	{"body":"[bn-3jqg report 1/3]\n\nRESULT (mess-w-3jqg) -- examples verified at main, then an A/B of the social example: today's main vs the fjall-era engine.\n\nWHAT THE DELTA CONTAINS (read this before the table)\nThis is NOT an isolated fjall-vs-pack A/B. It spans 24 commits, 29b23266..HEAD:\n  9764012f chore: bones\n  6e61df89 feat: snapshot coverage/compatibility public API - ADR 0002 trait break (bn-2gns)\n  b2bef908 chore: plain comment on thread_local macro invocation in alloc test (bn-33l3)\n  5fe6cb44 docs: measure and decline foldhash for the active-index streams map (bn-3odg)\n  10be5820 test: scope the zero-alloc counter to the measuring thread (bn-33l3)\n  91e9a259 fix: honest head-segment classification and owed-reseal severity split (bn-3m62)\n  cd2f7a32 fix: structurally collision-free temp DB paths in mess_db tests (bn-2lir)\n  4d09b47b feat: .reg registry-delta sidecars ride the backup cut and get a verify pass (bn-w5my)\n  315b0b5e feat: rebuild-index pack path via quarantine plus engine re-seal (bn-3qh0)\n  57c64374 feat: composed-engine observability surface and doctor authority section (bn-11ba)\n  57485425 feat: mess-bench live-tail / global-position read workload (bn-1r9c)\n  ef02c03c docs: ADR 0003 declines the v4 commit-capsule format (bn-1ojm)\n  d473744a feat: enable SealPack by default (bn-ccx1)\n  9fd5a9c0 perf: emit and consume REGISTRY_DELTA in SealPack (bn-3h64)\n  7dc8e85e fix: teach mess-cli the .seal and .refuted namespaces (bn-1w4h)\n  e1dfbfd8 perf: install just-sealed packs via the lazy attach (bn-2t2e)\n  5d445b4c feat: bind exact SealPack identity into the segment footer (bn-11g)\n  e2570a31 fix: sealed-directory chooser compares bytes, not Spike H lookup crossover (bn-we9x)\n  d893cd5f perf: eliminate SealPack whole-pack reopen residency (bn-dbz)\n  fe13ac86 fix: refuted sealed candidates converge to safe re-seal (bn-30u)\n  dd70fe47 perf: foldhash for the sealed-directory map (bn-dcr)\n  3dbac816 perf: per-segment registry deltas in a .reg sidecar cut cold open 38x (bn-26pp)\n  1b462430 feat: delete MetaStore, FjallSnapshotBackend, and the fjall dependency (bn-fj34)\n  a5d0bed0 feat: adopt the pack snapshot sidecar across apps and tooling (bn-3l8n)\nbn-bka2 (lazy .pcol attach) is already IN the old arm - it is 29b23266 itself.\n\nIMPORTANT: most of that chain is INERT on this corpus. The social large corpus is\n17.2 MB in ONE 256 MiB preallocated segment that never rolls, so it produces zero\nsealed segments, zero .reg sidecars and zero .pcol sidecars (verified by file count,\nsee footprint below). The SealPack chain (bn-11g/dbz/30u/dcr/we9x/2t2e/3h64/ccx1),\nthe bn-26pp .reg cold-open win and the bn-bka2 lazy .pcol attach therefore contribute\nnothing measurable here - neither cost nor benefit. What this A/B actually measures is\nthe SNAPSHOT SIDECAR swap: FjallSnapshotBackend at .snapshots vs PackSnapshotBackend at\n.snapshots.packs, plus the bn-2gns identity constants.\n\nSETUP\n  main arm : 9764012fc296c6455e559a6f996bf5cb58733c16 (repo root, never left main)\n  old arm  : 29b2326684e40fb22d467c0c223a9a7ef5b42663 (detached worktree, removed after)\n  null arm : byte-identical copy of the main binaries (md5 verified), run in the SAME\n             interleave so the noise band is measured under identical conditions\n  rustc 1.97.0 (2d8144b78 2026-07-07), cargo 1.97.0, release profile, both arms built\n  fully before any measured cell. AMD Ryzen Threadripper 9970X (64 threads), ext4/NVMe.\n  Interleave: palindromic unit [main old null null old main], repeated.\n  Corpus: social-seed --scale large --seed 1337 -> 54,602 commands, 108,810 events\n  folded (read watermark), 17,175,832 bytes of log - byte-identical at both commits.\n  Fresh store dir per seed run; the rebuild corpora are pre-seeded and pre-checkpointed\n  once per arm by an identical procedure (seed, serve, SIGINT to write the shutdown\n  checkpoint at head) and are read-only for --rebuild, which never writes.\n  All 102 rebuild runs resumed from a REAL checkpoint at position 108810 - no vacuous\n  from-0-vs-from-0 comparisons.\n\nLOADAVG (1-min, recorded per run): min 3.32, p50 8.83, max 13.23 over 300 measured runs.\nThis is ABOVE the under-3 target. The box is shared with other agents (clippy, java,\nllama-server) and never settled below 3 for more than a few minutes despite a 10-minute\npause. 64 threads means load 9 is roughly 14 percent utilisation, and the palindromic\ninterleave plus the concurrently-measured null arm are what protect the comparison:\nevery band below was produced under exactly the same background load as the deltas\nbeside it. Treat any delta inside the band as no difference and do not over-read the\nabsolute numbers."}	blake3:X72wuseRuR6X7wLfJN-VNhg-BwkQWS6BvCF800KbWwA
1785717022624470	mess-w-3jqg	itc:v3:AWKTJkkk5kk5OSSUAAAAQa1qq7VddVQAAAE2BQ		item.comment	bn-3jqg	{"body":"[bn-3jqg report 2/3]\n\nTHE TABLE (all times ms; negative delta means main is faster)\n\n  cell                                        arm    n     min      p50      max\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  C1  seed-large: process wall                main  36   245.1    286.5   1742.2\n  C1  seed-large: process wall                old   36   397.1    456.5   1822.2\n  C1  seed-large: process wall                null  36   231.0    278.5    484.2\n  C1a   ...of which generator self-report     main  36   170.0    205.0   1040.0\n  C1a   ...of which generator self-report     old   36   150.0    200.0   1050.0\n  C1a   ...of which generator self-report     null  36   150.0    200.0    370.0\n  C1b   ...of which store create/open/close   main  36    65.1     81.5    702.2\n  C1b   ...of which store create/open/close   old   36   243.6    260.0    772.2\n  C1b   ...of which store create/open/close   null  36    62.1     76.4    160.8\n  C2  rebuild cmd: process wall               main  32   137.5    153.0    162.6\n  C2  rebuild cmd: process wall               old   32   143.6    153.6    167.0\n  C2  rebuild cmd: process wall               null  32   146.1    155.2    165.6\n  C3  rebuild-from-zero (from0_build)         main  32    51.0     57.0     62.0\n  C3  rebuild-from-zero (from0_build)         old   32    52.0     56.0     60.0\n  C3  rebuild-from-zero (from0_build)         null  32    51.0     57.0     61.0\n  C4  warm-resume (resume_build)              main  32     5.0      6.0      6.0\n  C4  warm-resume (resume_build)              old   32     5.0      6.0      7.0\n  C4  warm-resume (resume_build)              null  32     5.0      6.0      7.0\n  C5  hot-post bench: process wall            main  32   415.7    477.8    492.1\n  C5  hot-post bench: process wall            old   32   622.5    671.4   1030.4\n  C5  hot-post bench: process wall            null  32   420.3    481.8    703.8\n  C6  hot-post: full replay load (no sidecar) main  32    31.28    36.13    39.52\n  C6  hot-post: full replay load (no sidecar) old   32    32.87    36.74    52.44\n  C6  hot-post: full replay load (no sidecar) null  32    31.04    36.57    47.65\n  C7  hot-post: snapshot+tail load_cached     main  32     2.365    2.742    3.199\n  C7  hot-post: snapshot+tail load_cached     old   32     3.158    3.646    5.230\n  C7  hot-post: snapshot+tail load_cached     null  32     2.320    2.769    4.249\n  C8  hot-post: first warm-miss command       main  32     1.695    1.982    3.441\n  C8  hot-post: first warm-miss command       old   32     2.471    2.912    3.520\n  C8  hot-post: first warm-miss command       null  32     1.711    2.006    2.884\n\n  cell                                        min d%   p50 d%   null band   verdict\n  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  C1  seed-large: process wall                 -38.3    -37.2    +/- 5.8    main FASTER\n  C1a   ...of which generator self-report      +13.3     +2.5    +/-11.8    no detectable difference\n  C1b   ...of which store create/open/close    -73.3    -68.7    +/-12.3    main FASTER\n  C2  rebuild cmd: process wall                 -4.2     -0.4    +/- 6.3    no detectable difference\n  C3  rebuild-from-zero (from0_build)           -1.9     +1.8    +/- 1.8    no detectable difference\n  C4  warm-resume (resume_build)                 0.0      0.0    +/-16.7    no detectable difference\n  C5  hot-post bench: process wall             -33.2    -28.8    +/- 1.1    main FASTER\n  C6  hot-post: full replay load (no sidecar)   -4.8     -1.7    +/- 1.2    marginal, effectively none\n  C7  hot-post: snapshot+tail load_cached      -25.1    -24.8    +/- 1.9    main FASTER\n  C8  hot-post: first warm-miss command        -31.4    -31.9    +/- 1.2    main FASTER\n\n  The band is the same-binary null arm delta vs main, floored at one measurement quantum\n  where the instrument is quantised (C1a/C1b report 10 ms, C3/C4 report 1 ms).\n  Verdict rule: the SMALLER of the two deltas must exceed 1.5x the band to say DETECTED.\n\nSEED THROUGHPUT over 108,810 events\n  main proc-wall      min 443,941 ev/s   p50 379,791 ev/s\n  old  proc-wall      min 274,012 ev/s   p50 238,383 ev/s\n  main generate-only  min 640,059 ev/s   p50 530,780 ev/s\n  old  generate-only  min 725,400 ev/s   p50 544,050 ev/s\n\nWHAT IT MEANS\n1. The seed win is ENTIRELY store lifecycle, not write throughput. Decomposing C1 into\n   the generator self-report (C1a) and everything else (C1b) shows the pure write path\n   is indistinguishable (p50 +2.5 percent inside an 11.8 percent band; at 16 samples this\n   cell looked like a 12 percent regression and it collapsed into the band at 36, so do\n   not report a write regression). The 170 ms saved per seed run is creating and closing\n   the sidecar: fjall builds 8 keyspaces / 20 files / 20 dirs with journals and fsyncs,\n   the pack sidecar writes 3 files. Very stable: old paid 244-338 ms, main pays 62-161 ms.\n2. Read paths that never touch the sidecar are unchanged, as they should be. C2/C3/C4\n   (open plus projection replay plus checkpoint resume) and C6 (full replay load) are all\n   inside their bands. Note C2 shows no difference even though C1b shows 170 ms: opening\n   an EXISTING fjall keyspace is cheap, CREATING one is not.\n3. The snapshot read path is genuinely faster: C7 load_cached (a real snapshot fetch of a\n   1.2 MB blob plus a 0-event tail) is 25 percent faster and C8 first-warm-miss 32 percent\n   faster, both against roughly 1-2 percent bands. C5, the whole hot-post benchmark\n   process, is 29 percent faster - that also carries the save_snapshot write and the\n   sidecar create/close, consistent with 1 and 3.\n4. Net for a demo-shaped workload: main is meaningfully faster wherever the snapshot\n   sidecar is created, written or read, and identical everywhere else."}	blake3:x7ezG3ZY4s4cC26yhPWjqUlK50Gw6sYClgSrNqm0JGY
1785717022645648	mess-w-3jqg	itc:v3:AWKTJkkk5kk5OSSUAAAAQa1qq7VddVQAAAE2Bg		item.comment	bn-3jqg	{"body":"[bn-3jqg report 3/3]\n\nSTORE FOOTPRINT (same corpus, same checkpoint, not timed)\n\n  family                        main (pack)              old (fjall)\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  log segments seg-*.log        17,175,832 B / 1 file    17,175,832 B / 1 file\n  sealed/                                0 B / 0 files            0 B / 0 files\n  registry sidecar *.reg                 0 B / 0 files            0 B / 0 files\n  payload sidecar *.pcol                 0 B / 0 files            0 B / 0 files\n  snapshot sidecar                      80 B / 3 files       11,358 B / 20 files\n  projection checkpoint          2,618,705 B / 1 file     2,618,705 B / 1 file\n  LOCK                                   8 B / 1 file             8 B / 1 file\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  total apparent                19,794,625 B             19,805,903 B\n  total files / dirs            6 files / 2 dirs         23 files / 20 dirs\n  on-disk (du -sh)              259 M                    259 M\n\n  The log bytes are IDENTICAL at both commits. The 259 M on-disk against 19.8 M apparent\n  is the preallocated 256 MiB head segment, same in both arms. The snapshot sidecar is\n  the only difference: 3 files (IDENTITY, LOCK, one .open pack) vs a 20-file fjall\n  keyspace tree. Both sidecars are EMPTY of snapshots - the social binaries never call\n  save_snapshot - so this is the cost of merely existing. The zero counts for sealed,\n  .reg and .pcol are why the SealPack transition shape does NOT appear here: nothing seals.\n\nASYMMETRIES AND CAVEATS (stated plainly)\n- bn-2gns broke snapshot identity today: old-scheme snapshots are not read by main and\n  vice versa. Each arm SEEDS and RESUMES with ITS OWN binary against ITS OWN store; no\n  store is ever cross-read. So the identity break does not contaminate any cell.\n- Loadavg was 3.3-13.2, above the under-3 target, because the machine is shared. Bands\n  were measured concurrently under the same load; absolute ms are inflated for both arms.\n- Runs are short (0.15-0.7 s). Two runs (one main, one old, adjacent in the interleave)\n  hit a system stall at roughly 1.0 s and show up as the max column; min/p50 are the\n  reportable statistics.\n- All rebuild cells run page-cache-warm (the same three corpora are re-read); no cache\n  drop was possible without root. Both arms are equally warm.\n- The C1a/C1b split inherits the seed binary 10 ms print granularity; the band is floored\n  at one quantum accordingly.\n- The social example exposes no segment-size knob, so there is no way through this\n  example to make a segment seal and exercise the SealPack/.reg chain. That would need a\n  different harness (mess-bench) and is out of scope for this bone.\n- The hot-post cell is examples/social/tests/hot_post_bench.rs run with --ignored, the\n  example crate's own snapshot benchmark. Its default seed is fixed (0x0B00B1E5), so the\n  workload is deterministic; it exists unchanged at both commits apart from the backend\n  type rename.\n\nPHASE 1 - EXAMPLES WORK AT MAIN\n  cargo test -p social          PASS  122 passed / 0 failed / 3 ignored across 10 suites\n                                      (lib 51, gwt 34, projections 18, read_models 9,\n                                       snapshots 4, store_roundtrip 4, hot_post_bench 1,\n                                       seed_profile 1, two bin suites, doctests 0)\n  cargo test -p bank            PASS  9 passed / 0 failed\n  cargo build --release -p social --bins            PASS (social-seed, social-web)\n  cargo run --release -p social --example social    PASS (prints social example OK)\n  cargo run --release -p bank   --example bank      PASS (prints bank example OK)\n  examples/local_db.rs          NOT A CARGO TARGET. The root Cargo.toml is a virtual\n                                manifest with no [package], so there is no local_db\n                                example target - cargo build --example local_db fails\n                                with \"no example target named local_db\". The file is a\n                                dead stub: its entire sqlx/rusqlite body is commented out\n                                and it is literally fn main() {}. Last touched in\n                                fb0dd177 \"refactor to mess_db. rusqlite read\", pre-v1. It\n                                does compile standalone (rustc --edition 2021 succeeds).\n                                Nothing to fix for this bone, but it is a stale file that\n                                someone should delete or re-home.\n  functional smoke beyond tests PASS  social-seed --scale demo into a fresh dir produced\n                                1,526 commands / 3,008 events, then social-web --rebuild\n                                printed rebuild-compare PASS (vacuous, no checkpoint yet);\n                                after serving briefly and SIGINT to write the shutdown\n                                checkpoint, --rebuild printed PASS with \"resumed from\n                                checkpoint at position 3008\" - a real suffix resume,\n                                byte-compared against a clean from-0 rebuild.\n  social-web                    Built. Only ever run as a server for the three corpus-prep\n                                invocations (timeout -s INT), all of which exited cleanly\n                                after writing their checkpoint. No server left running.\n  old arm sanity                29b23266 also builds clean and its demo seed + --rebuild\n                                PASS with the identical position 3008 and the identical\n                                111,555-byte state fingerprint.\n\nNOTHING FAILED TO BUILD at either commit. The only surprise was local_db (above).\n\nNo repo edits were made. No seal review requested - this is a no-diff measurement bone\nand this comment is the record."}	blake3:tv1dsmsNZwMXnAzJIfYLzmLAaqAXfU9P0vM4vkBIlog
1785717161218554	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExoxI		item.comment	bn-3jqg	{"body":"VERDICT (lead): comparison accepted and relayed to Bob. The honest scope statement is the key finding: this corpus (17.2 MB, one preallocated 256 MiB segment, never rolls) makes the entire SealPack chain and cold-open work inert, so the A/B isolates the fjall-to-pack snapshot swap plus bn-2gns. Within that scope: store lifecycle 68.7 percent faster (fjalls 8 keyspaces and 20 files with journals vs 3 files), snapshot-assisted read path 25-32 percent faster, pure write throughput and non-sidecar read paths unchanged (a spurious +12.5 percent write regression at N=16 correctly collapsed into the band at N=36), log bytes byte-identical, 6 files vs 23. Measurement quality: palindromic interleave with an md5-verified null arm inside it, loadavg above target (3.3-13.2) honestly disclosed and absorbed by the design. Segment-rolling comparison would need mess-bench, out of scope here."}	blake3:sAz3rxMARQl5HrB3Ujbq4arz1L9EgDbucCLnm2epaHo
1785717161246851	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExpBI		item.move	bn-3jqg	{"state":"done"}	blake3:-sLFHaOf9XRPWRY8qdSSjE-Yv2aHQFp1fjp1WdQa3x0
1785717161368280	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExpRI		item.create	bn-3ehy	{"description":"Found by bn-3jqg examples verification: examples/local_db.rs at the workspace root is not reachable by any cargo target (root Cargo.toml is a virtual manifest, so cargo build --example local_db fails), its entire sqlx/rusqlite body is commented out, and main() is empty. Last touched pre-v1 (fb0dd177). Decide: delete it, or re-home it as a real example under a package with a working body. One-decision cleanup.","kind":"task","title":"examples/local_db.rs is a dead stub: not a cargo target, body commented out"}	blake3:XaNKMjZIrBViKpxuR1fm9IFn1hT2jvrPvcDAh9KotYQ
1785725294995650	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExphI		item.create	bn-1m6c	{"description":"Motivated by bn-3jqg: the social example is maximally shallow (stream-per-aggregate, 1.007 events/stream, 17 MB, no segment-size knob) so no segment ever rolls and the sealed tier, .pcol, .reg, SealPack, quarantine/re-seal, retention, and cold-open-at-scale are structurally unreachable from examples/. Build the complementary shape: a chat/feed service (channels, users, messages, reactions) with deep Zipf-distributed streams.\n\nDesign points agreed with Bob 2026-08-02:\n- Domain: channels as long-lived streams (a few hot channels accumulate thousands of messages, long tail stays shallow), users register, messages and reactions append. Both registry pressure AND stream depth in one corpus.\n- Knobs: --channels --users --messages --segment-bytes (the knob social lacks) --seal-pack on/off --seed, plus --scale demo/large/huge presets. Realistic payload sizes, roughly 100-800 B lognormal. Demo scale should produce a multi-segment sealed store; large scale gigabytes.\n- Read side: per-channel projection plus a global timeline projection with a discardable checkpoint sidecar and a --rebuild byte-compare check (copy the proven social patterns); a scroll-back query command that reads sealed history (exercises .pcol); a tail command (subscriber shape, frontier-advanced per SUB2).\n- chatter-bench mode emitting cell timings as JSON (seed, cold reopen, scroll-back, tail catch-up, checkpoint resume) so A/B harnesses consume output directly. Process-level timing only, no engine-internal hooks - it stays an honest example, not a shadow bench suite.\n- Natural cells unlocked: cold open with many sealed segments (bn-26pp), sealed history reads through .pcol (bn-bka2), SealPack vs loose A/B via the seal_pack flag (bn-1yor matrix reproducible), backup/restore/verify/rebuild-index lifecycle on a store with something to lose, retention.\n- Strategic: the punted attestation bones bn-hvp1 and bn-37jh call for production-shaped large-corpus measurement; this example is that corpus generator. Consider a small follow-up bone wiring one chatter-based workload into mess-bench with loose floors, same pattern as bn-1r9c live_tail.\n\nSize m. Not yet dispatched - Bob asked to file only.","kind":"task","title":"examples/chatter: a deep-stream chat/feed example that rolls segments (the realistic bench instrument)"}	blake3:PKx62eZIcy1e1_Ww2ynhCBIe3r5CSrp4yc1UvBTKYk4
1785867307025056	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExpxI		item.comment	bn-1m6c	{"body":"Dispatched to worker opus-1m6c 2026-08-04. Workspace bn-1m6c to be created from main. Lead mess-dev will review the seal request, merge, and close."}	blake3:5W1h9HueB3cB3OcaCig6mkW54QgPY0SjoAw55AIYCHA
