1785867561010024	opus-1m6c	itc:v3:AWLMmSZOSZk-ckQAAAAAQdrVrq2vuoAAAAEyBA		item.comment	bn-1m6c	{"body":"Studied examples/social end to end (lib, contracts, domain, projections, rebuild, seed, store_backend, bins) plus mess-store EngineOptions (segment_size, seal_pack), Backend::read_global_page/GlobalPage frontier, Version paging, PackSnapshotBackend::inner for engine metrics. Design: channel-<id> deep streams (Created/MessagePosted/ReactionAdded/Archived) plus user-<id> shallow streams for registry pressure; bounded aggregates (reaction targets a message ordinal, validated against a bounded message counter); bounded projection (users, channels, recent-timeline window) so the checkpoint stays small at gigabyte scale. Persisting a store-config sidecar because LogEngine resume re-allocates the active segment to the opened segment_size, so a cold reopen must use the same knob. Starting implementation."}	blake3:-loKU_ikBNQRuRGxYvUWY-WN7s6EnB3mGoWQfdihfTg
1785870016489778	opus-1m6c	itc:v3:AWLMmSZOSZk-ckQAAAAAQdrVrq2vuoAAAAEyBQ		item.comment	bn-1m6c	{"body":"FINAL REPORT bn-1m6c. Workspace bn-1m6c, epoch f4951e1c42159663983d02660474354a296a6812, HEAD 693e1d0a20bf5d93e8b21c1f9bce00c077ba608a, seal review cr-3ac27j (reviewer mess-dev), committed and unmerged, nothing released.\n\nWHAT WAS BUILT. New workspace member examples/chatter (added to root Cargo.toml members). Domain: channel-<id> is a long-lived DEEP stream with events Created, MessagePosted, ReactionAdded, Archived and BOUNDED folded state (two bits, two short strings, two counters, a few dozen bytes at any depth). A message carries the channel-local ordinal the aggregate stamped on it in decide, and a reaction names that ordinal as its target, which makes target < messages a complete referential check against one stream own state with no id set and no cross-stream read. user-<id> stays shallow (Registered, DisplayNameChanged) and supplies registry pressure. Author existence is deliberately not checked in decide, the accept-and-reconcile posture social documents.\n\nFILES. examples/chatter/Cargo.toml, README.md, src/{lib.rs, id.rs, ops.rs, seed.rs, projections.rs, rebuild.rs, scrollback.rs, tail.rs, bench.rs, store_backend.rs}, src/domain/{mod.rs, channel.rs, user.rs, snapshot_codec.rs}, src/bin/chatter.rs, tests/{segments.rs, checkpoint.rs, determinism.rs, read_paths.rs, bench_json.rs, cli.rs}. Root Cargo.toml and Cargo.lock touched. Deps match social plus fastrand (seeded PRNG, already in tree via mess_db) and serde_json (already in tree via mess-core, mess-cli, mess-bench) for the bench JSON. No arg-parsing crate, hand-rolled over std::env::args like the social binaries. No web layer.\n\nTEST COUNTS. Before 1257, after 1323 (66 new: 45 lib unit, 21 integration), 28 skipped, full just test green in the workspace. cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean with zero warnings. just fmt-check clean.\n\nDEMO SCALE, RELEASE, OBSERVED. 400 users, 24 channels, 20000 messages, 12000 reactions, 40 renames, 2 archives = 32466 events in 0.30s. Store 12.5 MiB across 12 segment files. Sealed tier 11 segments, all 11 SealPack, all 11 with a payload accelerator. Busiest channel 5975 messages, quietest 180. LARGE SCALE, RELEASE: 25000 users, 256 channels, 3M messages, 2.4M reactions = 5427768 events in 28.5s, 1.9 GiB across 28 segment files, 27 sealed segments, busiest channel 761257 messages, peak RSS 765 MB.\n\nCHATTER-BENCH JSON, RELEASE, DEMO SCALE. Cells in order: seed 354 ms (32466 events, about 92k events/s), cold_reopen 1.0 ms (11 sealed segments, 32896 published events), scroll_back 11.8 ms (20 backward pages of 50 through a 5975-message channel), tail_catch_up 45.6 ms (32466 records from position 0), checkpoint_resume 0.24 ms versus 21.6 ms for the same read model rebuilt from 0. The blob also carries tool, profile (release or debug), seed, the store census, and corpus provenance.\n\nMANDATORY ASSERTIONS, ALL PRESENT. tests/segments.rs demo_scale_leaves_more_than_one_sealed_segment asserts sealed segments greater than 1 at the real demo preset, cross-checks the engine report against .seal files on disk, and asserts segment_files equals sealed plus 1 and payload_indexed equals sealed. Also segment_bytes_controls_the_seal_cadence (smaller segments seal more, so the knob is wired to the engine, not decorative) and seal_pack_off_produces_loose_sidecars_instead (one .pcol per sealed segment, zero .seal). tests/checkpoint.rs proves the rebuild byte-compare passes with a REAL resume (resumed_from greater than 0), that a deleted checkpoint rebuilds silently with a byte-identical fingerprint, and that truncated, garbage, head-corrupt, stale-fold-version and ahead-of-the-log checkpoints are all REPORTED (CheckpointStatus Rejected plus a stderr line) and then rebuilt from, never an error blocking a read. tests/determinism.rs asserts byte-identical global logs for one seed at concurrency 1, a different-seed control, and identical per-stream sequences between sequential and pipelined runs and between two pipelined runs. tests/read_paths.rs asserts scroll-back walks a deep channel newest-to-oldest exactly once over a verified multi-segment store, agrees with a forward read differentially, and that a post-frontier append is delivered exactly once with read_global_page(after) exclusivity asserted directly. tests/bench_json.rs parses the JSON with serde_json and asserts all five cell names in order plus per-cell contracts. tests/cli.rs drives the real binary end to end.\n\nDEVIATIONS AND JUDGMENT CALLS, ALL DELIBERATE. (1) The rebuild byte-compare is a chatter rebuild SUBCOMMAND rather than a flag, because chatter is a subcommand CLI (seed, stats, scrollback, tail, rebuild, bench) rather than the two single-purpose binaries social has; the check itself is social rebuild_compare, unchanged. (2) The projection is BOUNDED (per-user rows, per-channel counters, a fixed 256-message recent window) rather than holding every message the way social holds every post. At gigabyte scale an unbounded read model would need gigabytes of RAM and would write a gigabyte checkpoint every cadence tick, turning the acceleration into a liability. Message history stays in the log where scroll-back reads it, which is also what routes that read through the sealed tier. The window folds identically on both paths, which is exactly what the byte-compare proves. (3) A .chatter-store config sidecar records segment_bytes and seal_pack next to the log. EngineOptions is per-open and SegmentWriter resume re-allocates the resumed active segment to whatever segment_size it was opened with, so reopening a 1 MiB-segment store under the 256 MiB default would grow the active segment and stop rolling, making every cold-open measurement a measurement of a different store. A missing or corrupt sidecar falls back to engine defaults, never an error. (4) Corpus planning is CHUNKED (20000 messages per chunk, the RNG threaded through the boundary so the draw sequence is unchanged). A single-pass plan of the huge preset would hold roughly 5 GB of message bodies in RAM before the first write; measured large-scale peak RSS with chunking is 765 MB. (5) Messages and reactions are planned INTERLEAVED, not phased. A first cut planned all messages then all reactions, and the newest scroll-back pages were then a wall of reactions with zero messages, which is realistic of no chat system and hid the very read path the example exists to show. (6) --limit on chatter tail means stop once at least N records have been delivered, never truncating a batch, because the tailer cursor has already advanced past the page and printing a resume cursor mid-batch would advertise a cursor that skips records.\n\nPREMISE CHECK. Nothing in the bone description was refuted. The predicted shape held exactly: demo scale reaches 11 sealed segments, large reaches 27 and 1.9 GiB, and both the .pcol payload accelerator and the SealPack-versus-loose A/B are now reachable from examples/.\n\nONE FINDING THE INSTRUMENT PRODUCED IMMEDIATELY. Backward paging cost is linear in the CHANNEL STREAM DEPTH and independent of page size. At large scale, release, one machine, 100-record pages: #storage (about 1.37M stream events) 122 ms per page, #oncall (596k) 53 ms, #hiring (160k) 10 ms, #growth (38k) 1.8 ms. A 20-record page and a 1000-record page on #storage both cost about 130 ms, so this is a fixed per-read_stream seek cost that scales with stream depth, not with how much of the stream you asked for. I did not chase it: it is an engine characteristic, and my pager issues exactly one read_stream per page. It is recorded in the README as an open question for a bone with a measurement behind it. By contrast the checkpoint resume behaves exactly as advertised at the same scale: from-0 rebuild 8.15s versus checkpoint resume 0.007s, byte-identical.\n\nHOUSEKEEPING. The large-scale scratch store at /home/bob/chatter-large-scratch (1.9 GiB) is still on disk. The shell safety layer refuses recursive deletion under /home, so someone with a terminal should remove that directory by hand. The demo-scale scratch stores live under the session scratchpad in /tmp and sweep themselves.\n\nI did not create the mess-bench workload follow-up bone; it stays with the lead as instructed."}	blake3:MqhuLN_R-om4KdVT8RATQy3YDdyoHot1Y-5ski2kIU0
