1785872087017007	opus-e93g	itc:v3:AWLkkyTzJmZMknEAAAAAQeq1e1trVcAAAAEzBA		item.comment	bn-e93g	{"body":"bn-e93g FINDINGS 1/3 - MECHANISM AND ATTRIBUTION. Verdict: PROCEED-with-named-target (full verdict in part 3/3).\n\nPROVENANCE: workspace bn-e93g, epoch 09edc3ed04e6ecbe2c03b46ec6534accaac00b84, HEAD bd349bed6d01485511c627c7628ade1761feeedf, review cr-301b2z. Release builds, 64-core host. Corpora: chatter --scale large (5,453,030 events, 1.9 GiB, 27 sealed segments, seed 1337) plus a sealed/unsealed twin pair at 400,000 messages.\n\nMECHANISM AT SOURCE (named before measuring, then confirmed)\n\ncrates/mess-store/src/engine.rs:5322 LogEngine::read_stream computes start = after.next_position(), then branches at engine.rs:5346-5352:\n\n  let sealed = !self.inner.sealed.segments_for_stream(sid).is_empty();\n  let entries = if sealed { self.sealed_and_hot_entries(sid)? }\n                else { self.inner.active.stream_entries_from(sid, start, limit) };\n\nThe UNSEALED branch takes start and limit. crates/mess-index/src/active.rs:409 ActiveIndex::stream_entries_from binary-searches past the pre-cursor prefix and stops copying once the page is covered; its own doc says \"A paged read of a long stream thus costs O(page), not O(stream history)\". That is bn-2ib's work.\n\nThe SEALED branch takes NEITHER. crates/mess-store/src/engine.rs:4645 sealed_and_hot_entries(&self, stream_id) has no cursor and no limit parameter. It calls crates/mess-index/src/sealed/replay.rs:205 ReplaySet::stream_replay, which extend_from_slice's every decoded per-(segment,stream) pointer block for the whole stream; inserts every one of those entries into a BTreeMap<u64, StreamEntry>; unions the entire hot tail via crates/mess-index/src/active.rs:388 ActiveIndex::stream_entries (also unbounded); and collects the whole thing into a Vec. read_stream then discards the prefix with a linear skip at engine.rs:5356-5358: if e.last_version() < start { continue; }.\n\nPer-call cost is therefore O(total batch entries of the stream), independent of both the after cursor and the limit. Everything else in the call - the batch fetch and materialize at engine.rs:4671 - is O(limit).\n\nThis is an OMISSION, not an inherent cost. The information needed to seek is already present: SealedSegmentIndex::stream_head(stream) bounds each segment's version range (already used at crates/mess-index/src/sealed/store.rs:200), and each cached block is version-ascending by construction.\n\nThe bone's premise is NOT in the example's pager. examples/chatter/src/scrollback.rs:145-160 issues exactly one bounded read_stream per page, as documented. Premise confirmed.\n\nATTRIBUTION (coarse in-workspace instrumentation, since reverted; engine.rs is byte-identical to the epoch)\n\nOne read_stream call, #storage, 1,370,418 events, 27 sealed segments, warm block cache, after=NoStream, limit=1000:\n\n  resolve_entries                                    137.8 ms\n    segments_for_stream + ReplaySet::from_segments     0.00 ms\n    stream_replay (cache hits + memcpy of 1,325,863)   18.3 ms\n    BTreeMap build (1,325,863 inserts)                 83.0 ms  (60 pct)\n    hot-tail union (44,555 entries)                     3.4 ms\n    into_values collect (1,370,418-element Vec)        32.3 ms  (24 pct)\n  skip_and_batch                                       0.15 ms\n  materialize (the 1000 records actually returned)     0.21 ms\n\n140 ms of work to deliver 0.2 ms of page. A read positioned AT the head, returning ZERO records, still costs 135 ms."}	blake3:oQ9DcBBpMUws-USO6uXLefA4UgJCNjSxtA3ldrPPc2g
1785872110105120	opus-e93g	itc:v3:AWLkkyTzJmZMknEAAAAAQeq1e1trVcAAAAEzBQ		item.comment	bn-e93g	{"body":"bn-e93g FINDINGS 2/3 - MEASURED CURVE AND BLAST RADIUS.\n\n(a) LINEAR-TO-SLIGHTLY-SUPERLINEAR IN DEPTH. chatter --scale large, scrollback --page-size 20, median of pages 2-5 (page 1 excluded: it pays the cold block decode), loadavg 9.99-10.51, one contiguous run:\n\n  depth (events)   ms/page   ns/entry\n  38,212           1.88      49\n  86,465           4.74      55\n  112,851          6.31      56\n  160,467          9.98      62\n  198,383          13.4      68\n  259,244          24.4      94\n  366,282          37.9      103\n  595,966          61.9      104\n  1,370,418        141.6     103\n\nns/entry roughly doubles over a 36x depth range - the BTreeMap log factor plus growing cache-miss density. Slightly worse than linear.\n\n(b) FLAT IN PAGE SIZE. #storage, ABBA-ordered (20/1000/1000/20 then 100/5000/5000/100), loadavg 8.75-8.90, medians: page 20 -> 148.1 ms; page 100 -> 140.8 ms; page 1000 -> 150.7 ms; page 5000 -> 152.7 ms. A 250x change in requested records moves page cost under 10 pct.\n\n(c) FLAT IN CURSOR POSITION. #storage, limit 1000, best of 3 per cursor, loadavg 15.23: cursor 0 -> 121.2 ms; 171,302 -> 120.8; 342,604 -> 121.3; 685,209 -> 119.1; 1,370,418 (head, zero records returned) -> 125.8.\n\n(d) THE COUNTERFACTUAL, ABBA. Two corpora seeded with identical parameters (seed 1337, 256 channels, 400,000 messages), one --segment-bytes 64M (3 sealed segments), one --segment-bytes 2G (0 sealed segments). Same channel #watercooler, same depth 132,555. Order sealed/unsealed/unsealed/sealed, loadavg 3.76-4.00:\n\n  scrollback page (20 records):  sealed 9.5 ms         unsealed 0.01 ms\n  full EventStore::load:         sealed 1472/1487 ms   unsealed 141/139 ms\n\nInstrumented, same page (start=0 limit=1000): sealed entries=132,555 resolve=8.6 ms; unsealed entries=1,000 resolve=0.01 ms. The sealed branch resolves 132x more entries to serve the identical page.\n\nBLAST RADIUS. Every read_stream call against a stream with ANY sealed segment pays the tax. Non-test, non-bench callers:\n\n  store.rs:381 EventStore::load - HOT AND QUADRATIC. Pages from Version::NoStream with limit = page_size (DEFAULT_PAGE_SIZE 1000, store.rs:24) until a short page, so each of N/1000 calls re-resolves all N entries. Reached from command (store.rs:464), command_as (:527), save_snapshot (:652), and BOTH load_cached fallbacks (:745 NoSnapshot, :751 Invalidated).\n  store.rs:847 EventStore::replay_tail - snapshot-resume tail (seeded from SnapshotCoverage::resume_from at store.rs:824), command_cached conflict catch-up (:1010), load_hot warm-but-behind (:1084). Few calls, each paying full O(depth).\n  examples/chatter/src/scrollback.rs:154 - the interactive pager the bone was filed on. Rare.\n  crates/mess-soak/src/driver.rs:444 (limit 1, deep cursor) and :518 - soak harness only.\n  crates/mess-store/src/pack_snapshot/mod.rs:204 - transparent pass-through decorator.\n  crates/mess-store/src/registry/mod.rs:84 registry::replay - LATENT ONLY. Registry::bootstrap has no production caller; LogEngine rebuilds its registry from the .reg per-segment deltas (bn-26pp). A Registry<B> over some other Backend would pay it.\n  Projections and subscriptions do NOT use read_stream at all (EventStore::subscribe goes through read_global_page). mess-cli has zero read_stream calls in src/.\n\nSo the Amdahl denominator is aggregate rehydration, not scroll-back.\n\nHEADLINE MEASUREMENT: EventStore::load of #storage (1,370,418 events, 1371 read_stream calls) = 180,103.7 ms. Three minutes to rehydrate ONE aggregate. The unsealed twin measures 1.05 us/event, so the same load without the defect extrapolates to about 1.4 s.\n\nTwo facts that make this a live production shape, not a curiosity:\n\n1. examples/chatter never calls save_snapshot, so load_cached returns NoSnapshot (store.rs:745) and falls straight through to the full from-zero load. The first post_message to #storage after any restart or cache eviction costs 180 s. chatter's ops.rs doc comment already says \"a cache miss is a thousands-event replay\" - correct, but nobody costed that the replay is QUADRATIC on a sealed stream.\n\n2. A SNAPSHOT DOES NOT FIX IT. Snapshots bound the NUMBER of read_stream calls (one or two), not the cost of each. A correctly snapshotted 1.37M-event aggregate still pays a ~140 ms floor per cold command, and that floor grows linearly with total stream depth forever. That defeats the central promise of snapshots on exactly the shape snapshots exist for.\n\nNote the tax scales with BATCH entries, not events. chatter appends one event per command (1,325,863 sealed entries for 1,370,418 events) so they coincide here; an app that batches appends pays proportionally less."}	blake3:dgv4vq73Nmoda9gLYVNw8k-l2EtUI4EHh9ivP7g2MKA
1785872135980986	opus-e93g	itc:v3:AWLkkyTzJmZMknEAAAAAQeq1e1trVcAAAAEzBg		item.comment	bn-e93g	{"body":"bn-e93g FINDINGS 3/3 - AMDAHL BOUND AND VERDICT.\n\nAMDAHL ARITHMETIC. The post-fix per-call residual is measured, not guessed: the unsealed twin at the same depth serves the identical page in 0.01 ms (20 records) / 0.15 ms (1000 records). A sealed seek would additionally probe 27 segment heads (measured: segments_for_stream + ReplaySet::from_segments is 0.00 ms at 27 segments) and binary-search 1-2 cached blocks. Call the residual 0.2 ms at 1.37M depth - i.e. the materialize cost plus noise.\n\n  path                                          now       after    factor\n  snapshot-resume cold command, 1.37M stream    140 ms    0.2 ms   ~700x\n  command_cached conflict / behind catch-up     140 ms    0.2 ms   ~700x\n  full rehydration, no snapshot, 1.37M stream   180.1 s   ~1.4 s   ~126x\n  full rehydration, no snapshot, 132k stream    1.48 s    0.14 s   10.6x  (both arms measured)\n  scroll-back page, 1.37M stream                142 ms    0.2 ms   ~700x\n\nComposed over a realistic workload the honest number is NOT 700x. Warm command_cached hits read zero events and are unaffected. Shallow streams - every stream in examples/social, every user stream in chatter - are unaffected. Projections are unaffected. The win is concentrated entirely on cold or contended access to DEEP sealed streams.\n\nWhat makes it worth doing is not the multiplier but the SHAPE. Today the cost is unbounded in stream depth on the two paths that are supposed to be O(tail) and O(N): the snapshot tail and the cache-miss rehydration. A store whose channels keep growing gets monotonically slower at rehydrating them, with a quadratic term nobody budgeted for. The fix converts an unbounded term into a bounded one; the 126x on a measured three-minute aggregate load is the evidence that the term is already live at a scale the project ships an example for.\n\nSecond-order win worth naming: the block cache budget defaults to 64 MiB (engine.rs:2795). One 1.37M-entry stream needs roughly 53 MB of decoded entries resident to serve ANY page today, because sealed_and_hot_entries touches all 27 of its blocks. A seek touches 1-2, so the resident working set for a deep-stream read collapses by more than an order of magnitude - which matters at 256 channels against a 64 MiB budget.\n\nVERDICT: PROCEED-with-named-target.\n\nTarget, exactly:\n  (i) Add ReplaySet::stream_replay_from(stream_id, from_version, max_events, cache) in crates/mess-index/src/sealed/replay.rs alongside stream_replay at :205. Skip any segment whose stream_head(stream) < from_version; binary-search the cached version-ascending block for the first entry with last_version >= from_version; stop once max_events is covered. This mirrors ActiveIndex::stream_entries_from (crates/mess-index/src/active.rs:409) exactly.\n  (ii) Replace crates/mess-store/src/engine.rs:4645 sealed_and_hot_entries(sid) with a bounded sealed_and_hot_entries_from(sid, start, limit) that merges the bounded sealed slice with ActiveIndex::stream_entries_from's bounded hot slice. Both inputs are already version-ascending, so the BTreeMap dedupe (60 pct of current cost) becomes a two-way merge over at most a page of entries, and the whole-stream collect (24 pct) disappears.\n  (iii) Call it from engine.rs:5348 so BOTH branches of read_stream take start and limit.\n\nExpected win the construction bone must hold itself to: per-call sealed read_stream at 1.37M depth from ~140 ms to under 1 ms; EventStore::load of #storage from 180 s to under 3 s. Both directly measurable with chatter --scale large; no new instrument needed.\n\nRisk the construction bone must handle: the sealed/hot dedupe during seal handoff currently relies on the BTreeMap keyed by first_version (engine.rs:4655-4662, \"sealed entries win the dedupe\"). A bounded merge must preserve that across a window that may straddle the handoff boundary. The gates are crates/mess-store/tests/engine_reopen.rs, sealed_candidate_lifecycle.rs and engine_block_reads.rs (which already asserts sealed reads at non-zero cursors are identical to the from-zero read).\n\nSeparately worth a lead decision, NOT part of this target: examples/chatter never calls save_snapshot, so its documented \"cache miss is a thousands-event replay\" is in fact a 180 s stall on the deepest channel. That is an example-fidelity question independent of the engine fix.\n\nWHAT MERGED: only the open-question paragraph in examples/chatter/README.md, rewritten to name the mechanism, the two headline numbers, and the verdict. The instrumentation used for section 2 attribution and the throwaway probe binary were both reverted; crates/mess-store/src/engine.rs is byte-identical to the epoch. cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean, just fmt-check clean, just test 1323 passed / 0 failed / 28 skipped.\n\nDEVIATIONS DISCLOSED: this host had other agents active throughout. Loadavg is recorded per cell and ranged 3.3 to 15.2 on 64 cores. The depth curve was taken at loadavg ~10 in one contiguous run so its cells are mutually comparable; the ABBA counterfactual ran under loadavg 4. The measured effects are 10x to 950x, far outside plausible scheduling noise at these loads. The only non-measured figure is the ~1.4 s post-fix estimate for a 1.37M full load: it is an extrapolation from the unsealed twin's measured 1.05 us/event, because seeding an unsealed 1.9 GiB corpus would need a single 2 GiB+ active segment. Everything else is measured."}	blake3:OFTULfEnXEmJ4wW0I-Ma2ZPJYH5JDkGWcCZnyhA9C5A
1785872308045776	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExrxI		item.move	bn-e93g	{"state":"done"}	blake3:Te-jiWpc_pqN1iUb6A7XLM-Fyqe-vK-Z2rhOBguFvow
1785872308065680	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExsBI		item.comment	bn-e93g	{"body":"Merged to main at fae65a28a5a2 after lead review. All three load-bearing claims verified at source by mess-dev: the sealed branch at engine.rs 5346 calls sealed_and_hot_entries which at 4645 takes only the stream id and resolves the whole stream through a BTreeMap; the linear prefix skip at 5356; EventStore load at store.rs 378 loops read_stream per page so sealed-stream rehydration is quadratic, and the 180 s measurement is arithmetically consistent with the per-page floor. crates tree confirmed byte-identical to trunk, README-only diff. Verdict PROCEED-with-named-target accepted; construction bone filed separately."}	blake3:ykF3JzJhhpQP_4YhyVnQoWGoHm3C77YZcSV6nijhl6M
1785872325060926	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExsRI		item.create	bn-1u6p	{"description":"Construction authorized by measurement bone bn-e93g (PROCEED-with-named-target, 2026-08-04), mechanism verified at source by lead. THE DEFECT: LogEngine read_stream branches at crates/mess-store/src/engine.rs line 5346; the unsealed branch pushes cursor and limit into ActiveIndex stream_entries_from (bounded, bn-2ib), but the sealed branch calls sealed_and_hot_entries at line 4645 which takes only the stream id - it replays every batch entry of the whole stream across every sealed segment into a BTreeMap, unions the entire hot tail, and read_stream then discards the prefix with a linear skip at line 5356. Per-call cost is O(stream depth) regardless of cursor or limit: measured 140 ms of entry resolution around 0.2 ms of materialization at 1.37M events, and a head-positioned read returning zero records still pays 135 ms. BLAST RADIUS: EventStore load at crates/mess-store/src/store.rs line 378 loops read_stream per page, so cache-miss rehydration of a sealed stream is quadratic - measured 180 seconds for the deepest chatter channel at scale large. Snapshots do not remove the floor: they bound the number of read_stream calls, not the cost of each. THE TARGET: add a bounded stream_replay_from taking stream, from_version, max_events, cache to ReplaySet in crates/mess-index/src/sealed/replay.rs near line 205, using the per-segment stream_head bounds and version-ascending cached blocks that already exist, and rewrite sealed_and_hot_entries as a bounded two-way merge of sealed and hot entries instead of the whole-stream BTreeMap. WIN TO HOLD CONSTRUCTION TO, from bn-e93g measurements: per-call entry resolution at 1.37M depth from 140 ms to under 1 ms; full rehydration of the deepest chatter channel from 180 s to under 3 s. NAMED RISK: the sealed-wins-over-hot dedupe during seal handoff currently rides on the BTreeMap insert order; the bounded merge must preserve sealed-wins for a page window straddling the handoff, and needs a dedicated test reading across the handoff boundary. ACCEPTANCE: both win bounds hit in release ABBA cells on the chatter corpus (scroll-back page cost and a load cell on the deepest channel, before vs after), the handoff dedupe test present and passing, zero behavior change on unsealed streams, full suite green. Not yet dispatched.","kind":"task","title":"perf: bounded sealed-tier stream reads - give read_stream's sealed branch the seek the active tier already has (bn-e93g target)"}	blake3:LRFsDVSvKTGLVZErJ9qJp-3Yi7hVy6VRNjqgN02ELTk
1785872733700465	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExshI		item.comment	bn-1u6p	{"body":"Dispatched to worker opus-1u6p 2026-08-04. Engine read-path construction with hard acceptance bounds from bn-e93g: per-call entry resolution under 1 ms at 1.37M depth, deepest-channel rehydration under 3 s, seal-handoff dedupe test, exhaustive cursor-sweep equivalence test, baseline binary captured before any change for ABBA. Lead mess-dev reviews."}	blake3:DhIj5lvfxWugkOKggU6bPMVDjovWuuMJkF_-WE7RajQ
1785872751626894	opus-1u6p	itc:v3:AWLmTOZJmSTnJ0AAAAAAQe1u1bVd1wAAAAEwAQ		item.move	bn-1u6p	{"state":"doing"}	blake3:Fm69dRmWIKK5G07i5Odu_mAbWpEVAfq39KZp2MGAvbU
1785872751641935	opus-1u6p	itc:v3:AWLmTOZJmSTnJ0AAAAAAQe1u1bVd1wAAAAEwAg		item.assign	bn-1u6p	{"action":"assign","agent":"opus-1u6p"}	blake3:m6NtQZu77lACBGKyEWB4p08iLmH-aOtk-jNnwChKjVA
1785872751658839	opus-1u6p	itc:v3:AWLmTOZJmSTnJ0AAAAAAQe1u1bVd1wAAAAEwAw		item.comment	bn-1u6p	{"body":"Started in workspace bn-1u6p"}	blake3:YVXjhysHv7i4yMm9EpntqHrStB6ZM306dcPiffPJkzg
1785875295697585	opus-1u6p	itc:v3:AWLmTOZJmSTnJ0AAAAAAQe1u1bVd1wAAAAEwBA		item.comment	bn-1u6p	{"body":"bn-1u6p FINAL REPORT 1/3 - WHAT CHANGED AND WHY IT IS CORRECT.\n\nPROVENANCE: workspace bn-1u6p, epoch 9c647d040db57b2d64bb88e6b4c0417170d21ad1, HEAD cd6b133ba02451e1a8aae41eb18a5b1937c8b0dd, review cr-2e6jsu. Net diff vs epoch is exactly four files: crates/mess-index/src/sealed/replay.rs, crates/mess-store/src/engine.rs, crates/mess-store/tests/engine_sealed_paging.rs (new), examples/chatter/README.md. Gates: cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean, just fmt-check clean, just test 1333 passed / 0 failed / 28 skipped.\n\nTHE CHANGE\n\ncrates/mess-index/src/sealed/replay.rs:222 ReplaySet::stream_replay_from(stream_id, from_version, max_events, cache). Per segment in base-pos order: if stream_head(stream) is None or below from_version, skip WITHOUT touching the block (the directory probe is a hash lookup, no decode); otherwise take the cached version-ascending block and partition_point on last_version < from_version to land on the first relevant batch; push entries until they cover max_events events at or past the cursor, then stop. Two debug_asserts: strictly ascending, and no pre-cursor batch escapes.\n\ncrates/mess-store/src/engine.rs:4681 sealed_and_hot_entries_from(stream_id, from_version, limit) replaces sealed_and_hot_entries(stream_id). It calls stream_replay_from for the sealed side and ActiveIndex::stream_entries_from for the hot side - both bounded, both already version-ascending - and merges them with merge_sealed_and_hot at engine.rs:5326.\n\ncrates/mess-store/src/engine.rs:5326 merge_sealed_and_hot(sealed, hot, from_version, limit) is a free function so the dedupe rule is unit-testable: at the engine level the two tiers entries for one batch are byte-identical, so which one wins is otherwise unobservable. The Equal arm takes the SEALED entry and drops the hot one - the same rule the old BTreeMap encoded through insert order, now stated directly.\n\ncrates/mess-store/src/engine.rs:5488 read_stream now passes start and limit into BOTH branches. The linear prefix skip at what is now engine.rs:5498 is kept as an O(1) guard with a comment marking it vestigial.\n\nWHY THE BOUNDED MERGE CANNOT OPEN A GAP. Both inputs share one contract: they truncate ONLY when over-full, so a result covering fewer than max_events events is the complete remainder of that tier. The merge therefore stops once its own output covers limit events at or past the cursor, and it can only run off the end of one input while the other still has entries when that input was complete. There is no cursor/limit pair for which the merged prefix has a hole. This is asserted directly (stream_replay_from_short_result_is_complete in replay.rs) as well as end-to-end.\n\nONE DELIBERATE SEMANTIC NOTE FOR THE LEAD. With limit 0 on a sealed stream the old code resolved the whole stream, then fetched exactly one batch through reader.batch before the picks.len() >= limit break; the new code resolves nothing and fetches nothing. That makes the sealed branch match what the UNSEALED branch already did (stream_entries_from with max_events 0 returns empty), so this removes an inconsistency rather than introducing one - but it is a real, if tiny, behaviour change and I did not want it buried.\n\nmerge_sealed_and_hot uses saturating arithmetic for the per-batch coverage count. Both production inputs are seeked so a pre-cursor batch is a contract violation, but a violation must not panic in debug nor wrap in release; a debug_assert names the contract."}	blake3:EUoNicHmxLaIkIU8qQ8Znfp26-L_IKhtXWDEL0YYdVc
1785875318594546	opus-1u6p	itc:v3:AWLmTOZJmSTnJ0AAAAAAQe1u1bVd1wAAAAEwBQ		item.comment	bn-1u6p	{"body":"bn-1u6p FINAL REPORT 2/3 - TESTS.\n\nBOUNDED-RESOLVE EQUIVALENCE. crates/mess-store/tests/engine_sealed_paging.rs sealed_paging_is_byte_identical_across_every_cursor_and_limit: a corpus with 16 KiB segments and ~400 byte payloads so a few hundred appends really do span many sealed segments, seeded in two passes (before and after the seals) so the deep stream has sealed segments AND a genuine hot tail. Batches are 7 events wide so cursors land inside batches, at a batch first version, and at its last. The sweep is EXHAUSTIVE: every cursor from Version::NoStream through At(head+1) - that is 0, mid-first-segment, every segment boundary, every mid-segment and batch-straddling offset, the hot tail, the head, and past the head - crossed with limits 1, 2, 7, 8, 33, 1000, each page compared record-for-record against a full oracle (stream_id, message_type, data, stream_position, global_position) plus a density check that positions inside a page are consecutive. Run live, after a reopen, with the block cache DISABLED (budget 0) and with it starved to 512 bytes so it evicts on every read. Also a shallow companion stream (single-segment sealed coverage) and the whole stream paged from zero at page 13, which is the EventStore::load loop shape.\n\nAt the index level, crates/mess-index/src/sealed/replay.rs stream_replay_from_matches_filtered_whole_stream_replay sweeps from_version 0..=62 crossed with max_events 0, 1, 5, 9, 10, 11, 20, 60, 1000 over a three-segment stream and compares against an independently written oracle that filters the whole-stream coalesce; stream_replay_from_returns_the_batch_straddling_the_cursor pins the off-by-one explicitly (cursor at v25 inside batch [20,29] must return that batch; cursor at a batch first version, at its last version, and past the head).\n\nSEAL-HANDOFF DEDUPE (the named risk). Two tests.\n- crates/mess-store/tests/engine_sealed_paging.rs seal_handoff_window_delivers_every_event_exactly_once builds the real window the way engine_block_reads.rs does: ONE large segment so nothing rolls and nothing is evicted from the active index, 200 events appended, then seal_active, so versions 0..=199 are in the sealed sidecar AND still in the active index; then 100 more events that exist ONLY in the hot tail. Full cursor sweep across the whole stream at limits 1, 5, 6, 17, 300, plus pages deliberately centred on v199/v200 at five page sizes with a BTreeSet duplicate check, plus the whole stream paged from zero at page 7, plus the same sweep after a reopen.\n- crates/mess-store/src/engine.rs sealed_paging_tests::merge_gives_the_handoff_window_to_the_sealed_tier_exactly_once asserts the RULE, which the end-to-end test cannot: it constructs overlapping sealed and hot entries with DIFFERENT EventPtrs and asserts the surviving pointers are the sealed ones, both for a whole-stream merge and for a page straddling the boundary. In production the two tiers pointers for one batch are identical, so this is the only place sealed-wins is observable at all.\n\nSTRUCTURAL BOUNDEDNESS (not wall-clock). Two levels, both using observability that already exists rather than new instrumentation.\n- crates/mess-index/src/sealed/replay.rs stream_replay_from_decodes_only_the_segments_it_needs: with a real enabled BlockCache, the whole-stream replay records 3 misses over a three-segment stream; a seek into the last segment records 1; a one-batch page from version 0 records 1; a cursor past the head records 0 and leaves the cache empty.\n- crates/mess-store/tests/engine_sealed_paging.rs deep_cursor_page_decodes_only_the_blocks_it_needs: a freshly reopened engine over a many-segment sealed stream starts at cache_misses 0 (asserted); one page at a cursor near the head, and one page from zero, must each cost at most 2 block-cache misses.\n\nTHAT LAST TEST IS A REAL GATE, VERIFIED. I temporarily restored the old whole-stream resolve inside sealed_and_hot_entries_from and re-ran: it reports 28 block-cache misses for a single page against a 28-segment stream, and fails. The equivalence and handoff tests PASS under the old resolve, which is correct and expected - trunk was correct, just unbounded - so those two are equivalence gates on my change, and the miss counter is the gate on the actual defect."}	blake3:JtZYel-cGFvF77Tt9wpPodYJGJrqqYJX9Mtoo-dUdmM
1785875394152029	opus-1u6p	itc:v3:AWLmTOZJmSTnJ0AAAAAAQe1u1bVd1wAAAAEwBg		item.comment	bn-1u6p	{"body":"bn-1u6p FINAL REPORT 3/3 - MEASUREMENT. ONE WIN BOUND HIT, ONE MISSED. STOPPING FOR THE LEAD CALL.\n\nMETHOD. Release builds only. The BEFORE arm binary was built and copied out of the untouched workspace BEFORE any edit. Both arms were built before any measured cell. Stronger provenance than that: after all edits I regenerated the epoch sources in the workspace and rebuilt - the resulting chatter binary is BIT-IDENTICAL to the baseline binary saved before any change, so the before arm is provably trunk. The after-arm binaries likewise reproduce bit-identically. ONE corpus, seeded once by the BASELINE binary (chatter seed --scale large --seed 1337): 5,427,768 events, 1.9 GiB, 27 sealed segments, deepest channel #storage at 761,257 messages / 1,370,418 stream events - the same depth bn-e93g measured. Both binaries read that same store; the format assumption was verified, not assumed: chatter scrollback output on #storage is byte-identical between the two arms with timings stripped. Every cell is ABBA (baseline, after, after, baseline) with loadavg recorded per cell. This is a shared 64-core host with other agents active; loadavg was 4.5 to 10.3 throughout except where noted.\n\nCELL 1 - SCROLL-BACK PAGE COST, #storage, 8 pages of 20, shipped chatter CLI both arms. Per-page ms:\n  baseline (loadavg 6.81) 145.09 121.39 120.14 126.53 121.94 128.64 132.72 133.78\n  after    (loadavg 6.83) 0.02 0.01 0.01 0.01 0.02 0.01 0.01 0.01\n  after    (loadavg 6.69) 0.03 0.01 0.01 0.01 0.01 0.01 0.01 0.01\n  baseline (loadavg 6.52) 126.88 124.30 135.18 128.51 125.74 131.89 129.15 129.17\nNULL BAND: the two same-binary baseline cells give medians 126.5 and 129.2 ms - about 2 percent. The two after cells are identical at 0.01 ms. The effect is four orders of magnitude outside that band.\n\nCELL 2 - ONE read_stream, limit 1000, three cursors, 5 reps each. Medians:\n  cursor 0        baseline 120.9 / 124.4 ms   after 0.535 / 0.389 ms\n  cursor 685,000  baseline 126.2 / 125.9 ms   after 0.513 / 0.451 ms\n  cursor head     baseline 138.7 / 142.3 ms   after 0.051 / 0.048 ms   (418 records)\nloadavg 5.60 to 8.71. First rep of each after cell is 0.8 to 1.8 ms - the one-time cold decode of the single straddling pointer block; every subsequent call is sub-millisecond. WIN BOUND 1 (per-call entry resolution at 1.37M depth from 140 ms to under 1 ms): HIT. The 0.39 to 0.51 ms figure INCLUDES materialising 1000 records, which bn-e93g measured at 0.21 ms, so entry resolution proper is roughly 0.2 to 0.3 ms.\n\nCELL 3 - FULL COLD EventStore::load OF #storage (1,370,418 events, 1371 read_stream calls), one process per cell so the block cache starts cold:\n  baseline (loadavg 6.01) 178,273 ms\n  after    (loadavg 4.47)   3,455 ms\n  after    (loadavg 4.56)   3,542 ms\n  baseline (loadavg 4.55) 178,614 ms\nThree further cold after-arm runs: 3,367 ms at loadavg 7.84, then 6,551 ms and 4,238 ms as another agent drove loadavg to 28.5 - I disclose those two and exclude them as contaminated. Central estimate 3.37 to 3.54 s. 51.7x.\nWIN BOUND 2 (full rehydration of the deepest chatter channel from 180 s to UNDER 3 s): MISSED, by about 15 percent. I am not shipping past it; this is the lead call.\n\nWHERE THE RESIDUAL IS, MEASURED NOT ARGUED. I added a page-all probe mode that runs the identical paging loop with NO Event::decode and NO Aggregate::apply, and rebuilt BOTH arms for it by the same epoch-regeneration procedure:\n  baseline 181,701 ms and 182,128 ms; after 3,247 ms and 3,160 ms (ABBA, loadavg 5.4 to 8.7).\nSo the application deserialisation is only about 0.25 s of the 3.46 s: the remaining 3.2 s is inside the engine. Then the discriminator - after arm, same total records, varying page size so the number of read_stream CALLS changes 20x:\n  limit 1000  -> 1371 calls -> 3200, 3194, 3438 ms\n  limit 5000  ->  275 calls -> 2813, 2827, 2745 ms\n  limit 20000 ->   69 calls -> 2730, 2851, 2913 ms\nCutting calls 20x moves the total only from about 3.2 s to about 2.8 s. Per-call cost solves to (3199-2730)/(1371-69) = 0.36 ms, self-consistent with cell 2. The other ~2.7 s is PER-RECORD: fetching and materialising 1,370,418 records through the sealed payload accelerator (batch fetch, payload copy, two String allocations per record in LogEngine::materialize). It scales with records, not with calls, and this bone target does not touch it. An entry resolve that cost literally zero would still land at about 2.7 to 2.8 s at EventStore default page size 1000.\n\nMY READ: the 3 s bound came from bn-e93g extrapolating its unsealed twin at 1.05 us/event. This corpus materialises sealed records at about 2.0 us/event - the twin read uncompressed active-segment bytes, this reads through SealPack payload sections. That is where the estimate slipped, and it is a materialisation constant, not the quadratic term the bone was filed on. The SHAPE the bone set out to fix IS fixed: per-call cost is now flat in stream depth, and the aggregate load is linear in records rather than quadratic in them. But the literal number was missed and I am not going to call 3.46 under 3.\n\nREPRODUCTION. Cells 1 and 2 need no probe. Cell 3 needs the throwaway rehydrate_probe, which is NOT in HEAD - its source is in this workspace history at commit c4e22f33 (load and page modes) and 0a81a1f2 (page-all mode). HEAD is byte-identical to the tests commit 1e0656d7 plus the README edit; the probe leaves no trace in the net diff.\n\nDOCS JUDGMENT CALL. I rewrote the chatter README open-question section that bn-e93g merged: it described the whole-stream resolve as live behaviour, which is now false. It is past tense now, with the fix and the measured cells named. Drop that hunk if you would rather the README change land separately.\n\nSCRATCH CLEANUP: /home/bob/.cache/mess-bench/1u6p-corpus, 1u6p-baseline and 1u6p-after all removed with rm -r; nothing matching 1u6p remains under /home/bob/.cache/mess-bench. No background load generators or background loops were started at any point."}	blake3:mW3b_JWMqjlixGKJ-KOWUcn7AJPWLLptYg5Sz5xk1ks
1785875929462969	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExsxI		item.move	bn-1u6p	{"state":"done"}	blake3:doY5F6j2INJVRRbWtmyJzqjymCBPKPH-2-qNBLIavXc
1785875929483232	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExtBI		item.comment	bn-1u6p	{"body":"Merged to main at af19606f878d after lead review. Verified at source: stream_replay_from seek logic including the partition_point straddle handling and the short-implies-complete contract, merge_sealed_and_hot gaplessness traced through the truncation cases with sealed-wins in the Equal arm, per-frame straddle trim at the read_stream call site, handoff test creates a genuine both-tiers window via seal_active. Gates rerun by lead: first full-suite run flaked (rerun green 1333 of 1333, new paging tests stable across 5 repeats, soak smoke green - consistent with the known soak flake under load). Lead independently measured 0.04 ms marginal per scroll-back page at 1.37M depth, loadavg 16 disclosed. RULING ON THE MISSED BOUND: the 3.0 s rehydration bound is recorded as missed at 3.46 s and ACCEPTED. Rationale: the bound was extrapolated in bn-e93g from the unsealed twin at 1.05 us per event, which does not model sealed payload-accelerator materialization near 2.0 us per event; the bone's named mechanism, entry resolution, is eliminated at 140 ms to 0.4 ms per call and rehydration improved 51x. The residual is linear-once per-record materialization, corroborated by the tail cell at 1.3 us per record and the from-0 rebuild at 1.76 us per event - same ballpark on three independent paths, so it is the materialization floor, not a defect. No follow-up filed: all affected paths are linear-once and snapshots now bound replay length as designed."}	blake3:xiUEdfkPU-uc-xKU0txZ5YSESrK6ajVDRnAIvhJcx7U
1785884033752663	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExtRI		item.create	bn-2u64	{"description":"Part 1 of the engine.rs seam split agreed with Bob 2026-08-05. engine.rs in crates/mess-store is 5500+ lines and nearly every perf and observability bone lands in it, which keeps defeating the disjoint-by-file wave model and slows source-level review. This bone extracts the READ SIDE into submodule files under a new src/engine/ directory with engine.rs remaining the parent module: read_stream, read_global and read_global_page, the sealed and hot resolve (sealed_and_hot_entries_from and merge_sealed_and_hot), materialize, and whatever private plumbing those items force along - the worker maps the exact closure in the workspace and lists it in the report. HARD RAILS, move-only: no renames, no signature or behavior changes, no comment rewording - bn citations in doc comments move intact - no new traits or abstractions, no logic reordering, no drive-by improvements. Visibility may widen only where a module boundary forces it, minimally pub crate or pub super, each widening listed and justified in the report. Multiple inherent impl blocks for LogEngine across the new files are the expected mechanism. The parent module re-exports the moved items so every path outside the file keeps working: ZERO diff outside crates/mess-store/src/engine.rs and the new engine directory is the scope check the lead will run. Pattern-setting: this bone establishes the module layout conventions parts 2 and 3 follow; document them in the report. Acceptance: full suite green, cargo check workspace all-targets clean, fmt clean, worker presents move-only evidence such as a summarized git diff with color-moved, and the lead runs the mess-bench floor gate after merge because codegen-unit partitioning can shift with module structure. Blocks part 2 (seal machinery) which blocks part 3 (open and recovery).","kind":"task","title":"refactor: extract the read paths from engine.rs (move-only seam split, part 1 of 3)"}	blake3:iPW4t2RlkV-VL6h6XO6FTfOsnHe35-MX3BpPiPjA5pw
1785884046005329	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExthI		item.create	bn-21ow	{"description":"Part 2 of the engine.rs seam split, blocked by bn-2u64 which establishes the module layout conventions. Extract the seal-side machinery from crates/mess-store/src/engine.rs into engine submodule files: seal_active, the background sealer and seal queue handling including the SealJobGuard interplay, segment roll, reseal, the reg delta emission at seal time, and the quarantine and withdrawal call sites - the worker maps the exact closure in the workspace and lists it in the report. Same HARD RAILS as bn-2u64: move-only, no renames or signature or behavior changes, no comment rewording with bn citations moving intact, no new abstractions, visibility widened only where forced and each widening justified, parent-module re-exports so there is zero diff outside crates/mess-store/src/engine.rs and the engine directory. Follow the layout conventions bn-2u64 documented. Acceptance identical: full suite green, workspace check clean, fmt clean, move-only evidence presented, lead runs the mess-bench floor gate after merge. Blocks part 3.","kind":"task","title":"refactor: extract the seal/roll/reseal machinery from engine.rs (move-only seam split, part 2 of 3)"}	blake3:dCkp9tTjnVHLc1PfS3RmyH98ag19wTDBr2BBtyGDaEs
1785884046143560	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExtxI		item.create	bn-3g9f	{"description":"Part 3 of the engine.rs seam split, blocked by part 2. Extract the open and recovery side from crates/mess-store/src/engine.rs into engine submodule files: open_with and its wiring, the lazy sidecar attach at open from bn-bka2, registry delta admission and fallback from bn-26pp, and segment resume wiring - the worker maps the exact closure in the workspace and lists it in the report. This is invariant-heavy recovery code: the move-only rails matter most here. Same HARD RAILS as bn-2u64 and part 2: move-only, no renames or signature or behavior changes, no comment rewording with bn citations moving intact, no new abstractions, visibility widened only where forced and justified, parent-module re-exports, zero diff outside crates/mess-store/src/engine.rs and the engine directory. Follow the layout conventions the earlier parts documented. Acceptance identical: full suite green, workspace check clean, fmt clean, move-only evidence, lead runs the mess-bench floor gate after merge. After this part, engine.rs retains the commit pipeline and owner machinery as the residual core - deliberately NOT extracted.","kind":"task","title":"refactor: extract open/recovery from engine.rs (move-only seam split, part 3 of 3)"}	blake3:0ePOcx4nQ6dm5Z9xJ-B8zfnBrTrdgMuW93hF7I3EES8
