1785884115877581	opus-2u64	itc:v3:AWLnJknmSSTM5MQAAAAAQe61e1VbdYAAAAEyAw		item.comment	bn-2u64	{"body":"Started in workspace bn-2u64"}	blake3:X9MgDOdaNWQrZaiHftmKS0HGutbXjaAjIJCe_ibgBEg
1785884792323910	opus-2u64	itc:v3:AWLnJknmSSTM5MQAAAAAQe61e1VbdYAAAAEyBA		item.comment	bn-2u64	{"body":"bn-2u64 final report 1 of 2 (move-only seam split, part 1 of 3). Workspace bn-2u64, epoch 7240a12bc9c2cb40c37055d666a222e07e028410, HEAD 58290d682ea8c19a0ddf7e029860b4e6ced0fbe0, review cr-2gcy8i (reviewer mess-dev). Two commits: 8e45f567 (read plumbing) and 58290d68 (the Backend trait impl).\n\nTHE ONE STRUCTURAL FINDING, which shaped the layout. read_stream, read_global and read_global_page are NOT inherent methods - they are methods of the single impl Backend for LogEngine block, which also holds head, append_batch and append_batch_owned. Rust forbids splitting one trait impl across files, and splitting it by making the trait methods delegate to new inherent methods would be a signature change plus a new abstraction, which the hard rails forbid. So the three named read entry points could only move as a unit with the whole trait impl. I asked on rite before doing it and said I would take that option if no answer came; none had arrived by the time the gates were green, so the whole impl Backend for LogEngine moved and head plus append_batch plus append_batch_owned came along as forced passengers. The new file is named backend_impl.rs rather than read.rs precisely so the name does not lie about those passengers, and its module header says why they are there. If the lead prefers the alternative - leave impl Backend in engine.rs and move only the read plumbing - that is exactly commit 8e45f567 on its own, so reverting 58290d68 gives it with no rework.\n\nMOVED ITEMS BY FILE. crates/mess-store/src/engine/read.rs, 322 lines, new: one inherent impl LogEngine block holding sealed_and_hot_entries_from and materialize with their full doc comments (bn-1u6p, bn-e93g, bn-2ib citations intact), then the free fn merge_sealed_and_hot with its doc, then the cfg(test) mod sealed_paging_tests with its doc header, its entry helper and its three tests. crates/mess-store/src/engine/backend_impl.rs, 469 lines, new: the entire impl Backend for LogEngine - type Error, head, read_stream, read_global, read_global_page (with its long bn-2di why-the-global-sequence-has-holes doc), append_batch, append_batch_owned. crates/mess-store/src/engine.rs drops from 6163 to 5400 lines.\n\nLEFT BEHIND ON PURPOSE, under the shared-helper rail. BlockReader and its whole capsule tier - DecodedBatch, PayloadLayout, BatchExpect, CapsuleKey, CapsuleWeighter, GlobalDirWeighter, PcolBlock, PcolWeighter, CAPSULE_OVERHEAD_BYTES, pread_exact - stay in engine.rs: they are read machinery by name, but the append owner constructs a DecodedBatch and warms the capsule cache in FlatOwner retire_domain, LogEngine recover constructs the BlockReader, and fold_registry builds BatchExpect values, so they are shared with code parts 2 and 3 own. read_batch_payloads also stays: read-shaped name, but its only caller is finish_recovery, which is part 3. impl SubscribeBackend for LogEngine stays: not named in the bone, and moving it would be scope I was not given. No helper was duplicated and none was relocated.\n\nVISIBILITY WIDENINGS - exactly two, both forced, both minimal. First, LogEngine sealed_and_hot_entries_from goes from fn to pub(super) fn; forcing use-site is read_stream in crates/mess-store/src/engine/backend_impl.rs, the self.sealed_and_hot_entries_from(sid, start, limit) call, which is now a sibling module so the item must be visible in the parent engine module. Second, LogEngine materialize goes from fn to pub(super) fn; forcing use-sites are read_stream (let page = self.materialize of picks) and read_global_page (let records = self.materialize of picks) in the same file. merge_sealed_and_hot stayed fully private - its only callers are inside read.rs and its own test module. NO pub use re-exports were needed at all: everything moved is either a trait impl, which has no path, or a private item whose callers are all inside the engine subtree. Scope check confirms it: git diff --name-only over epoch..HEAD lists exactly crates/mess-store/src/engine.rs, crates/mess-store/src/engine/backend_impl.rs and crates/mess-store/src/engine/read.rs, nothing else."}	blake3:xogz-dv5IWGJb-gvMtWE_iQCyhMlOQPfIBbYe47HmfA
1785884815346377	opus-2u64	itc:v3:AWLnJknmSSTM5MQAAAAAQe61e1VbdYAAAAEyBQ		item.comment	bn-2u64	{"body":"bn-2u64 final report 2 of 2 - evidence, gates, and the conventions parts 2 and 3 inherit.\n\nMOVE-ONLY EVIDENCE. git diff --color-moved=zebra --color-moved-ws=allow-indentation-change over epoch..HEAD, classified by ANSI colour: 762 lines detected as moved on the removed side (305 oldMoved plus 457 oldMovedAlternative) and 762 on the added side (632 newMoved plus 130 newMovedAlternative). Totals are 794 insertions and 766 deletions, so 32 added lines and 4 removed lines are not moves. Every one of them is accounted for. The 4 removals: the two old signature lines fn sealed_and_hot_entries_from and fn materialize, plus two blank lines that trailed the moved blocks. The 32 additions: the two mod declarations (mod backend_impl, mod read) and a blank, the two module headers (10 lines and 9 lines), two use super::* lines, the impl LogEngine wrapper open and close brace, the two rewritten pub(super) signature lines, and five blanks. That is exactly the allowed set - mod declarations, use statements, module headers, visibility keywords, impl block wrappers - and there were no pub use re-exports to add. Second, independent check, because colour-moved proves blocks moved but not that their content is unchanged: a multiset comparison of every line of engine.rs at the epoch against the union of all lines of the three files at HEAD reports exactly those same differences - only the two old signature lines vanish, and only headers, use super::*, mod declarations, the impl wrapper and blanks appear. Zero content lines changed anywhere, so no in-body edit or reordering can be hiding inside a moved block.\n\nGATES. cargo check --workspace --all-targets clean, no warnings. just test full suite green: 1333 tests run, 1333 passed, 28 skipped - including engine_sealed_paging (both the byte-identical cursor sweep and the deep-cursor block-decode test), differential_model, snapshot_law and both soak smokes. No test needed a rerun, so the soak flake did not appear. just fmt-check clean on the first try, so there is NO formatting churn for the lead to separate from real edits: rustfmt did not reflow a single moved line in its new context. Two extra baselines I ran unprompted because a module split can shift lints: the rustdoc warning set from cargo doc -p mess-store --no-deps is byte-identical between epoch and HEAD, so no intra-doc link broke and no new private-intra-doc-link warning appeared; and the clippy warning set from cargo clippy -p mess-store --all-targets is byte-identical too. Both baselines were taken by swapping the epoch file in and back out again; the tree is clean at HEAD and nothing from those runs is committed.\n\nLAYOUT CONVENTIONS FOR PARTS 2 AND 3 - follow these mechanically. One: Rust 2018 layout is preserved. engine.rs stays the parent module file and new siblings are crates/mess-store/src/engine/NAME.rs. Do not create engine/mod.rs - the parent path staying put is what preserves its git history. Two: the mod NAME declarations live in engine.rs in one small block immediately after use crate::version::Version and before the CATEGORY_ID const, kept alphabetical (today mod backend_impl then mod read). The cfg(test) mod append_gate_tests declaration stays where it is, down with the test modules, so production and test module declarations do not mix. Three: every new file opens with a short //! header - a few lines saying what the module holds and its contract, cribbed from the moved items own docs - then a blank line, then use super::* and nothing else. That single glob is what makes the move byte-only: a child module can see every private item AND every private use binding of its ancestors, so no import list has to be reconstructed, no import can drift, and parent-to-child access needs no widening at all. Keep the header in plain backticks rather than rustdoc link brackets so it cannot add a private-intra-doc-link warning; the header is the only new prose you write. Four: moved methods go into a fresh inherent impl LogEngine block in the new file and their bodies are not touched. Multiple such blocks across files is the mechanism, not a smell. Five: trait impls are indivisible. impl Backend for LogEngine and impl SubscribeBackend for LogEngine each move whole or not at all - check whether an item you were told to move is a trait method BEFORE planning the file split, because that is the one thing that can force passengers on you. Six: visibility. A child-to-parent or child-to-sibling reference is the only thing that forces a widening. Use pub(super) first, which makes an item visible in engine and therefore in every engine sibling module too, and reach for pub(crate) only if something outside engine needs it. Nothing else on the signature line changes. Seven: a test module moves with the item it tests, keeping its doc header, and becomes a cfg(test) mod inside the new file with its own use super::* - the glob of a glob resolves fine. Eight: produce both evidence artefacts, colour-moved for the block-level story and a multiset line comparison of the union of the touched files against the epoch for the content-equality story, because colour-moved alone will not catch a one-word edit inside a moved block.\n\nNOTHING ELSE TO FLAG. I found no defect worth reporting while moving the code, so there is no defect comment to file. I did not push, did not merge, did not run bn done, and did not approve the review. Workspace bn-2u64 is committed and unmerged; no claims released."}	blake3:v3rz41n7hqxPizgIBxM_pj44KAlap3uwvk8MNs2vI50
1785885183896628	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExuxI		item.move	bn-2u64	{"state":"done"}	blake3:h62C-aGiH1KFX5U0eallCW2-9RFlM1ICFNWtaZMSimk
1785885183929607	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExvBI		item.comment	bn-2u64	{"body":"Merged to main at 347760f1db42 after lead review. Move-only proven independently: multiset line comparison of epoch engine.rs vs the union of the three result files showed 32 delta lines, every one matching a structural pattern (module headers, mod decls, use super, impl wrapper braces, the two justified pub super rewrites and their removed old signatures), zero content lines changed. Passenger ruling: moving the whole impl Backend block accepted - the trait impl is indivisible, delegation wrappers would violate the rails, and backend_impl.rs names the contents honestly. Post-merge gates: trunk suite 1333 of 1333, full mess-bench floor gate PASS all 20 within tolerance - no codegen-unit shift from the new module structure."}	blake3:sF5dXw9G13SlEAfARZUedGLyxaR9Kntc2nWhmrR0LQY
1785885227453791	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExvRI		item.comment	bn-21ow	{"body":"Dispatched to worker opus-21ow 2026-08-05 after bn-2u64 merged and its floor gate passed. Same rails and evidence bar as part 1: multiset comparison must show only structural delta lines, zero content changes. Worker instructed to follow the layout conventions documented in bn-2u64 comments."}	blake3:2UzvY28zAYXwI0bAcqXgPp9sBiihpQaAAJJ5Uwz4y5o
