1785902662981851	sonnet-3g9f	itc:v3:AWKScmSZJOZkyZxAAAAAQautWq7a1uAAAAE0BA		item.comment	bn-3g9f	{"body":"bn-3g9f final report 1 of 2 (move-only seam split, part 3 of 3). Workspace bn-3g9f, epoch e3b09dd11d603049a5d2fb7c23a7831216a4ccae, HEAD 6602eed78978a168d37c3fde8fef0d5a29f92be8, review cr-1vtt8d (reviewer mess-dev). One commit. Scope check: git status and the staged diff list exactly crates/mess-store/src/engine.rs and crates/mess-store/src/engine/open.rs, nothing else.\n\nFILE CHOICE. ONE new file, crates/mess-store/src/engine/open.rs, 1461 lines. open_with calls Self::load_sealed and Self::recover, and recover calls Self::refute_orphans and Self::finish_recovery, and load_sealed calls Self::check_named_pack and Self::refute_unparsable — every one of those calls is currently a private method call within the same impl block. Splitting the closure across two files would have forced at least one of those private calls to cross a module boundary and need a pub(super) widening for no gain, so the whole open/recovery closure is one module, same reasoning part 2 gave for keeping seal.rs to one file. engine.rs drops from 4894 to 3446 lines.\n\nTHE EXACT CLOSURE I MAPPED, in the order the items sit in open.rs. First, six private supporting types that sat together as one contiguous block in engine.rs between publish_batch and the owner-ring constants, used nowhere outside this closure: ResumePlan (the resume-vs-fresh decision), Recovered (what recover hands open_with), PendingCandidate (a parsed-but-unproven sealed index plus the path the refutation path needs to quarantine it), LoadedSealed (what load_sealed hands open_with), ScanOutput (recovery step 1s output, held until the registry fold), ResumeInfo (the resume state threaded into SegmentWriter::resume). Second, a fresh inherent impl LogEngine block: open (the pub fn open(dir) wrapper — three lines, calls open_with, moved with it since a lone one-impl-block file just for a forwarding wrapper made no sense), open_with (the full open path — store dir creation, lock acquisition, load_sealed, recover, segment writer create-or-resume, the sealer-thread spawn with its long bn-30u re-seal-convergence comment, the roll-channel reseal_tx clone and enqueue loop, BlockReader/PublishState/FlatOwner construction, and the owner-thread spawn), recover (spec 04 section 7.1s three-step scan/fold/resolve, including the bn-26pp registry-delta admit-or-fallback branch and the bn-11ba reg_delta_admitted/reg_delta_fallback counters), refute_orphans, finish_recovery (steps 2 and 3 — the registry fold and the REG21 name-resolution refusal, including the max_event_type_id density check from review F3), load_sealed (the bn-3of dual-read of .seal packs and legacy .pidx sidecars, the bn-11g named-pack check dispatch, and the bn-30u quarantine-marker collection), check_named_pack (load_sealeds only caller — the bn-11g SealPackIdentity cross-check), refute_unparsable. Third, the free fn read_batch_payloads (finish_recoverys only caller — the bounded pread used to materialize a registry batch located by pointer instead of by segment image).\n\nLEFT BEHIND ON PURPOSE, under the shared-helper rail. segment_path stays in engine.rs: called from BlockReader and from seal.rs (part 2 already established this), and now also from open_with, recover, load_sealed and read_batch_payloads in open.rs — eleven call sites total across the whole engine module, none of which needed segment_path to change at all, since open.rs reaches it through use super::* like every sibling does. enumerate_segment_ids stays in engine.rs for the identical reason: called from recover (moving) and from seal.rs (already a sibling). Book and its whole impl block stay: recover and finish_recovery call Book::new and Book::from_registry and read book.heads/stream_name_opt/type_name_opt, but apply_registration, alloc_registry_versions and registry_head are called from retire_domain and process_registry, which are owner/commit-pipeline code staying in engine.rs residual core — Book is shared, not recovery-only. BlockReader and the whole capsule-cache tier (DecodedBatch, PayloadLayout, BatchExpect, CapsuleKey/Weighter, PcolBlock/Weighter, pread_exact) stay: open_with constructs one BlockReader, but the read paths in backend_impl.rs and read.rs are its other callers. Inner, AppendOwner, OwnerStatus, FlatOwner and SpinConfig stay: open_with constructs literals of all of them (Inner especially, the whole engine handle), but they belong to the commit/owner machinery the bone says to leave alone. Pre stays: it looks recovery-adjacent by name but its three call sites (plan_domain, retire_domain, process_registry) are all owner-pipeline code, not our closure — confirmed by grep before I planned the split, exactly the trap part 2 flagged for run_roll_sealer/seal_active (check whether an item is entangled with the residual core before planning the file boundary)."}	blake3:BXC5q2SI-1mByQgw8iJRqI35qP_fkHJZnFdJUuBeycM
1785902696213155	sonnet-3g9f	itc:v3:AWKScmSZJOZkyZxAAAAAQautWq7a1uAAAAE0BQ		item.comment	bn-3g9f	{"body":"bn-3g9f final report 2 of 2 - visibility, move-only evidence, gates, conventions followed.\n\nVISIBILITY WIDENINGS: ZERO. This is the one that differs from parts 1 and 2. open and open_with were already fully pub (open_with is called from mess-cli, mess-bench, mess-soak and the engine test suites via LogEngine::open_with — a plain pub fn is reachable from a new file with no change at all). recover, refute_orphans, finish_recovery, load_sealed, check_named_pack and refute_unparsable are called only from inside this same closure — I grepped every Self:: call and every LogEngine::name reference across crates/mess-store, crates/mess-cli, crates/mess-bench and crates/mess-soak before moving anything, and none of the six has a caller outside the group, so all six stayed exactly as private as they were, just relocated together. The one cross-module call inside open_with, Self::run_roll_sealer, already returned pub(super) from bn-21ow and needed nothing further — pub(super) on an item in engine/seal.rs is visible throughout crate::engine, which includes engine/open.rs as a sibling, exactly as bn-2u64s report described. segment_path and enumerate_segment_ids stayed private in engine.rs and open.rs reaches them with no widening at all, through use super::* and the ancestor-private-item visibility rule (a child module of engine sees every private item, including private struct fields, of its ancestor) — open_with also builds Inner, AppendOwner, OwnerStatus and FlatOwner struct literals directly by their private field names for the same reason, with zero change to any of those definitions.\n\nMOVE-ONLY EVIDENCE, artefact one: git diff --cached --color-moved=zebra --color-moved-ws=allow-indentation-change, classified by ANSI colour. Totals are 1462 insertions and 1449 deletions (matches git diff --stat exactly). 1447 removed lines and 1447 added lines are coloured as moved — one to one, every moved block paired. 2 removed lines are plain, uncoloured: both are blank lines, trimmed at the two of the three excision seams to avoid a doubled blank once the surrounding content closed up (a third excision seam blank fully absorbed into a moved pairing instead, which is the same below-block-detection-threshold blank noise part 1 and part 2 both hit). 15 added lines are plain: mod open, the eight-line module header, one blank after it, use super::*, one blank after that, impl LogEngine { , the matching closing brace, and one more blank before read_batch_payloads. That is exactly the allowed set — mod declaration, module header, use statement, impl block wrapper, and blanks — and there were no pub use re-exports to add, since nothing that moved needed one.\n\nMOVE-ONLY EVIDENCE, artefact two, the multiset check the lead will re-run: I first verified crates/mess-store/src/engine/append_gate_tests.rs, backend_impl.rs, read.rs and seal.rs are BYTE-IDENTICAL between the epoch and HEAD (diff -q on all four, all identical), so they contribute nothing to either side. Then a proper multiset comparison (python collections.Counter, so repeated lines like blanks and closing braces are weighed by multiplicity, not just sorted-and-diffed) of every line of the epoch engine.rs (4894 lines) against every line of HEAD engine.rs plus HEAD engine/open.rs combined (3446 + 1461 = 4907 lines): ZERO lines appear only in the epoch file — not one content line, not even a signature line, since no widening meant no signature had to change at all. Exactly 13 line-instances appear only at HEAD: mod open (1), the eight module-header lines (1), use super::* (1), impl LogEngine { (1), its closing brace (1), and a net surplus of exactly one blank line (four blanks were added as new separators in open.rs against three extra blanks trimmed from engine.rs at the excision seams — Counter nets these since blank content is fungible, which is why this number is smaller and asymmetric compared with the raw color-moved diff above, the same phenomenon parts 1 and 2 both noted). Total delta 13 lines, all structural, ZERO content lines changed anywhere. Part 1s delta was 32, part 2s was 15; this one is smaller because it needed no widenings at all — nothing to remove-and-rewrite on the epoch side.\n\nGATES. cargo check --workspace --all-targets: clean, zero warnings. just test full suite: 1333 tests run, 1333 passed, 28 skipped — including both soak smokes (soak_smoke_crashless_clean_pass and soak_smoke_survives_crashes_with_all_probes_on), snapshot_law, differential_model, engine_sealed_paging (both cursor tests), engine_reopen_cycles, registry_delta_differential, and pack_snapshot. Nothing needed a rerun; the soak flake did not appear. just fmt-check: clean — the only reflow rustfmt wanted was in my own new module-header prose (an 8-line wrap it reflowed to a different line break before I ran fmt-check the first time), which I fixed by hand; ZERO reflow anywhere in moved code, matching parts 1 and 2.\n\nTWO EXTRA BASELINES, taken unprompted, both by git stash -u to swap the epoch tree back in and popping it back after. cargo clippy -p mess-store --all-targets: IDENTICAL diagnostic set between epoch and HEAD (2 mess-store-lib warnings, both type_complexity on the BlockReader cache fields, same lints same files, line numbers shifted by exactly +1 for the new mod open declaration — diffed the two runs directly to confirm). cargo doc -p mess-store --no-deps: IDENTICAL diagnostic set too (37 diagnostics both runs), only two line numbers shifted to reflect where recover_payload_decodes and sealed_segment_count doc comments now sit relative to the shrunk residual impl block — the intra-doc links to recover and open_with resolve exactly as they did before, since rustdoc resolves them against the type, not the file. Tree was clean at HEAD both times the stash was popped, nothing from those runs is committed.\n\nCONVENTIONS FROM bn-2u64 AND bn-21ow THAT I FOLLOWED. Rust 2018 layout preserved, no engine/mod.rs. mod open; went into the existing small alphabetical block: backend_impl, open, read, seal. The file opens with a short //! header naming the bn citations (bn-20b, bn-2ib, bn-30u, bn-26pp) in plain backticks, not rustdoc link brackets, then a blank, then use super::* and nothing else. I checked the trait-impl trap first: neither open nor open_with nor any of the six recovery methods is a trait method, all inherent, so no forced passengers. Every doc comment and bn citation moved byte-intact — I did not touch a single moved line, including the ones I confirmed reference items that are now in other files (recover_payload_decodes docs cite Self::recover, which now lives in this new file; that link still resolves, and I left the citing text exactly as it was, matching part 1s explicit precedent of leaving an inter-file doc reference alone). No defect found while moving — the recovery closure was clean.\n\nI did not push, did not merge, did not run bn done, and did not approve the review. Workspace bn-3g9f is committed and unmerged; no claims released."}	blake3:Q_ke07jCjYaeLHD8vWKpRNTSK9Q00YjypSty7UnyNPM
1785902995064346	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExwRI		item.move	bn-3g9f	{"state":"done"}	blake3:FOznr2WGexZ1sNxfaMC_Dd_P2wclmywtdtSZXc3H75Q
1785902995094553	mess-dev	itc:v3:AWKTOcmSTM5M8lAAAAAAQa3darbreoAAAAExwhI		item.comment	bn-3g9f	{"body":"Merged to main at a8e27cb6e0ce after lead review. Move-only proven independently: multiset comparison showed a 13-line all-structural delta, zero content lines changed, all four sibling engine files byte-identical, and the absence of signature-rewrite lines confirms the zero-widenings claim. One procedural note: the merge initially refused because the lead's doc-comment chore and journal commits sat outside maw's epoch; maw epoch sync absorbed them cleanly since the workspace was created after those commits. Post-merge gates: trunk suite 1333 of 1333, full mess-bench floor gate PASS all 20 within tolerance at loadavg 6. Seam split complete: engine.rs 6163 to 3446 lines across three merges, residual core is the commit pipeline and owner machinery."}	blake3:jEwZwL5-e2Zqdgc9LBNkHVdQOp5o8-GrFnna2658UZs
